 In this interview, former Gainesville Women for Equal Rights (GWER) member Rosa B. Williams goes into depth of her life experiences in the Civil Rights Movement in Gainesville, Florida. She extends on the many activities she was involved in GWER and the programs she helped create to desegregate Gainesville. Mrs Mr. Isaac Chandler begins with a discussion on Mr. Chandler’s time of service in WWII. Upon returning from the war, Mr. Chandler became an extension agent in a 4H program in Florida. Mr. Chandler discusses segregation in Jasper focusing mostly on how it affected his career and neighborhood. He discusses historical figures such as Emmett Till, Rosa Parks, Jackie Robinson, Joe Louis and how their movements affected him and the Black people in his community. There is conversation of sit-ins that took place in Tallahassee, striving for equal pay during his time of work with the Farmer’s Administration, and the assassination of President Kennedy. The interview concludes with a brief discussion on Mr. Chandler’s role in the SER Club (Society, Economics, and Resources) for African American extension agents Mr. Isaac Chandler describes his duties and experiences as a 4H agent in Florida in the 1950s and 1960s. The discussion then moves to how African Americans bought, and eventually lost, land in Florida. Mr. Chandler then talks about how he came about buying his own estate, motivating more African Americans to study and pursue careers in agriculture, his own work with schools in north Florida, and the importance of teaching students about black history and culture. Mr. Chandler discusses the mission of the SER (Society, Economics, and Resources) Club for African American extension agents and how when he was an agent, he and his colleagues fought for equal pay. The interview concludes with Mr. Chandler and Weston recognizing African American people in Florida politics and positions of power today Mattie Williams, 101 years old at the time of this interview, talks with Marna Weston about her life in Florida over the years. She mentions changes she has seen in the community, and shares stories of her childhood. Ms. Williams talks about her parent’s background and their journey to Gainesville and High Springs, where she was raised by her mother, and her limited experiences with school growing up Marna Weston talks to Ann Douglass Skinner about her life living in Lake City, Columbia County, Florida. She discusses her time as a high school teacher during integration in the 1970s. She reflects on the struggles that both the teachers and the students faced. Mrs. Skinner notes the inequality that many of her Black friends and students faced living in rural North Florida. The interviewee then discusses her late husband, Sherrill N. “Pete” Skinner. He served in the Vietnam war and later went on to serve in the Peace Corps in India and the Caribbean. Pete studied political science at the University of Florida and served as a member of the state House of Representatives and the Florid State Senate in the 1970s and 1980s. Mrs. Skinner remembers her home always being busy with politicians and her husband’s constituents In this interview, Mack Mizell speaks about his experiences growing up in Hamilton County, Florida. He discusses his education at a church that did not teach students past the eighth grade. He also describes his job as a turpentine worker and why he decided to work there. Mr. Mizell shares memories about his military service during World War II, and what he did upon his return Oliver Hugh Jones was born in Pensacola, Florida in 1920. His family moved to Gainesville when he was a toddler because high father accepted the position of Principal to Union Academy, the precursor to Lincoln High School. His father served in education for 42 years and the whole nuclear family worked as educators. Jones reflects on his childhood and the introduction of electricity, sewer lines, and indoor plumbing. Due to segregation, he never was at the University of Florida, but attended Florida A&M College. He discusses teacher’s wages, transportation, and economic pricing In this interview, Mrs. Christine Holmes recounts growing up in Perry, Florida where she lived with her parents, four sisters, and three brothers on a cotton farm. Her father participated in sharecropping, which was what allowed their family to live on the land. Even though this was the 1930s, Mrs. Holmes remembers having close friendships with some of the White children that lived in the area. She describes the race relations in her town as civil but not overly-friendly, and tells the story of a Black boy who was killed for looking at a White girl when she was young. During the Great Depression, her family was comfortable because her father was a farmer and was able to provide food. They were better off than most people in town, but she did not realize this until the pastor at her church preached about the lack of food. Church played an important role in her childhood and carried into adulthood, when she served as an usher for many years. She places a high value on religion, and it has guided her through her life In this interview, Mrs. Christine Holmes recounts a terrible storm in 1935 that destroyed their home. They had no official alert that the storm was coming but her father sensed that a storm was picking up and had the family seek shelter in a building on the property with a cement floor. Mrs. Holmes vividly remembers the sky being clear and sunny, with the only sign of a storm being a wind that caused her rope swing to swing violently. After the storm passed, her mother came home from work and thought her family was trapped in the house that had been destroyed. Fortunately, her father brought her mother back to the shelter they were staying in and calmed her down. They lost everything in the storm and had to live in a house that was flooded, and slept on the floor in the water In this interview, Arnold Mitchell talks about growing up in Gainesville. He discusses attending Lincoln High School, and his experiences getting involved in the music industry, first locally and then nationally. Mr. Mitchell shares his reflections on integrating the Gator Growl at the University of Florida In this interview, Arnold Mitchell talks about growing up in Gainesville, Florida’s music scene. In this interview he speaks about both his experience growing up in a segregated Gainesville and about his life in show business. Arnold Mitchell was signed to a contract when he was only ten years old after a record producer heard him singing in the market. After his ninth grade year Mr. Mitchell moved from Tampa to Gainesville to live with his grandmother and uncle, attending Lincoln High School. When he was fifteen years old, he and his band were asked by the University of Florida to perform at Gator Growl, and he describes the integration of Gator Growl through the process. After this performance, Mr. Mitchell began touring around the United States and was tutored while on the road. He performed in many major places including the Apollo theatre and the Ed Sullivan show Paul Cohen discusses his relationship with Mr. Arnold Mitchell, whose band integrated the Gator Growl event at the University of Florida. He also talks about living in Nigeria when he was younger while his father was working on an ethnography. The country was already headed toward civil war, and some of his father’s colleagues were trying to work to prevent the massacre of Biafrans In this interview, Marna Weston describes his immediate family, specifically when and where his parents met and where each of them were from, and the occupations of his two younger brothers. Then he goes on to explain his involvement at the University of Florida, where he was the second Black treasurer of the student body and the first Black president of the speech and debate team. He received bachelor’s degrees in telecommunication and history, which he used to be a public school teacher and the secretary of the Gainesville Democratic Party. He returned to school at Florida State University to pursue a masters in policy and political communication while also serving as coach of their debate team. Currently (at the time of this interview) he is pursuing a doctorate in agriculture at the University of Florida while also working with the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program In this interview, Larnell Vickers, recently elected as student body president of University of Florida, talks about his experience growing up in Miami and attending a private Christian school. He also talks about life at the University of Florida, his experiences in speech and debate, his idea of leadership and what that means for African Americans, and his Christian faith In this interview, Ethel Williams primarily describes her family and memories of the cities she lived in. She was an only child who was raised by her grandfather and mother in Perry, Florida. She loved school and reading, and was sent to boarding school in Live Oak for high school because there were schoolhouses being burned near Perry. As a young adult, she loved to go dances in Jacksonville, and met her husband at one of them. They ended up living in Shamrock and had a daughter together. Mrs In this interview, Laura Scott Reaves describes her experiences growing up in Perry, and later going on to teach school there. Although the University of Florida did not allow Black students to attend campus when she was younger, it did allow Black students to obtain correspondence degrees, and she describes the process she underwent to earn hers. She also describes what she calls the “Civil War of 1923,” in which White supremacist powermongering led to a massive racialized labor dispute centered around the Burton and Swartz cypress mill and the skilled Black laborers who moved with the mill from its original location in Louisiana. She also describes a variety of other experiences with segregation and White supremacy in Perry and the surrounding area, from bus incidents to voting rights (and denial thereof), and including a Ku Klux Klan cross burning in more recent years In this follow-up to a previous interview, Laura Scott Reaves reflects on her experiences teaching at Boyd High School in Foley, Taylor County. She also relates different experiences with integration of schools in the county. A considerable portion of the exchange includes discussion of documents that the interview participants are examining. It also includes some planning for future events such as the interviews with Jerkins High School alumni that Marna Weston later led In this interview, Laura Scott Reaves continues her reflections on her career as an educator in Taylor County, as well as on the educators who inspired her when she was going to school. She attributes her success as an educator to “loving people,” and she relates a number of student conflicts that she had to navigate during her career. She also relates her memories of Henry R. Jerkins, Jr., the principal who served as the namesake of the Black High School in Perry. In the process, she relates the previous schools which were burned over the years, and then discusses a specific moment of White supremacist terrorism that she calls “the Civil War of [19]23.” She also relates some family history, and mentions some of the elders who she learned from over the years Raised in Sanford, Florida, Virgil Hayes discusses some of his experiences in the education system before moving into his successes in speech and debate tournaments in high school, at Seminole Community College, and eventually at Florida State University. His achievements include winning five state titles on a two-year circuit. He reflects on the challenges that he faced in forensics and what it took for him to succeed, and his family’s support. Mr. Hayes also discusses the personal meaning of a statue on Florida State’s campus; titled “Integration,” the statue depicts several of the African American ‘firsts’ at Florida State, and he relates to the statue in terms of its meaning as a strong statement about the institution that houses it In this interview, Atkins Warren talks about his experience growing up in Missouri. He also discusses his time in the Coast Guard in the 1940s, and his employment with the Department of Justice in Kansas City. He describes his experience on a ship facing a Japanese attack during World War II. He also tells the story of how he came to Gainesville to serve as the city’s first African American Chief of Police In this interview, Juanita Scott Williams was joined by her sister, Laura Scott Reaves to describe their lives in Taylor County, Florida. They live on a street that was named after their father, Elmo Scott Avenue because their family owns the entire block of property by the street. As far as school goes, Laura’s school was burned down, so they had to have school in various churches because they didn’t have a building to serve as a school. Juanita has a Master of Education from Florida A&M University. She worked as a teacher in Taylor County, Lafayette County, and Thomasville, Georgia for 36 years and made sure that her daughter went to college as well. Her daughter faced a good amount of racial discrimination in school, from both other students and administration, but she worked through it and received a Doctorate in Pharmacology from Florida A&M University In this interview, Albert White discusses growing up in Gainesville, Florida, living in the Sugarhill neighborhood and working at his father’s restaurant in the Porter’s Quarters neighborhood. He relates memories of his grandfather, Al Higgins, as a widely respected and very hard-working man who did a lot to help his community. Mr. White offers some memories of going to school at North Carolina A&T State University in Greensboro, including the civil rights activism there and the National Guard firing on a dorm there. He particularly offers some extended reflections on the complex impacts that racial integration has had on the African American community, and in so doing, the experience of going to Lincoln High School. As he describes it, Lincoln instilled a sense of identity, community, and achievement in students that he feels was lost after integration, when Lincoln was closed and then demoted to a middle school. He ruminates on the concern that one day there will be no more Lincolnians Deloris Jenkins discusses her experiences growing up in Gainesville, Florida, including her time at Lincoln High School. She reflects on Lincoln, her mentors there, and her involvement in activities at the school. Mrs. Jenkins recalls highly dedicated instructors and administrators, and describes a similar overall ethic in the wider community. In discussing more recent years, she also shares her thoughts about her career working in the Physical Plant at the University of Florida Pastor David Richardson discusses his time as director of the University of Florida Gospel Choir, and some of the history of UF as he experienced it. He also describes growing up in Hastings, Florida, and the meaning of music and of religion in his life This interview was conducted by a student in a class taught by SPOHP director Paul Ortiz in 2010. Isaac Jones discusses some of his family history and his parents’ occupations, and what he experienced growing up in a segregated society. He includes reflection the gradual process of becoming increasingly aware of these intentionally-created racial disparities and their significance, from public amenities like water fountains, to lack of funding and support for Black schools, to the psychological impact of a lack of African American representation on television and advertisements. He also discusses the personal significance of his alma mater, Lincoln High School, and his experiences enlisting in the Air Force and going on to enroll, through the G.I. Bill, in Howard University and then the University of Florida. He also reflects on his career in the education system. He concludes with considerations of what he learned from his parents, and what sorts of mentalities and attitudes drove him to strive for excellence in his life Brenda Washington describes her career as a retired juvenile delinquency counselor. She is a member of the last graduating class (1969) of Lincoln High School in Gainesville Florida. Ms. Washington went on to receive an Associate of Arts degree from Santa Fe Community College, and a Bachelor’s in Sociology with a minor in Social Welfare from Florida A&M University. Reflecting on her time growing up, Ms Linda Butler describes her experiences growing up in Gainesville, Florida, during the era of the Civil Rights Movement. She shares her memories of attending Lincoln High School, and offers reflections on the continued significance of its legacy as a community institution In this interview, Patricia Burnett reflects on her life growing up in Gainesville, specifically the vibrant community surrounding Lincoln High School and the devastating effect that integration had on the community. Ms. Burnett goes on to describe Lincoln High School as the center of the community. Everyone was connected through the high school, the teachers and administration knew your parents and your siblings, and they all took care of each other. Integration destroyed those connections. Ms. Burnett says it felt as though she lost her community. She also describes how the assassination of President Kennedy was devastating to the students and faculty. They believed that he would have brought positive change to their lives, but he too was taken from them. In terms of Ms. Burnett’s profession, she was initially a social worker in Starke, then worked in the school board, the cafeteria at a school, an anatomy lab at UF, a mental health clinic in Starke, and finally ended up coming back to the lab at UF where she worked as a lab manager for thirty years. Following her retirement, she worked as a senior clerical at the front desk of Duval Elementary School In this interview, Alvin Butler describes his life growing up in the Gainesville area. His father was a farmer with many acres of land where he grew crops to sell and eat. Mr. Butler would travel up north with his father to sell their crops. He attended Lincoln High School for two years and graduated in 1944. When he had children, his son was selected to attend Howard Bishop School during integration because he had a high IQ. He served in the war and when he returned, worked as a cook for Kappa Alpha at UF for 50 years. In terms of values, his family would always give food to those in need growing up, and he continued that into adulthood. He strongly states his beliefs in treating other people fairly Mr. Bernard Hicks is a native of Gainesville who attended Lincoln High School from 1959 to 1965. Mr. Hicks was a music teacher and today he is the commander of the Flossie B. McLendon Memorial Drill Team. In this interview, Mr. Hicks talks about his childhood in Gainesville, the years he spent as a student and band member at Lincoln High School and what effect that had on his adult life. Mr. Hicks reflects on the life lessons he was taught by teachers and by those in his community. He talks about his time as a college student at North Carolina A&T, in Greensboro, North Carolina, and shares his thoughts on education and segregation Earl Williams graduated from Lincoln High School in Gainesville, Florida in 1964. He served for nine years in the Army Reserves. Mr. Williams shares his memories of growing up in Alachua County during Jim Crow segregation, and shares some of his family history in the area. He reflects at length on his experiences at Lincoln High and the continued importance that those experiences have had in his life In this interview, Jeff McMeekin, a White man from Hawthorne, describes his family’s history in the citrus industry and being driven by the “big freeze” to resettle in Alachua County. He recounts some of the history of Hawthorne, Grove Park, and other settlements in southern Alachua County, especially regarding the turpentine industry, kaolin mining, and other industries in the area. He ruminates from a White perspective on the Civil Rights Movement, and the implementation and legacy of integration, and his narrative is intertwined with a good deal of family history Mary Moore is a White woman raised in Nashville who married into the Moore family of Hawthorne, Florida. In this interview, she relates not only her own experiences in Hawthorne, but also stories of her husband Francis Moore and her knowledge of his and his family’s life in the area—including history of the Moore Hotel, which they owned. She especially focuses on the history of the Hawthorne library, from its founding to its present-day growth and development. Mrs. Moore also describes the general history of Hawthorne, and relates stories of race relations and integration In this interview, Evelyn Moore Mickle reflects on her time at the University of Florida at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala. Mrs. Mickle was the first African American graduate of the College of Nursing at UF, and was offered a scholarship to study there. She defined her time at UF as a struggle to prove her worth as an African American student. She also discusses the importance of Mt. Carmel Baptist Church in her life, as well as its longtime pastor Rev. T.A. Wright In this interview, W. George Allen discusses his experience of being the first African American graduate from the University of Florida at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala. Two years into his attendance at the school, he realized he was the only Black student, so he recruited nine more students to come and join him. He convinced White students to bring Black people to the football games and ended up integrating the stadium. Some instrumental people in his life during his time at UF were Reverend T.A. Wright, Stephan Mickle, Dr. Cullen Banks, and John Dukes In this interview, Dr. Gerald Jerome Johnson discusses his experience of being one of the first African American students at the University of Florida at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala. He was enrolled in the veterinary school and describes no outright discrimination, but faced experiences such as people leaving empty seats around him. His mentor at UF was Dr. Llewellyn Payton, who was the first Black graduate of the veterinary school at the University of Minnesota In this interview, Johncyna McRae discusses her experience of being one of the first African American students at the University of Florida at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala. She was one of two Black undergraduates to attend the University of Florida, the other student being her roommate, Rose Green. At first, she questioned her decision to go to UF instead of FAMU, but eventually changed her mind. Her support system consisted of her mother’s friends who lived in the community, as well as Reverend T. A. Wright In this interview, Lee Bailey discusses his experience of being one of the first African American students at the University of Florida at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala. He attended UF to study music education, then went back to school to be a registered nurse. He feels a great sense of pride and accomplishment in being one of the “Firsts” at UF In this interview at the Celebrate the Firsts Weekend Gala, Dr. Reuben E. Brigety discusses his experience of being one of the first African American students at the University of Florida. He grew up hearing about Virgil Hawkins, who was denied admittance into the UF law school many times throughout the 1940s and 1950s, which made Dr. Brigety want to attend UF. He remembers the entire Black community in Gainesville rallying for him and the other Black students to persevere and graduate Stephan Mickle is a judge and serves on the Alumni Association Board of Directors. In 1962, he was one of the first Black students to matriculate at the University of Florida’s undergraduate program, and became the first Black undergraduate student to graduate from UF. He would later attend UF law school as the sole Black student enrolled, and would become vice-president of the senior class. When he graduated, he found that no White firms would hire Black lawyers, and had to work a government job instead. He discusses the loneliness on campus he felt as the only Black student— nobody would speak a word to him—and gives advice for students today. He also talks about incentives for funding higher education for underrepresented students Dr. Bernard Allen Mackey, from Jacksonville, was the first Fine Arts graduate of the University of Florida. He talks about the transition between segregated grade schools to integrated universities. At the time, there were no Black faculty, financial aid, or counseling for Black students. He also talks about Steve Spurrier and sports at UF. He discusses the band program, in which he played trombone and euphonium, and everything else that helped him succeed. Finally, considers the future of the institution in diversity and overall prominence Andrew Mickle grew up in Camden, South Carolina, and after serving in the US military he attended Bethune-Cookman College. Upon graduating, he began teaching at Lincoln High School in Gainesville, Florida, where he was particularly remembered for teaching tailoring and swimming to students. Today, there is a public swimming pool named after him in Gainesville for that reason. Mr. Mickle reflects on the qualities that made Lincoln meaningful to its faculty and alumni, and discusses how integration was implemented in Alachua County Jean Chalmers is a White woman from Alberta, Canada, who describes some of her life experiences leading to her moving to Gainesville, Florida at the age of twenty-five to attend the University of Florida. She relates the culture shock of being a Northerner who moved into Southern segregated society, and relates memories of Jim Crow segregation in Gainesville. Mrs. Chalmers describes her involvement in the Civil Rights Movement while in Gainesville, including work with Gainesville Women for Equal Rights (GWER) and the Southern Regional Council, and co-founding an integrated nursery school with LaKay Banks. She particularly elaborates on the activities of GWER and how it grew and developed as an organization Rosa B. Williams is a Gainesville Civil Rights Movement veteran. She relates some of her family history, and her experiences living in Gainesville and Bronson. She explains how she first got involved in the Civil Rights Movement, and describes some of the direct action campaigns that she was involved with, as well as her involvement with the Gainesville Women for Equal Rights (GWER). She also offers some reflections relating the election campaigns for Barack Obama in 2008 to the organizing that went into voter registration drives during the Movement in the 1960s. Mrs. Williams describes some of what it was like to live under Jim Crow segregation in Gainesville, and assesses the degree and significance of the changes that she has seen since the Movement Evelyn Marie Moore Mickle grew up in Live Oak, FL, and she describes the experience of growing up there during the Jim Crow era. She also describes some of her family history, growing up on a farm with a father who was a pastor and also served as NAACP president for several years. She describes both of her parents as people and in terms of their parenting philosophy. Mrs. Mickle also relates the story of how she ended up attending the University of Florida, and what her experiences were in becoming the first African American graduate of the UF College of Nursing—as well as the effect that those experiences have had on her life since. She closes by discussing the inauguration of Barack Obama and the meaning that it held for her mother and the rest of her family Evelyn Marie Moore Mickle describes growing up in Live Oak, Florida in the mid-20th century. Building on her previous interview, she elaborates on her parents, her family life and its dynamics, and her memories of the Black community in Live Oak, including her schooling there. Also among the memories are the murder of her cousin, Harry T. Moore, and how her father became NAACP president in Live Oak after his predecessor had been driven out of town by White supremacists. She relates what she was able to understand of her father’s organizing activities when she was still too young to be invited into the conversations. She also describes her mother’s garden, and her mother and father’s parenting approaches and philosophies Reverend Willie Mayberry grew up in Jonesville, Florida. He relates a multigenerational family history rooted in the Gainesville area that stretches back before Emancipation. He also describes what it was like to grow up under Jim Crow segregation, including the marked inferiority of segregated public facilities. He also describes his education, and some of the teachers who mentored him in his youth. And he describes his career as a pastor, particularly in terms of the service and community engagement through which he distinguished himself in Alachua County. Rev. Mayberry also relates some thoughts on his work with the NAACP Dr. Mildred Hill-Lubin grew up in Russell County, Alabama during the Roosevelt era. She describes a number of memories from that time and related family history, including memory of a major storm that struck the area when she was a small child. Dr. Hill-Lubin describes attending Catholic school, and relates the story of how she ended up going to Paine College in Augusta, Georgia, and he she persisted in completing her degree over the course of a decade in the face of several obstacles. As she completed her degree, she became involved in the Civil Rights Movement, and in 1968 her son, as a first-grader, was the first to integrate his school in Augusta. Dr. Hill- Lubin relates some of the stress and frustration of that period, during which she was also teaching and working hard to make her academic career successful. She also relates some of her early experiences after being hired at the University of Florida in 1974, and the context of her hiring. And she sees both the assassination of Martin Luther King and the election of President Barack Obama as two major pivotal moments in her life Dr. Mildred Hill-Lubin discusses how she became interested in African and African American literature as a major part of her research and pedagogy as a university professor. She also discusses some of her thoughts on aspects of African literature and how it relates to African American literature, and what it was like to be among the first to teach African literature at the University of Florida in the early 1970s. A significant portion of the interview discusses her lead role in planning the African Literature Association conference in 1980 which UF hosted, and which featured a number of distinguished guests—including James Baldwin and Chinua Achebe, who met each other for the first time at the event. Dr. Hill-Lubin also discusses the legacy the event left at UF, and some of her continued experiences and efforts in regard to African literature Thomas Coward grew up in Gainesville, Florida, graduating from Lincoln High School, and soon being drafted to serve in the US Navy during World War II. The Navy was segregated at that time, and he describes his experiences in wartime as well as after returning home. He explains that his experiences in the military inspired him to become a social studies teacher, in hopes of creating change. After attending Lincoln University in Jefferson City, Missouri, he returned to Gainesville and taught at Lincoln High School. Because Black history was excluded from textbooks and established curriculum, he describes the efforts he undertook to introduce Black history in his classrooms, and the ways he incorporated discussion of the Civil Rights Movement only to be accused of “subversive activity.” He also describes some of his own direct action in relation to integrating Gainesville businesses and other establishments Dr. Samuel Stafford grew up in Tallahassee, Florida, and describes the educational conditions he experienced under a segregated school system. He explains that because Florida State University is in the center of Tallahassee, and segregation was fiercely enforced there, his family always needed to travel far out of their way to avoid the area when they headed from their home over to the Black businesses on the other side of the city. Dr. Stafford also discusses what it was like to attend FSU from 1968-1972 Marna Weston discusses his research on Patricia Stephens Due and Priscilla Stephens Kruize and their role in the Civil Rights Movement, and his career of service work, civic engagement, and his experiences both serving on and coaching debate teams. He also reflects on an event he organized through SPOHP, titled Florida Black History: Where We Stand In the Age of Barack Obama. He describes the significance of the event in relation to establishing a more visible commitment at the University of Florida to diversity and inclusion. He contrasts UF’s lack of a Civil Rights monument with the prominent and finely-made monuments on campuses like the University of Mississippi and Florida State University. He also relates his thoughts to some reflections on the election of Barack Obama Paul Pedro Ortiz grew up in Houston, Texas in the 1940s and 1950s, and describes memories from his childhood, including various life lessons that he learned from climbing trees and other things, and how as a child he navigated Mexican- American identity in an area predominantly settled by Eastern European immigrants. He relates the influence that his grandmother had on his life, and the numerous jobs he took beginning in adolescence. He moves into his eventual entry into the US Marine Corps, discussing experiences in boot camp, and then in Okinawa, and the reasons why he never ended up in Vietnam itself during the war. He then describes his experiences in fighting for diversity in the workforce, including in the Puget Sound Naval Shipyard and as a federal Native American Outreach Coordinator in the state of Washington Alonzo Felder, a University of Florida Graduate and a Duke University faculty member, begins the interview by discussing his religious history growing up. He came from a long line of Methodists but attended a Catholic school. At the University of Florida in 1974, he joined the Jewish fraternity Alpha Epsilon Pi. Despite being and an African American and not a practicing Jew, he says that he never experienced any discrimination. He later joined the Church of Christ. Felder dives into the subject of racism. While he cites multiple examples of race discrimination in his own life, he says that he doesn’t let the way other people view him affect how he lives his life. Felder describes how it seems race tensions are more pronounced at the University of Florida than at Duke University. The interviewer asks about the recent election of Barack Obama and Felder explains that he believes that it won’t ultimately change the minds of racist people because individuals need to personally interact with people of different races to overcome prejudice In this follow-up interview to Felder’s first interview in 2010, Felder discusses his work in diagramming his family tree. He says that it began in 1996 when his mother passed. He wanted to know more about his lineage and meet relatives outside of his immediate family. He explains that his grandmother moved to St. Petersburg, Florida to live with her aunt and this is where many of his family members stayed. The interviewer and the interviewee go back and forth discussing the details of Albert Sydney James Allen, also known as A.S.J, who was Felder’s great grandfather. Felder had been working for eight years collecting information on his murder. Dunnavant and Felder talk about strategies for uncovering more information on the story as well as on the burning of a Black church formerly called Mt. Pleasant Plains Alonzo Felder, in his third interview with University of Florida’s African Ameircan History Project, discusses his late great-grandfather Albert Syndey James Allen. Felder refers to him A.S.J., and says how influential the man was in his community as a preacher, a farmer and a friend. A.S.J was a minister at Mt. Nebo United Methodist Church and Felder says how his family continued to attend the church for many years. In fact, seven generations of his ancestors are buried in the cemetery of Mt. Nebo. Felder explains the details of A.S.J.’s death and its impact on his family. He also defines his perception of the word “Negro” versus “African American” or “Black” Patricia Hilliard Nunn was born in Denver, Colorado, raised in Morovia, Liberia, West Africa, and San Francisco, and graduated high school in East Pointe, Georgia. She talks about why she’s so interested in history and educating others. Her father was a historian and her mother was an African art teacher. She then went to film school at Howard University, where she met and debated famous filmmakers. After this, was awarded the McKnight Doctoral Fellowship at Florida State University. She utilizes her love of history in documentary-making about the stories of African Americans in Gainesville using oral history and interviews in an effort to argue against marginalization and assumptions about black and African cultures, especially since, as argued by Dr In this interview, Dr. Paul Andrew Ortiz describes his beginnings in oral history and his position as the director of the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program. His official start in oral history was field work experience interviewing elder African Americans in the South, but he also produced a radio program for a few years at Evergreen State College where he interviewed people regarding international issues in Central America. He also interviewed migrant farm workers while working with the United Farm Workers of America. While pursuing his PhD at Duke University, Dr. Ortiz worked on the “Behind the Veil Project,” an oral history project focused on African American life in the Jim Crow South. He began teaching a class called “American Communities,” at Duke University as a graduate student and then again as a professor at the University of California at Santa Cruz. This class evolved into the seminar that Dr. Ortiz currently teaches at the University of Florida. He elaborates on why oral history is such an important field and lists the ways it eliminates racial, political, and age barriers Ray Eberling was born outside New York City in a town called Suffern, and his family wintered in Palm Beach, Florida due to his mother’s asthma. Because he went back and forth from North to South, he noticed differences in schooling and prospects for Black children in the 1950s and 1960s. When Florida started to integrate, Eberling noticed that the school system would do as little as possible to comply with new laws. He compares this with his New York school, where he had Black friends from affluent families. When he graduated, he went to college for two years at Palm Beach Junior College, where he had his first Black professor. He then transferred to the University of Florida in 1968 to get a Bachelors in Education, where he saw the transition from a majority sorority and fraternity campus to a culture of “hippie radicals” and a burgeoning Civil Rights Movement In this interview, Dr. Betty Stewart-Fullwood describes her life and work in the OASIS program at the University of Florida. She defines the OASIS program as a place to receive academic resources as well as social support. The program helps students adjust to campus life because many new students are overwhelmed. Dr. Fullwood grew up in a predominantly African American community and initially attended Bethune- Cookman College then transferred to the University of Florida. She experienced a high degree of culture shock when she first came to UF even though she grew up in Gainesville, because there was no support system there for her. OASIS helps new students, especially minorities, not to feel that same way and have a smooth college transition In this interview, Dr. Gwendolyn Zoharah Simmons describes her life growing up in Memphis, Tennessee and her work as a coordinator with the Mississippi Freedom Summer in Laurel, Mississippi. Her grandparents were devout Baptists, thus religion played a big role in her life growing up. She attended Spelman University and noticed a stark difference in the way Black people were treated in Memphis versus Atlanta. Dr In this interview, Dr. Gwendolyn Zoharah Simmons describes her involvement in the Civil Rights Movement, specifically her membership in the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC). During her childhood, she lived in a loving community and was taught about slavery from her grandmother. While at Spelman University, she left school to join SNCC and was sent to Laurel, Mississippi to help organize the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party to register African Americans in a mock voter registration to challenge the existing Democratic Party. Her mission was also to help teach African American children in two freedom schools, which ended up being firebombed by the Ku Klux Klan. Later in life, Dr. Simmons became a regional coordinator for National Council of Negro Women, then moved on to work for the American Friends Service Committee. She was able to travel nationally and internationally with those jobs and eventually went back to school to get her doctorate for which she earned a Fulbright scholarship to study women and Islam in Jordan In this continuation of previous interviews, Dr. Simmons talks about her observation of the Black Campus Movement that was taking place that took place on her college campus at Spellman. She became involved with SNCC in her freshman year and in her sophomore year, she was elected to it coordinating committee. As a part of the coordinating committee she was selected to start the Freedom School in Laurel, Mississippi. She describes her time in Mississippi as director of the school and her involvement in politics. She eventually came to the University of Florida as a professor gives an account of the struggle of Black student life through the eyes of a professor In this interview Gwendolyn Zoharah Simmons continues to talk about her time SNCC and how it changed her. She initially got involved in SNCC back in her days of Spelman as a freshman. Dr. Simmons goes on to talk about the people that influenced her while in SNCC. She became the Freedom School Director in Laurel, Mississippi. IN the beginning, the school was run out of their office. The Klan bombed the building and they lost all of their curriculum resources. The school continued in a community school in Laurel. Dr. Simmons eventually became a professor at the University of Florida. She goes on to connect her past with the Freedom School with her present day profession In this interview Gwendolyn Zoharah Simmons continues to talk about her time SNCC and how it changed her. She initially got involved in SNCC back in her days of Spelman as a freshman. Dr. Simmons goes on to talk about the people that influenced her while in SNCC. She became the Freedom School Director in Laurel, Mississippi. IN the beginning, the school was run out of their office. The Klan bombed the building and they lost all of their curriculum resources. The school continued in a community school in Laurel. Dr. Simmons eventually became a professor at the University of Florida. She goes on to connect her past with the Freedom School with her present day profession Doris Manning was born and raised in Archer, Florida, in the 1940s. She relates some of the history of her mother and her father, and talks about growing up in Alachua County in the mid-20th century. She describes some of her experiences with segregation, including the ways her parents sheltered her from it, such that she was in high school when she was first confronted by requirements to go to the back door of a restaurant, etc. She also relates that Halloween was a time that White people in Archer would harass the Black community and play mean-spirited pranks. She also relates her development as a cook over her lifetime, from baking cakes to making pickles, and how she learned much of it from her mother. She describes a number of the service organizations that she was involved in at the time of the interview, and also relates some of her thoughts on showing respect for elders Sherry Sherrod Dupree discusses some of the personal and historical significance of the Rosewood Massacre in her view as a researcher who has devoted a great deal of her time into not only uncovering more of the history, but creating exhibits and tours, and outreaching to Rosewood survivors and their descendants. She relates her experiences in working with these families, and in creating public programming and exhibits. She also discusses the challenges in bringing Black history curriculum into schools in Florida, and more generally creating a more honest portrayal of the state’s history. Ms. Dupree talks about some of her family history, which sprawls from Florida to North Carolina, to New York, and into Montreal, and her memories of growing up in North Carolina and dealing with segregation both where she lived and when she traveled. She also describes some of her work on the Dozier School for Boys in Marianna, Florida, and some of her other work in fighting historical injustices that have not yet been redressed Sherry Sherrod Dupree discusses some of the personal and historical significance of the Rosewood Massacre in her view as a researcher who has devoted a great deal of her time into not only uncovering more of the history, but creating exhibits and tours, and outreaching to Rosewood survivors and their descendants. She relates her experiences in working with these families, and in creating public programming and exhibits. She also discusses the challenges in bringing Black history curriculum into schools in Florida, and more generally creating a more honest portrayal of the state’s history. Ms. Dupree talks about some of her family history, which sprawls from Florida to North Carolina, to New York, and into Montreal, and her memories of growing up in North Carolina and dealing with segregation both where she lived and when she traveled. She also describes some of her work on the Dozier School for Boys in Marianna, Florida, and some of her other work in fighting historical injustices that have not yet been redressed Sherry Sherrod Dupree discusses growing up in Johnston County, North Carolina, including her parents’ occupations and the things they did to raise their family together. Her father was a railroad porter, and she describes some of the history of Black railroad porters as well. Mrs. Dupree also describes her experiences with higher education, acquiring two masters degrees, and she relates how she met her husband and how she ended up working at the University of Florida. She describes some dramatic moments from working at Smathers Library in the 1980s, including a shooting that took place involving a disgruntled employee. She also describes her career since that time, and some of the projects she has worked on In this interview, Joel Buchanan describes some of his family history and his experiences growing up in Gainesville, Florida, in the 5th Avenue neighborhood. Mr Joel Buchanan gave this talk at the SPOHP event, Black History in Florida: Where We Stand In the Age of Barack Obama. He shares his experiences in depth of being one of the first three Black students to integrate Gainesville High School. The discussion ranges from the initial planning that led to the integration, to the difficult experiences of the students in actually integrating the school, and the difficulty he found in finding any assistance whenever he needed help with subject matter. He also describes some of the sense of rejection he experienced from the Black community after he left Lincoln High School to integrate the White school in town. The talk concludes with question and answer with the audience Jessica Irvin Jones grew up in Green Cove Springs, Florida, and moved to Alachua when she was fourteen. In describing her family history, she relates that her grandmother had Native ancestry while her grandfather was what was known as a “quarterman.” She shares some memories of going to A.L. Mebane High School in Alachua, and the long walk she and her peers had to undertake every day from LaCrosse to Alachua to go to the school. She also discusses her family history of farming, and her husband’s work as a minister in the African Methodist Episcopal church. Ms. Jones describes her philosophy as being one of generosity and openness, and laments the gradual loss of a sense of community that she feels was much stronger when she was younger Alicia Antone was born in Providence, Rhode Island. She describes her family life and the study habits her older sister instilled in her, and the effect that family trips had on her in terms of appreciating cultural diversity. She also describes the path that led her from initially pursuing veterinary science to eventually moving into acquiring masters degrees in library science and public administration. Ms. Antone describes some of the fundraising and event planning activities that she undertook as part of her work Clarence Pollard was born in Gainesville, Florida, and his parents came from Rosewood. He attended Lincoln High School in Gainesville, and entered the US Army before graduating, serving in the Mediterranean theatre during World War II. He relates the significance he finds in personal conduct and comportment, and the importance of religion in his life Isaiah Branton grew up in Otter Creek, Florida, and is a US Marine Corps veteran. He shares some of his family history, including his father’s pastoring history, and also the oral history passed down to him regarding some of his enslaved ancestors, who were brought by the plantation owner from South Carolina down to Alachua County, Florida. He relates a complex and detailed history of the enslaved people of Alachua County and the family histories before and after Emancipation. He explains how much of this history was passed down to him from his grandmother Laura Dixie, a lifelong union organizer, political activist, and Civil Rights Movement veteran, describes some of her activism over the years. At W. T. Edwards hospital in Tallahassee, she worked with a White secretary who would secretly share administrative memos so that she could confront the realities of racist and discriminatory practices through her organizing. She also describes taking integration into her own hands by using a fingernail file to take down all of the “White” and “Colored” signs segregating the bathrooms and throwing them in the trash. She describes what working at the hospital was like in general, and then how she began her involvement in the American Federation of State, County, and Municipal Employees (AFSCME) union and some of the activities within the union. She also relates memories of Reverend C.K. Steele, and the Civil Rights Movement activities that they both were involved in. She contextualizes some of these activities with discussion of her memories and experiences of Jim Crow Florida, and relates the importance of having multiple active organizations like NAACP, SNCC, and so on, that fight to defend the people’s rights and create opportunities for them This interview takes place during Black Alumni weekend at the Hilton hotel September 5th, 2013. Ms. Deborah Moss talks about her journey before and after being a student at the University of Florida. She describes her student life at Broward Hall and how that was the reunion place of her and her friends. This place was known as the gathering place for a lot of African Americans in the 1970s, as she described that this was the place where she met her husband. In addition, Ms. Moss says that her and her husband have influenced her daughters decision of coming to UF and her daughters journey when applying and being accepted in UF In this interview conducted at Black Alumni Weekend, University of Florida alumna Retha Mae Foxworth goes in depth about her experiences as a Black student in the 1970s. She describes what it was like for her to navigate the racial environment of UF at that time. She talks also about her classes and studying, as well as the recreational activities that she and her peers engaged in. Ms. Foxworth concludes with her appreciation for UF and the life lessons it taught her Jimmy Bobbitt was born in Harperville Mississippi, lived in Cocoa Beach, and attended a segregated elementary and High School before he transferred to Rockledge High as part of integration. After graduation, he attended community college for a week and dropped out to work as a mail clerk for Pan American Airlines on the Patrick air Force Base and lived at home. Then, his old principal came to his door and told him that UF was trying to admit additional minority students, and later matriculated at the University. At UF he found that minority groups usually didn’t associate with others Yvonne Hinson was born in 1947 in Gainesville, Florida. She graduate from Lincoln High School while some of her siblings moved up North to New York. She talks about her siblings and family life, and her parents’ and grandparents’ strong belief in education; college was an expectation. She participated in sit-ins, pickets, and parades in order to bring awareness to the fight for racial equality, and in 2008 canvassed for President Barack Obama This interview took place at Black Alumni Weekend in 2009. Jerricka Gunter talks about her experience in the University of Florida as an undergraduate and graduate student in Business. She talks about her family background and upbringing in Haines City, and her family’s educational background and how they influenced her decision in attending college at UF In this interview, University of Florida alumnus Warren Arthur McCluney details his family’s rich history, the importance of family in his life, and as his experience leaving it all behind to come to university. McCluney grew up in a rural neighborhood and he describes his early years in school when integration first came into effect and the Black experience during that time. Furthermore, he explains the transition and shock that comes with moving from a small town to being a first-generation college student Diana Bell was born in Daytona Beach, and attended Daytona Beach Junior College. She then transferred to the University of Florida for her undergraduate degree in English Education from 1967-1969, and came back to receive her master’s in Special Education in 1971 after seeing emerging need to educate special needs students, and completed her education a year later. She describes school in the 1960s as turbulent, with social unrest, drug culture, and Civil Rights. At UF, she was excluded from the sororities and had no invitations to join organizations. She helped establish the first Black Student Union, which brought in important Black speakers to campus, such as Muhammad Ali. She also discusses the sit-in at Tigert Hall, and the difficulties in wanting to participate in demonstrations but being too afraid to get thrown out of school and disappoint her parents. Finally, she talks about social changes at UF through her children’s eyes This interview was conducted at the 2009 University of Florida Association of Black Alumni reunion. Keith Yarbrough grew up in Rockledge, Florida, and attributes attending UF to an opportunity that he had to see the campus when he was in ninth grade. He explains the decision-making process that led to his majoring in Pharmacy In this interview conducted at the Association of Black Alumni annual reunion in September 2010, Keith Yarbrough briefly describes how he ended up at the University of Florida, and what his experience was like as a student. He also offers some advice for current and future students in terms of getting the most out of their experience at UF In this interview, University of Florida (UF) alumna Westanna Harvey Bobbit explains how she was able to afford school without funding from her family as well as her experiences as an African American student. She goes in depth about the weird reactions she got for being one of the only black students to ride a bicycle around campus. Mrs. Bobbit is a die-hard gator who recalls her time at UF fondly. Furthermore, Bobbit recalls the difficulties she and her husband faced in finding a job after graduating and how this eventually landed them in the military, another experience she is greatly appreciative of. Lastly, Bobbit expresses her disillusionment with UF for not have any recorded history its black students In this interview, University of Florida (UF) alumna Stephanie Pollard details her love for education as well as her experiences as an outspoken woman during her academic years. She goes in depth about the stereotypes she felt she had to combat about the role of women in and out of the home. Furthermore, Ms. Pollard highlights the importance of diversity within the school to aid in moving away from this mindset. She also outlines what she would change about the UF Public Health program if she were president of the school. Lastly, she explains her concern for the existing Black culture in Gainesville due to gentrification, a lack of inclusivity within the community, and limited minority programs within the university Sam Taylor grew up in the 5th Avenue neighborhood of Gainesville, Florida, raised by a father who was a business owner with an eighth grade education and a mother with a master’s degree from Columbia University and an undergraduate degree from Tuskegee University. He relates the general lack of connection that he experienced growing up between the University of Florida and the Black community of Gainesville, other than once sitting in the segregated bleachers at a UF football game, and having an uncle who worked as a custodian. Mr. Taylor attended Lincoln High School in Gainesville, and he describes his family life and what it was like to grow up in Gainesville during the Jim Crow era. He also describes some of his family history and the impact that his parents had on him growing up, as well as Lincoln teachers who had particular impact. He matriculated into the University of Florida in 1964, and later went on to become the first Black student body president. He describes the importance of those leadership roles in terms of their impact on his later life. He also was running for president during Black Thursday, the student demonstration that led to the founding of the Institute of Black Culture and the African American Studies Program, and he describes the event as he experienced it In this interview conducted during the 2009 UF Black Alumni Weekend, Tonyaa Weathersbee describes some of her experiences in high school in Jacksonville during the early 1970s, which was the beginning period of K-12 integration in Florida Kristen Yarbrough was born in Rockledge, Florida. In this interview during the 2009 University of Florida Black Alumni Weekend, she relates some of her family history, and explains how she ended up attending UF and what her experiences were as a student—having just graduated about half a year prior to the interview. She offers a very positive account of her student experience, and also offers some advice to prospective students who are looking to go to college In this interview conducted at the 2009 University of Florida Black Alumni Weekend, Carlton Gant relates that he was raised in Delray Beach, Florida, and describes the process that led to his attending UF. He discusses some of the things that helped him to be successful as a student. Mr. Gant describes having an open debate in class to defend his beliefs as a Creationist in the face of his professor’s teaching of evolution. He mentions his involvement in the Black Student Union, and other organizations and activities on campus. Mr. Gant also offers some thoughts on what UF could do to improve its recruitment and retention of a diverse and excellent group of students, and his concern that some of the programs that had been in place were eroding by the time of the interview. He also relates that he was part of the group that created the Student Nighttime Auxiliary Patrol (SNAP) on the UF campus, and expresses his pride that it is still a successful and active program This interview was conducted in collaboration with the Hawthorne branch of the Alachua County Library. Rev. Carnell Henderson and his wife Jettie lived in an area outside of Hawthorne, Florida known as Corn Crossing, until at the age of about nineteen years they moved to Hawthorne proper. Henry W. Jones was raised in Hawthorne itself, and lived for a time in Campfield, Florida. The two men, who have known each other for most of their lives, are treated as the primary focus of the interview, though Mrs. Henderson speaks up periodically throughout, and becomes a more central narrator toward the end of the interview. The men relate a lot of their family history, including some reflections on Chester Shell, for whom the Black High School in Hawthorne was named. They talk at considerable length about Hawthorne and the surrounding area in the early 20th century, including the experience of living under Jim Crow segregation, and labor conditions for migrant farmworkers, and in pulpwood logging camps. They also discuss a number of people and buildings related to Hawthorne history specifically, and some of the traditions to be found there such as the annual Homecoming. Rev. Henderson also relates his love of, and adeptness at, fishing. Mrs. Henderson relates her wartime experience on the homefront, as well as her experiences working at Tacachale Developmental Disability Center and the process of racial integration there. Mrs. Henderson also describes some of her world travel, including going to Buenos Aires and to Australia. The interview closes with some reflections on religion and current social problems Hazel Gordon was raised in Gainesville, Florida, and discusses her experiences in and reflections on Lincoln High School. She was going to Lincoln when schools were integrated and she was transferred to Howard Bishop Middle School. She describes a considerable disparity between the school environment she remembers at Lincoln and what she perceives as the current conditions of schools. She also relates that she met her husband at Lincoln High in seventh grade, and they started dating seriously in eleventh grade. She also offers some thoughts on the presidency of Barack Obama and the prospects of ending racism in the future Alma Russ describes growing up in Gainesville, Florida, including weekly routines and leisure activities like watching baseball games or going to Silver Springs. She also describes what it was like to grow up under Jim Crow segregation, and how it impacted her mother in the workforce, and the family’s entertainment options in terms of things like movie theaters. She also discusses her experiences in Lincoln High School, including some of her favorite memories and people who influenced her. After graduating, she worked at the Federal Bureau of Investigations in Washington DC for three months, after which, fed up with city life, she moved back to Gainesville. She also describes attending Florida A&M University for a while before returning to Gainesville and working at the sheriff’s office until she retired. She expresses regret that Lincoln closed, and offers some thoughts on the current state of race relations in Gainesville In this interview, Jeraldine Williams-Shaw recounts his time attending Middletown High School in Tampa, Fl. Owing his success to his community of friends and family, Mr. Williams became a strong leader in his journalistic career. He talks of his trip to South Africa as part of a non-governmental organization to observe for the election of Nelson Mandela and how it allowed him to capture history by writing for Ebony, South Africa. Mr. Williams states his wish for more cohesiveness in the university for its future students as he remembers his college career during the 1960s Mable Lock was interviewed on June 12, 2010 by Marna Weston at the Armstrong High School 1964 class reunion. Ms. Lock was one of the leaders in organizing the reunion for the high school and this was their third since 1993. She remembers her family and where they all grew up, and briefly talks about what she remembers as a student at Armstrong High School. Ms. Lock mentions David Pugh and Andrew Wallace as both being inspiring educators Ms. Burks begins the interview with stating where she and her family are from. She says that her great grandfather was involved in Florida’s Rosewood incident. She tells the interviewer that her family avidly went to church and the interview concludes with Ms. Burks listing the names of her siblings At her forty-sixth high school reunion, Helen Spells Nelson is interviewed about her experience attending the Black-only Armstrong High School. Ms. Nelson attended this high school at the start of the Civil Rights Movement, during a time when integrating schools was heavily discussed. Ms. Nelson explains her excitement for the event and claims attending her Black-only school was a “blessing” that helped her better understand her background. She connects this sense of understanding with the growth of leadership skills. At the end of the interview, she contributes success to her trust in God In Fayetteville, North Carolina, Janice McMillan is interviewed at the Armstrong High School reunion for the class of 1964. Ms. McMillan discusses her family lineage and then elaborates on her experiences at Armstrong High School, a Black-only school. Ms. McMillan argues that in her days, school was a place of work while in the present, children lack the same initiative due to their lifestyles at home. Her class produced many leaders, and she contributes this success to the involvement of parents. Following this conversation, Ms. McMillan provides her various reactions to historical events ranging from assassinations to Americans walking on the moon. Lastly, she discusses her initial exposure to Jim Crow and how she responded through activism Mr. Carey Godwin is being interviewed by Marna Weston on June 12, 2010 at the Armstrong High School Class of 1964 Reunion. The interview begins with Mr Mary Maple Smith martin was born February 20, 1926 and is the great-aunt of the interviewer Marna Weston. She reflects on her childhood living on a farm as well as her early education at Lonely Hill. Mrs. Martin also goes into detail about her family and being the seventh out of ten siblings. She recounted her daily chores as well as went into detail regarding what she would do for fun. Mrs. Martin then reflected on attending Armstrong High School and her involvement in extracurricular activities and events such as Glee Club, prom and graduation. Towards the end, she talks about voting for Kennedy and recalls the day of his assassination. She closes with her daily activities living in Roseboro, North Carolina Geraldine Smith was born June 14, 1942 and is the cousin of the interviewer Marna Weston. She was born and raised in Autryville, North Carolina. She reflects on her childhood growing up and working on a farm. She mentions her early education at Lonely Hill, as well as Union Hill Baptist Church, and J.W. Seabrook, the first Black school. She discusses voting and working in factories the Cedar Creek district during segregation. She mentions organizing at her jobsite to desegregate a Whites-only bathroom. She recalls hearing about events like President Kennedy’s assassination Alice Hayes grew up in Gainesville, Florida. She attended Lincoln High School, and attended Santa Fe Community College after graduating. She describes some of the impacts that Jim Crow segregation had on her life in terms of public facilities and the public education funding and supplies at Lincoln. She recalls some of the Civil Rights Movement activities in Gainesville, including the burning of several White-owned businesses on the Black business district on 5th Avenue. She also relates a number of the Black-owned businesses that she remembers there. Ms. Hayes describes her history of church membership, and some thoughts on religion. She also describes her work history as a medical technologist, working for thirty years at a clinical laboratory and twenty years at the University of Florida student infirmary Willette Walker was born and raised in Gainesville, Florida, and attended Lincoln High School. Her parents got married at the ages of sixteen and twelve; her father worked as a plumber and owned land out of town that the family kept cattle and goats on, and she talks about their overall family dynamic in the household. Ms. Walker relates that she had been selected to integrate Gainesville High School, but she decided she didn’t want to do it and eloped instead, getting her GED later. She talks about her experiences with education, and the differences that she sees in the school system today. She also discusses the role of religion in her life growing up, which mandated a particularly strict code of conduct. She reflects on the ways that some things which were prohibited by the community years ago may now be unfairly stigmatized out of context. Ms. Walker describes what it was like to live under segregation in Gainesville, and talks about her involvement in the Civil Rights Movement, the lack of knowledge among current generations of icons like Fannie Lou Hamer or songs like the Negro National Anthem, and the persistence of racism. She also relates more information about her family, and her connections to other people at the family reunion where the interview takes place Richard Black participated in the Civil Rights Movement in South Florida (apparently in Palm Bay, Melbourne, or Homestead), and begins the interview describing what it was like to be on the picket lines and deal with harassment without responding violently. He also talks about current community dynamics in Miami and South Florida, and describes what the Black community and Black-owned businesses were like prior to integration. He and several interlocutors nearby also describe some of the juke joints that they remember. Mr. Black also describes how the Vietnam War impacted the Black community Treva Walker grew up in Perry, Florida, and describes her experiences with education in Perry. She also describes a little of her family history, and mentions having attended Florida A&M University after graduation, and living in different parts of the US after that Jimmy Pittman begins the interview by stating that he has lived in Perry, Florida for his entire life. Jerkins was an all-Black school in Perry, but after integration in 1970, Pittman took welding classes on its campus. He discusses what it was like in Perry at the time of integration and the experience of being White in a formerly Black school. He stresses that it was “not a big deal.” Pittman says that integration was forced on the people and that White and Blacks alike did not want it to happen. He says that people eventually accepted integration because they knew there was nothing they could do to stop it, and Blacks and Whites in Perry have learned to get along since then. The interview concludes with Pittman talking about Jerkins’ abandoned facilities and how they could be useful to people today Sarah McCray Hall describes attending Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida; she was a valedictorian, a member of the chorus, and an athlete. She talks about her family life growing up in Perry, and the values and discipline instilled in her in school and at home. She also describes what segregation was like in Perry, and how she learned to navigate it in her life. She also recalls seeing Ray Charles perform when he was still a young musician not widely known. Ms. Hall reflects on some of the societal changes she has seen over her lifetime Estic Rollings describes going to Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida. She recalls the impacts of Jim Crow segregation on the school, and also the impact that the teachers’ dedication and commitment had on students at Jerkins. Their lessons inspired her to become a career educator in Taylor County, and she relates that inspiration also to the success of her children. Ms. Rollings discusses changes between her youth and today, and discusses some of the complexity of the impact of integration on the Black community This interview with Horace McLeod discusses the community outreach at the Jerkins Community Center in Perry, Florida. He describes the community’s necessity to interact with one another and how education plays a part in this county not only for its young citizens, but for the elderly as well. He discusses food projects and clothes outreach to poor people, seniors, and other people in need in the community. Mr Dr. McLeod is interviewed at the Jerkins High School Reunion in Perry, Florida. The interview begins with discussion about why the Taylor County Leadership Council put together the reunion and how it is important for entire families, especially children, to attend. McLeod then talks about how the Leadership is trying to raise money to establish a Jerkins African American Museum. He then talks about Lockridge Park in Perry and the lawsuit that made it come to be. McLeod ends the interview by saying it is important to continue the Jerkins High School Reunions because the alumnus are a dying breed and much can be, and still is, learned from them Drew Frazier describes attending Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida, after which he entered the US Army 82nd Airborne Division during the Korean War, and after that the US Air Force. He speaks fondly of his experiences at Jerkins, particularly in athletics, and talks about the overall discipline and structure that he grew up with in the community and particularly through his mother. He also talks about the philosophies that he has developed throughout his lifetime Willie Hightower discusses his experiences attending Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida. He graduated in 1968, two years prior to when the school was closed during integration. He describes some of the frustration in the community during integration, as well as various aspects of Jerkins’ curriculum and athletics programs that endeared it to the community. He also relates some of his family history in terms of moving to and living in Perry. Mr. Hightower served in the US Air Force reserve during the Vietnam War, and he describes some of that experience also. Upon returning, he attended Florida A&M University, and at the time of the interview had just retired from a thirty-five year career with the US Post Office Lillie Tinsley describes her experiences attending Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida. Her mother, Minnie Thomas, was the first cook who was hired at Jerkins—attending Florida A&M University to become certified to be able to plan the meals for the children. Ms. Tinsley talks about some of the influential teachers and other individuals who inspired her at Jerkins, and offers thoughts on life in the community overall, both in the past and in contemporary times William Monroe describes the personal significance of Jerkins High School, which he attended while growing up in Perry, Florida In this interview, Arthur Thomas details his young life spent on the move and the discrepancies he noticed between the North and the South within education and gender roles. Mr. Thomas also explains the difference in education between segregated and integrated schools as well as the sense of community that could be fostered within the former. Furthermore, he details his historical beliefs as well as the importance that religion holds within his life. He focuses heavily on what plagues the black population and how it relates to religion Flora Underwood was raised in Perry, Florida, and attended Jerkins High School. In this interview, she describes her family, and her experiences going to Jerkins, as well as some of the history of racism in Perry. She offers some reflections on Black community life and dynamics in Perry as well Walter Bishop was interviewed in Perry, Florida at the Jerkins Historic Marker Dedication and Reunion. First he discusses how he was formerly the Vice President of the NAACP in Taylor County (what he believes to be one of the most racist towns in the South). Mr. Bishop mentions Martin Luther King and how he inspired him to fight racism nonviolently. He then discusses Jerkins High School and what integration and the effect of the closing of the school on the Black community. He then discusses the importance of education and for Black students to have Black teachers and other school administrators to relate to. Mr. Bishops talks about how technology has taken away jobs from Black workers and then the interview transitions into him discussing his genealogy. The interview concludes with Mr. Bishop stating that the Reunion is an important event because it is imperative for young Blacks to remember the Jerkins legacy Isabella Diane Taylor Bollet grew up in Norristown, Pennsylvania, raised by a father from Norristown and a mother from Jacksonville, Florida. She describes her family history, including various aspects of their collective mixed-race heritage, and also describes growing up in Norristown, and also her activities with the NAACP there. In 1961, she moved to Hawaii In this interview, Lawrence Hughes Jr. recalls his time at Jerkins High School during its reunion and the sense of community within his home and the school which aided in his academic success. He also details the struggle of consolidating his identities as black and American at the same time. Furthermore, Mr. Hughes recalls his family’s role in his success as well as the success that his siblings have seen. He also explains how he believes that a lot of hunger for growth and knowledge was lost once schools were integrated. Overall, he focuses on the importance of community, family, and religion Rev. Chester Demps discusses his experiences going to Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida. He describes living in Perry all his life, except for the time of his service in the US Army during the Vietnam War. He also relates some of his family history in Perry. Rev. Demps reflects on the particular personal importance he places on the athletics program at Jerkins, and how it impacted him later in life and prepared him for leadership positions. He discusses also the influential role that certain teachers had on him and his peers, and also reflects on the impact of segregation on Jerkins and on Black life in Perry more broadly Kenneth Dennis was interviewed at the Jerkins Community Center in Perry, Florida. Mr. Dennis graduated from Taylor County High School, and at the time of the interview was serving as a member of the school board. He discusses different phases of a project he is involved in to preserve and revitalize Jerkins High and the African American community in Perry. He says this effort and events such as the ongoing reunion are a way to “rekindle” the pride and dignity of the Black community. After the interview, Mr. Dennis is going to speak at the reunion and he wants to discuss how the community needs to communicate and be encouragement to each other Ms. Holmes was interviewed at the Historic Marker Dedication and Jerkins High School Reunion in Perry, Florida. The beginning of the interview is Ms. Holmes’ short experience at Jerkins. Most of the interview is Ms. Holmes frying chicken for the event. There is discussion of a dance at Jerkins where James Brown’s “Papa Got a Brand New Bag” was performed. At the end of the interview, Ms. Holmes states she thinks three hundred people are going to show up to the reunion Walter Wright was born and raised in Newton, Georgia, and was interviewed at the 2010 Jerkins High School Reunion in Perry, Florida, about his experiences on the Taylor County Leadership Council. He grew up on a farm with his grandparents, and describes the experience of living there. He also offers some reflections on current community dynamics and the younger generations, and shares some of his family history Kenny Sanders describes his and his parent’s experiences going to Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida. He also offers some description of his grilling practices at the Jerkins Reunion where the interview takes place. He shares some of his family history as well. Mr. Sanders reflects on the continued problems of racism and race relations in Perry, and offers thoughts on the value of the reunions Deloris Dunwoody Jay reflects on growing up in Foley, Florida and attending Jerkins High School in the town of Perry. She also relates some of her family history, how her parents ended up in Perry, and other memories of their lives. She describes her own experiences going to Jerkins, and then her experiences with higher education, attending North Florida Junior College and then Florida A&M University. She then relates her work history, primarily in the education system in Taylor County, and talks about her children Thelma Walker Newberry reflects on growing up in Perry, Florida, and attending the Black school in Perry that preceded Jerkins High School. She describes some of the moves her family made to different parts of town, a particularly memorable teacher, and some of the changes that she has seen in the community over time. Her school stopped at the tenth grade, so to graduate high school she needed to be awarded a scholarship, and she describes commuting by train Betty White Hughes reflects on growing up in Perry, Florida, and the experiences of herself and most of her family attending Jerkins High School. She describes some of her family history, and her early schooling which took place in churches and other buildings before she arrived at Jerkins. She also discusses the community values she grew up with, and the impacts that integration had on the Black community in Perry. And she mentions some of the teachers at Jerkins who most impacted her Edwina Moore grew up in Perry, Florida, in Lee Cypress Quarters, and in seventh grade attended Boylan Haven boarding school in Jacksonville. She describes life in the quarters growing up, and eating a considerable amount of wild game. She also describes some of her family history, and then describes some of the different Black neighborhoods in Perry. After graduating high school, she attended Florida A&M University and became involved in the Civil Rights Movement there. She concludes with some reflections about her mother and about the change she’s seen in the sense of community in Perry Carolyn Mickens Glenn was born in Ty Ty, Georgia, and graduated from Jerkins High School in Perry, Florida, about which she shares her memories and thoughts in this interview. She talks about the importance of the heritage of Jerkins as well as the change in a sense of community that she feels has taken place over her lifetime, and then relates some of her family history. She also talks about her friends from when she was young, and what she would do for recreation in those times. And she describes her participation in Jerkins athletics Lottie Mae White Brown grew up in Lamont, Florida, and describes her family’s history that includes Sermon, Florida, and Colquitt, Georgia. Her family worked as sharecroppers in Lamont, and she attended school three months out of the year. She participated in the Jerkins High Reunion partly because at the time of the interview she lived across the street from the building, but she also obtained her GED from Jerkins later in life. She mentions some of the Jerkins teachers who were influential in her life, and asserts that just being near Jerkins and interacting with the people involved in it was empowering to her. Ms. Brown describes what it was like to work as a domestic worker in Perry, which was where she was when she learned Martin Luther King Jr. had been shot. She also offers some messages to today’s youth Mary Gant was born in Ty Ty, Georgia, and later moved to Perry, Florida and attended Jerkins High School. She describes the importance of what she learned at Jerkins, and some of the community dynamics that were part of the school. She also laments the change in a sense of community in contrast to what she experienced when she was growing up. She offers advice to the current generation, and also ruminates on the state of racism in the present day This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Jackie Ayers was born and raised in Nashville, Arkansas, and then went to school in Essex, Missouri. He describes some of his experiences growing up, as well as his family history. He explains that he ended up at UF because he was recruited by Rod McDavis, who insisted that UF was the “Stanford of the South,” and then he worked a variety of administrative jobs at UF for a number of years. He also speaks highly of what it was like to work with former UF president John Lombardi, and he shares his reflections on the student murders in Gainesville in 1990. Dr. Ayers also reconciles his experiences with racism at UF and wider Gainesville with his belief that UF offers a strong foundation for current and future students This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. James and Michele Miller discuss some of their respective family histories; they met and married in Atlanta. It is Mrs. Miller’s first time attending Black Alumni Weekend, but Mr. Miller, the alum of the two, is attending for his third time and speaks about his experiences going to UF This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Eugene Pettis was the keynote speaker of the occasion. He explains how and why he ended up coming to UF, where he completed his undergraduate studies and his law degree both. Mr. Pettis was the first Black Student Body Treasurer at UF, and he describes some of his other achievements while a student, and talks about the opportunities that he feels he was afforded at UF. He describes some of the Black-owned businesses he used to go to off-campus when he was a student, as well as other interactions with the city of Gainesville outside of UF. Mr. Pettis also notes some of his instructors and mentors, and then discusses some of his vision and hopes for the future of the UF Association of Black Alumni This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Born and raised in Tampa, Clarence Brown matriculated to UF in 1982 after John Boatwright came to his high school to recruit students. He describes some of his family’s history, and his experiences growing up in Tampa and experiencing the beginnings of integration and school bussing while seeing on television some of the more violent responses to the process elsewhere in the US. He describes his dismay with his dormmates at UF because of the lack of respect they showed the housekeeper, Ms. Katie. He also describes some of his adventures while attending UF. Mr. Brown’s first job at UF was at the Institute of Black Culture (IBC). He also describes participating in the UF Gospel Choir, Black Student Union, Student Government, and pledging Alpha Phi Omega fraternity. He explains some of what he finds most meaningful about being part of the Association of Black Alumni, and of UF Gators football games This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Joe McCloud grew up with four other siblings who were raised by his mother and grandmother. His mother always instilled that education was necessary for survival. McCloud participated in the upward bound program, which a program to better help high school students transition and adjust to college work and life. After arriving at UF, he quickly became involved with organizing and direct action. During his undergraduate career he helped lead rallies and marches, raised money for the local boys and girls club to reopen its doors, and helped start the Black Student Union. He was also a charter member of Omega Psi Phi fraternity and helped found the institute of Black culture. He was a participant in the Black Thursday protests on the UF campus. In his spare time on weekends he hung out on 5th Avenue and went to the bars and local clubs This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Jacquelyn Williams Jones grew up in southeast Gainesville. She talks about her family, particularly her father and the jobs he worked, and then shares her educational history. Ms. Jones explains the process that led to her coming to UF, as well as how she settled on her major, and some of campus life and the mentors who inspired her as a student. After graduation, she worked for AT&T. She describes the experience of attending Black Alumni Weekend and what it means to her This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Tara Miller was born and raised in Philadelphia. She nearly ignored a possible full ride to UF, but her father brought her to visit the campus and she ended up attending. She discusses her family and the ways they supported her ambitions, and the adjustment it took to move to Florida. Ms. Miller’s first semester at UF was the same semester as the 1990 Gainesville murders, and she describes how she experienced that time. She also describes her love of UF football, and some stories of what campus life was like for her overall This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Maria Golden, graduate of UF College of Nursing in 1985, and her daughter Alicia, who graduated in 2008, interview each other after some coordination by Anna Brodrecht. They discuss Maria’s husband (Alicia’s father), why they each chose to attend UF, and some of their experiences while attending, from parties, to dining options, to recreation. Through the conversation, they compare and contrast the generational and circumstantial differences This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. After AAHP team member Anna Brodrecht coached the two on the nature of the interview, alum Rocky Cusseaux conducted the interview with Ms. Nickie. She explains her reasons for attending UF, and what her experience was like. She also discusses how she navigated the sheer size of the university and her classes, including participation in student organizations, and offers some hopes for the future of UF This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Jerome Josey relates why he came to UF, some of his experiences while a student there, and what he hopes to get out of membership in the Association of Black Alumni This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Andre Gainey grew up in Miami, Florida, and describes the community and some of his educational experiences there. He describes being confronted by a racist White UF student as soon as he first got to his dorm as an incoming freshman. He also describes other variously positive and negative experiences with diversity at UF, and feels that his experiences helped to prepare him for later life. Mr. Gainey recalls some of the things that students did for fun, and some of the friends and mentors he developed during his time as a Gator. Lastly, he expresses his fondness for UF and offers some hopes for its future as an institution This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Kenya Ellis relates some of her family history that ranges from Florida to the Carolinas, and talks about her experiences with education in Orlando, Florida, including the teachers who most influenced her in K-12. She also served in the US Marine Corps after graduating from UF, and discusses life as a Marine as well as the relative scarcity of Black women serving as officers in the Marines. She then relates how she met her husband. Ms. Ellis transitions to describing what she likes the most about UF as an institution, and some of the places she intends to visit while she’s in Gainesville. She also relates some of her memories from her time at UF This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida, primarily by alumna Rachel Nickie after some facilitation by Anna Brodrecht. Mr. Cusseaux grew up in Tampa, and talks about how he ended up at UF and what his early experiences were on campus. He describes being active with the Black Student Union, and with several dance troupes. He also worked at the Institute of Black Culture, and describes the personal importance of IBC and the student organizations he participated in. He concludes by relating the personal significance of the Association of Black Alumni and his hopes for the future of UF In this interview, Henry Lewis talks about his experience growing up in Gainesville and attending the University of Florida in the 1960s. He was a member of the band The Blenders, which first integrated the Gator Growl Katesha Riley and Cydney Hargro interview each other at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend, discussing their experiences going to the University of Florida and the meaning of returning as alumni to events such as the one they are attending In this interview, Kelvin Henry talks about his time as a student at the University of Florida in the early 1980s. He was a member of Kappa Alpha Si and ROTC. He also talks briefly about his time in the Air Force and working for Pepsi Co In this short interview, Dwayne Shaw discusses his time at the University of Florida in the early 2000s In this interview, Cranford Ronald Coleman Jr. talks a little about growing up in Ocala during integration. He also discusses his years at the University of Florida in the late 1960s and early 1970s. He was the first Black student athlete on scholarship, and he shares his experiences on the track and field team and in the classroom at that time. He describes the Civil Rights protests that took place on campus while he was there. He also gives examples of racism he experienced while traveling through the South for track events Ron Coleman talks about what life was like living on the west side of Ocala during the 1950s, including the thriving middle-class Black community he was raised in, and the successful Black businesses on Magnolia Avenue and other locations. Mr This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. The Goodriches interview each other after facilitation by Anna Brodrecht. Stephen Goodrich is interviewed first, describing his upbringing and family history before describing how he ended up going to UF in 1977. He also describes some of his activities on and off campus, and his strategies for success as a student, and asserts that he had to work too hard to party or have too much fun. He then interviews Sikita Goodrich, who also matriculated in 1977. She describes having a difficult time adjusting to Gainesville having come from city life. She also talks about experiences on campus and memories of her time at UF. Both also mention some of the off-campus landmarks in the Black community that they recall going to, such as Mom’s Kitchen or Red’s Two-Spot This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Michael Ashby’s parents immigrated from the Bahamas in the 1940s to Cleveland, Ohio. He matriculated to UF in 1982, and describes the transition that he experienced and how and why he chose UF. He enjoyed math and science classes but struggled with Liberal Arts, and then went into a career as a nuclear engineer. He offers advice to any future incoming students, and describes the struggles he underwent to succeed as a student—including living for four days in the UF Shands Hospital Library because he and his roommate couldn’t afford to turn on the electricity This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Gerald McGill was born in Longstool, Germany, to a father serving in the US Air Force and a mother who was a schoolteacher from South Carolina This interview was conducted at the 2010 Black Alumni Weekend at the University of Florida. Madeline Gervine McCloud grew up in Albany, Georgia, and relates some of her family’s history from the area. She describes herself as a third- generation social worker. She then describes the path that led her to attend the University of Georgia. She was present at Black Alumni Weekend because her husband, Joe McCloud, was attending the event. She talks about how she approaches her work as a social worker, and why she enjoys it This was recorded with several of the children present at a family reunion where Isaiah Branton had invited SPOHP to conduct oral history interviews. The children speak about their families, and some of their preferred recreational activities, while also learning how to use the Marantz recorder the team was using for the interviews Thelma Welch first lived in Richland, Georgia, but moved to Gainesville, Florida when she was six years old, where she stayed with Ms. Fannie Glover, co- owner of the Glover and Gill building on 5th Avenue. Mrs. Welch attended Lincoln High School, and she relates her experiences there, including graduating in 1934 as valedictorian. She also describes a lifelong love of music. After graduation, she attended Bethune-Cookman College, and she describes her firsthand memories of Mary McLeod Bethune. However, for financial reasons she transferred to Florida A&M University (FAMU), which she graduated from. She was hired as a teacher by Principa Beatrice Certain was raised in LaCrosse, Florida, and as an adult she worked as a custodian at A.L. Mebane high school. Though she was not a teacher, teachers would sometimes call her in to settle students because she had that kind of influence over them. She relates some of her family history, including her father’s farming activities. She also recalls the names of many of the teachers and administrators who worked contemporaneously with her at Mebane. She also describes enjoying attendance at sporting events at Mebane. She also describes some of the people associated with her time at Mebane, and the things she had to do as custodian. Mrs Roger King was born and raised in High Springs, Florida, and attended Douglass High School in that town until tenth grade, when he started at Mebane High School in Alachua, Florida. He describes it as exciting because Douglass was so small that the library was also a classroom and the bell-ringing room, so the additional resources and space at Mebane were very welcome. Mr. King graduated in 1959, only the third graduating class from A.L. Mebane High. He describes his experiences at the school, and also talks about segregation and its effect on his life and on public spaces and facilities, including schools. He talks about the teachers’ philosophies and approaches in Mebane, and some of his favorite teachers. He also talks about his wife and how they met, and talks about some of his work history after graduating, including service in the US Army, and later working as a surgical nurse after obtaining his Nursing degree from the University of Florida. He also participated in the Civil Rights Movement in Gainesville, Florida, and discusses some of those activities This interview was conducted in front of an audience as part of an oral history workshop at Mt. Carmel United Methodist Church. Roger King relates his earliest memories of High Springs, Florida, and who his neighbors were in the area where he grew up. He also talks about the Black businesses that were running in his youth, and some White-owned businesses that catered to a Black clientele. He also talks about what conditions were like for the Black community during Jim Crow segregation in High Springs, and the sort of community that people built for themselves. He also offers some considerations of the impact and overall significance of the presidency of Barack Obama. Mr. King shares some stories about some of the professional athletes to come out of High Springs, including his brother who played for the Detroit Tigers, and Tracy Ham, who is in the Canadian Football League Hall of Fame Herbert Jones grew up in High Springs, Florida, and transferred from Douglass High School to A.L. Mebane High School in Alachua in 1962. He says that he never came face-to-face with racist segregation, though he knew about water fountains and other examples of it. He shares some of his experiences at Mebane and his memories of High Springs in the 1960s. Mr. Jones attended University of Florida, and participated in the Black Thursday protest in 1971. He withdrew from UF in protest along with many of the other students, and then transferred to the University of South Florida. He talks about some of the clubs and activities at Mebane when he went there, and the food they had in the cafeteria. He also discusses discipline at Mebane, and the activities he participated in such as singing and basketball. He and the interviewer also talk about 1960s fashion Daniel Gainey grew up in Monteocha, Florida, and went to Alachua County Training School and A.L. Mebane High School. He describes his family history, and describes the family farm where he grew up. He shares some of what it was like to live under Jim Crow segregation, and how he learned to navigate it growing up. Mebane High opened toward the end of his high school career, and he was a member of the first graduating class there. He was a football player for the Mebane Hornets. Dr. Gainey recalls some of the teachers who most inspired him at Mebane, and his service in the US Army. He then describes starting in ministry after leaving the Service. He also describes his work history, including union membership and some union organizing work. Dr. Gainey also offers his thoughts on the election of Barack Obama and the current state of society today Marie Calhoun grew up in farm country outside of Newberry, Florida. She describes the community she grew up in, including the gatherings initiated by church events, and the farmwork that she and most other children did growing up, including picking tobacco in the summers. She started attending A.L. Mebane High School in eighth grade. Her father ran his own sawmill. Ms. Calhoun describes some of the activities that she was involved in during her time at Mebane, and also recalls the annual May Day celebrations. She also offers her recollections of segregation, and the Civil Rights Movement in Gainesville. She also offers some thoughts on voting, and then reflects on the importance of her family to her Charles Moore attended A.L. Mebane High School in Alachua, Florida and then went to Florida A&M University. While there, hehad a roommate in college whose father, Eugene White, played baseball in the Negro League. He relates some of the stories that he heard from Mr. White, and some of the other aspects of Negro League history that he learned over the years. He also talks about baseball in the general north/north-central Florida area, and the significance the game had in communities there. Mixed in, he also talks about how he experienced baseball growing up, both as a player and as a fan keeping up with the Major Leagues. He also describes his high school teaching and coaching career of thirty-three years in Florida Cassandra Gassett Davis was born and raised in High Springs, Florida; her father was a teacher at A.L. Mebane High School in Alachua, and her mother taught at Douglass High School in High Springs. She herself was not old enough to attend Mebane before it was closed for integration, so she attended Santa Fe High School in Alachua, but at the time of this interview Ms. Davis was serving as the president of the Mebane High School alumni association. She describes the activities of the alumni association, including the annual reunion. Ms. Davis also talks about her own experiences in the education system, and the importance placed on education in her household. She talks about some of what she knows of her parents’ experiences, and the “little juke” that her father ran Annabelle Williams Baines was born and raised in West Palm Beach, Florida, where she attended Industrial High School. She talks about what she remembers about the community from her youth, including local businesses, and the dynamics of her household in terms of chores and activities. She also describes her parents and the role of her church in her life. She recalls that her favorite teacher, Mr. Stevens was an activist in an early era, in that he was fired for standing up for the rights of Black teachers. She talks about some of the local organizations and personalities, and discusses the NAACP activity in the area Verdell Robinson was part of conducting an oral history collection on Ogden Elementary School, a Black school in Bland, Florida; the project is catalogued at SPOHP as OSP. She talks about those interviews, and about the school and her own experiences going there—including pageants and musical events. Ms. Robinson was born and raised in Bland, and she describes some of her family history going back multiple generations, including some of the settlement of Bland. She discusses the chores she was responsible for growing up, and the family dynamics in her household. She also talks about picking tobacco when she was a child, and about some of the local businesses she remembers going to during the Jim Crow era Glynell Presley grew up in Lake City, Florida, and attended Richardson High School there. He was an active member of the Lake City NAACP at the time of the interview. He talks about growing up in Lake City and his experiences at Richardson, including in athletics. He particularly talks about baseball in the area. Mr. Presley also relates some of his experiences serving in the US Air Force Bernice Daniels Presley was hired to teach in Lake City, Florida in 1963 after she finished her master’s degree in Mathematics from Florida A&M University. Schools in Columbia County were still segregated at that time, and she describes her experiences there and how Jim Crow affected her in her professional and her personal life. She describes participating in a sit-in demonstration in Tallahassee in some detail as well. Mrs. Presley also talks about her family history, which stems back to Georgia, and the hopes that her parents had for her and her siblings’ futures. She also offers some reflections on her hopes for the future, and some advice for younger generations Martha Nell Harris was born and raised in White Springs, Florida, and moved to Lake City at the age of eighteen. She attended Carver High School, and describes the effects of Jim Crow segregation on her life, including her education. The interview is then cut short due to some sort of interruption John Mayo was the president of the Columbia County NAACP at the time of this interview. He was raised on a farm five miles outside of Lake City, Florida, and he missed a good deal of school growing up because he and his siblings had to work on the farm to keep it going while their father worked at a sawmill. They also would sell firewood in the winter. He talks about race relations under Jim Crow segregation in Lake City at that time, and the fear his mother experienced when he joined the NAACP as a teenager in the mid-1950s. He also talks about the need for the NAACP at the time of the interview, and his reflections on ongoing issues with racism in the US Dessie Ella Lee Robinson was raised in Archer, Florida, and was among the earlier Black graduates of the University of Florida. She relates some of her parents’ history, including her mother’s youth in Columbia, South Carolina, and her father’s adventures as a piano player. Ms. Robinson herself graduated from Lincoln High School in Gainesville, Florida. She attended Tuskegee University for her bachelor’s degree, and then got a master’s in Nursing at UF. Her time at Tuskegee coincided with the Civil Rights Movement, and though she did not participate, she shares some memories of the events. She talks about the Black business districts both near Tuskegee and in Gainesville. Ms. Robinson also served in the US Air Force, and offers some thoughts on her experiences, and also talks about her nursing career Mae Isabel Vesta Islar grew up in Tampa, Florida, on the fringes of Ybor City. In this interview, she discusses her family history and their connection to Tampa, as well as her own memories of growing up there. Unlike most Southerners, she went to Catholic school growing up, despite her family attending Baptist and Methodist churches. Her grandfather was a cigarmaker, and her great-grandfather worked as a shrimper in Pensacola. She also was told that her grandmother, from Key West, had a mix of African American, Native American, and Spanish ancestry. She states that her great-grandfather was involved in the founding of Florida A&M University, which she attended for her undergraduate studies. Ms. Islar describes typical daily and weekly life from when she was growing up, and the community in Tampa, which was rather complex. Mrs. Islar’s first teaching job was at Euclid High School in DeLand, Florida, and she escorted a student on a field trip to Cuba just before Castro took power. She then taught at Blake High School in Tampa, then in Newberry, Florida, and then at Lincoln High School in Gainesville, Florida. She also talks at length about her life experiences and adventures overall Yasmin Small grew up in Brooklyn, Jacksonville, and South Florida. She speaks in this interview about Black beauty aesthetics, and how they relate to her life and experiences, mass media, and how beauty is represented and marketed, particularly in racially-marked ways Elsa Frederic grew up in Miami, Florida. She speaks in this interview about Black beauty aesthetics, and how they relate to her life and experiences, and how she gathers information and ideas regarding makeup and beauty. Her mother was born in Haiti, and Ms. Frederic contrasts her mother’s aesthetics with her own Monica Smith is a White woman interviewed for AAHP because she ended up becoming one of the co-founders of the Pleasant Street Historic Society (PSHS). Growing up, she lived in multiple countries, went to college in Greensburg, Pennsylvania, worked at the Library of Congress in DC after graduation, and then moved to New Hampshire with her husband, where they fixed up a home from the 1740s and she learned about historic preservation. When her husband got a job in Gainesville in 1978 at the University of Florida, she began to learn about the destruction of old Black-owned homes in historic neighborhoods of Gainesville, and became involved in fighting it through a call for the enforcement of historic preservation laws and policies. She describes the process of collaborating with 5th Avenue neighborhood residents to get the Pleasant Street Historic District onto the National Register of Historic Places, and talks about some of the activities of the PSHS. She also discusses her experiences with and observatiosn on race relations in Gainesville, and some of the city projects and development projects she was involved with over the years Tom Fay is a White man interviewed for AAHP because his family home is in the southern, historically-White part of the Pleasant Street Historic District, most of which is a historically-Black neighborhood that used to be part of what was known as the 5th Avenue neighborhood. He relates his family history in considerable detail, and he also relates his memories of segregation in Gainesville and his limited interactions with the Black community. Dr. Fay discusses his years attending the University of Florida, his service in the US Army during World War II, and some of his time living in New York and interacting with Julliard students, including a young Leontine Price Gladys Wright was born and raised between Gainesville, Florida and Rochelle, Florida. She attended Shell High in Hawthorne, and then Lincoln High School in Gainesville. After graduating, she attended Gibbs Junior College and then transferred to Florida A&M University, graduating with a degree in Education and working as an educator for the rest of her professional career. She describes some of her memories and experiences during these periods, and offers reflections on what she perceives as a change in the sense of community in society Portia Taylor was born in Raleigh, North Carolina, and moved at the age of seven to Hampton, Virginia, where she grew up. She describes her family history, and both the character of Jim Crow segregation in Hampton that she experienced as well as the process by which she became aware of it. She also talks about her experiences with the Ku Klux Klan, including a cross-burning that was intended to deter her church’s pastor from supporting the Civil Rights Movement. She also attended the all-Black Phoenix High School in Raleigh, and compares her experiences to what she has learned of Lincoln High School in Gainesville from talking to people in Alachua County. She also talks about the important social and cultural role of church in her life and in her community growing up. She shares her memories of Gainesville when she arrived in the 1960s, and also talks about her experiences working at Santa Fe College In this 2011 telephone interview, Emory Harris (born October 5, 1943) discusses his early life and education in Albany, GA, but focuses on his experience in the civil rights movement, especially as a freedom singer for the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee and the Southern Christian Leadership Council. Harris stresses the importance of singing and faith to the movement, and talks about integration strategies and the movement in the Albany, GA which he underscores was successful. He talks about the importance of setting up markers for remembering the movement as well as the need to educate children to continue in the legacy of the civil rights movement Carolyn Palmer grew up in Pensacola, Florida, and was in sixth grade when integration came into effect in regard to her schooling. She describes the neighborhood she lived in and other aspects of the Black community in Pensacola at that time. She also describes the unrest and tension that came with integration, and she describes the presence of the Ku Klux Klan in Pensacola as she witnessed it growing up. Ms. Palmer ended up attending Pensacola Junior College and then Florida A&M, graduating with a Journalism major. She talks about her journalism career, including working in Gainesville, where the interview took place Dr. John C. Rawls was born in East Palatka, Florida, and grew up in Gainesville. He describes his family history and his home life from those early times, including his parents’ parenting philosophy and their family dynamics. Dr. Rawls describes going to Lincoln High School in his youth, and then later returning there to teach—and he reflects on the teaching philosophy that guided him through his career, and that characterized Lincoln High more broadly. Dr. Rawls attended North Carolina A&T University, and shares some of his memories of those experiences as well Madeline Edwards Thompson is a White woman who grew up in High Springs, Florida, and she describes a number of the businesses and the culture of the area as she experienced it growing up. She also discusses some of the Black people who interacted with her and her family during Jim Crow segregation. Ms. Thompson insists that she does not remember there being a tense separation between races, though she does remember tensions during school integration and she describes those in detail. She also talks about other aspects of High Springs currently, and ruminates on how it has and hasn’t changed In this interview, Cornelius Clayton, Jr. (b. October 19, 1943) discusses his family history, his upbringing and childhood education in Jacksonville, Johnson, Melrose, and Palatka, Florida in the 1950s and 1960s, including recollections about living in a segregated society in North Florida. He briefly discusses his career at Shands hospital following high school graduation as well as his two marriages. The bulk of the interview, however, concerns Clayton’s experience in the Vietnam War and with th In this interview, Cornelius Clayton Jr. relates his family history, and talks about growing up and his early experiences in the education system. He also describes Jim Crow segregation in Melrose, Florida, as he experienced it firsthand. Mr. Clayton attended Central Academy High School in Palatka, Florida, and he describes those experiences as well, including the athletics program and what he remembered from Central playing the teams of other area schools. Mr. Clayton also talks about how he started in photography, and relates details of his experience in the Vietnam War, and his post-war struggles in dealing with posttraumatic stress disorder Scherwin Henry, who was a city commissioner in Gainesville, Florida at the time of this interview, was born and raised in Gainesville. He describes his family history, particularly his grandparents and parents, and current family dynamics Ceola Watkins grew up in High Springs, Florida, and she describes her family history and her experiences growing up including the three jobs she worked through high school—all domestic work for White families. In her youth, when her school in High Springs burned down, she moved to Levy County to go to school, and then moved back after they opened the new Douglass High School in High Springs. She attended Florida Normal College in St. Augustine, and graduated from Bethune Cookman College in Daytona Nkwanda Jah grew up in Grandin, Florida, and she talks about her family relationships there and the various things she and other children did to amuse themselves in that rural area. She also recalls watching the film Tarzan at her grandmother’s house on the television, not realizing until adulthood that the ‘exotic’ locale it was filmed in was Ocala, Florida, near to where she was living; her analysis connects the movie to Jim Crow segregation and to persisting racist ideologies and aesthetics. She notes that her high school in Palatka, Central Academy High School, was the first accredited Black high school in the state of Florida. However, she was in ninth grade when integration was enforced, and so she ended up going to school in Melrose and found the experience to be miserable. Ms. Jah first saw Gainesville in the 1960s, and describes some of the Black-owned businesses and other aspects of the community at that time. She also describes starting the Fifth Avenue Festival, and describes her Gainesville-based community activism in extensive detail Paul Ortiz interviews Lawrence Goodwyn about his experience in St. Augustine during the Civil Rights Movement. The resulting article, “Anarchy in St. Augustine,” details the events of the fateful day and Goodwyn’s role as an observer and an investigative journalist. Goodwyn describes how the St. Augustine police allowed member of the Ku Klux Klan in the area, operating as the Ancient City Gun Club, to chase down Civil Rights marchers. Goodwyn believes he heard the Klan members performing the historical Rebel Yell as they chased down Black Civil Rights activists Randy Klemm speaks about representations of African Americans in the Civil War in museum exhibits, and also about visitor/viewer engagement with exhibits. Much of the conversation also deals with the best ways to challenge preconceptions and strengthen visitor engagement with exhibits and with materials Haridelle Taylor Bright was born in Keysville, Georgia, and describes the entanglements of her family history with a slave-owning White family there. She also describes her father as a very successful businessman, who supplied music to juke joints and owned one or more dime stores. Later, her family moved to Augusta, Georgia, and she attended a Catholic school which had always been integrated. She moved to Lawtey, Florida in 1947, and she describes what the town and community was like at that time during the Jim Crow era. She suggests that there wasn’t much racism per se in Lawtey, but does describe witnessing Ku Klux Klan activity in a variety of contexts and times. Offering personal reflections, Mrs. Bright describes her husband and how she met him. And upon prompts by the interviewer, Mrs. Bright offers her memories of various events from Barack Obama’s election to the assassinations of John F. Kennedy and Martin Luther King Jr Alethia Viola Brown grew up in Gordon Chapel, Florida, and starting in seventh grade she had to walk across the county line to attend school in Hawthorne, Florida. She describes the family farm she grew up on in considerable detail, and also shares some of her experiences in school. During World War II, she moved to Portsmouth, Virginia to work as a welder in the Naval shipyard, and she discusses both the pay discrimination she faced as a woman as well as the satisfaction she garnered from welding. After returning to Hawthorne, she started working at Sunland Training Center as a teacher for students with special needs. Mrs. Brown discusses her memories of Jim Crow segregation as well. Because Brown suffered from dementia at the time the interview was conducted, she was assisted in the interview by her sister and daughter, Cheryle B. Sommons Florida Airnetha Yvonne Bridgewater Alford was born in Nassau, Bahamas. She describes her family history, including the move from Nassau to Miami, Florida when she was in middle school, and explains that upon graduating from the University of Florida she was the first college graduate in her family. It was her high school soccer coach who convinced her to apply for college instead of simply enlisting in the US Army due to a lack of funds. The main focus for this interview is Mrs. Alford’s role in planning the events for Celebrating the Firsts, the commemoration from 2008-2009 of a number of the first Black students at the University of Florida to graduate from a variety of majors and programs. She describes the vision for the celebration, as well as the significance of it and of the people whom it was celebrating Jane Ann Adams was Vice President of the University of Florida at the time of this interview. A White woman, she was interviewed for AAHP to share her perspectives on diversity at UF given its relationship to her position at the university, and because of her role in supporting the programs “Celebrating the Firsts,” which between 2008 and 2009 commemorated a number of first Black graduates of different UF majors and programs. Vice President Adams and interviewer Marna Weston share a dialogue reflecting on the events themselves as well as their significance Leitha Nichols begins this interview with the story of a young Black girl who was abducted and murdered in her hometown of Palatka when Ms. Nichols was also a child. She then continues on to talk about life in Palatka when she was growing up, including her educational experiences at Central Academy High School and the mentors she had there. Her father was from Ocala and worked in a sawmill. Ms. Nichols also talks about the family’s self-sufficiency due to the wide variety of crops her grandmother raised, and the work that went into taking care of all of those crops and their livestock Nathaniel Hall grew up in Rochelle, Florida, and he learned quite a bit of the area history over the years. Health problems prevented him from speaking as freely as he might have wished in this interview, but he shares what he can with the help of his wife Rebecca. She also shares what she remembers from when she first arrived in Rochelle in 1938, including the nearby families, businesses, condition of the roads, and athletics and recreation. As Mr. Hall gets tired, Mrs. Hall shares more of her story; she was the first bus driver for the Black students out in the Grove Park/Rochelle/Hawthorne area. Mr. Hall fixed up a panel-body truck with benches in the back because the county would not provide a vehicle initially; as years went by, the county started to provide increasingly better vehicles until she was driving a full school bus. She stopped driving buses when schools closed for integration, and then began working in the school cafeterias. She also describes her own childhood, growing up about five miles south of Micanopy, Florida, near the Alachua County/Marion County line. She closes by sharing the story of how the two of them met Yvonne Robinson grew up in Jacksonville, Florida, and attended Matthew W. Gilbert High School, graduating in 1956. Ms. Robinson attended Florida A&M University, and describes her participation in civil rights demonstrations in downtown Tallahassee during that time. She later ended up teaching at Robert Jenkins Ellerson (RJE) High School in Starke, Florida, and she contrasts the urban and rural ways of life as she experienced them. She discusses her teaching philosophy and the strategies she adopted, and her experiences overall as a teacher at RJE. She also took master’s level courses at University of Florida, Edward Waters College, and Howard University. At the end of the interview, she describes her firsthand memories of and reflections on the process of integration Deborah Walker Sims was born in 1954 and is the adopted daughter of Elizabeth Givens Walker. She reflects on her schooling and community at Robinson Jenkins Ellerson School in Gainesville, FL. It was a tight-knit community. Her mother served as the president of the NAACP for a few years. Ms. Sims discusses girlhood memories of segregation when she was 5. In high school, she enjoyed art and was an officer of the drill team. She had a semi-integrated prom. She joined the Women’s Army Core after graduation. She reflects on where she has lived and educational challenges for her and her peers This interview was conducted at an event celebrating the legacy of Robert Jenkins Ellerson High School in Starke, Florida. Melverine Morris attended during integration, so she experienced the transition to Bradford High School. The interview is very brief This interview was conducted at an event celebrating the legacy of Robert Jenkins Ellerson High School in Starke, Florida. Ms. Jenkins relates that she is actually a graduate of A.L. Mebane High School in Alachua, Florida, and she describes other events commemorating Black schools in the area This interview was conducted at an event celebrating the legacy of Robert Jenkins Ellerson (RJE) High School in Starke, Florida. Mr. Wade is an RJE alum from 1965. He describes playing on the basketball team as perhaps his fondest memory among many, and he relates that he also served in the US 82nd Airborne in the Vietnam War. He talks about the impact that segregation had on the Black community in Starke Valara Petteway, the president of the Alumni Association for the former Black school Robinson Jenkins Ellison in Starke, Florida is interviewed by Marna Weston. She talks about her time attending R.J.E., including her favorite teachers and her participation in the marching and concert bands. She also discusses the summer of 1966 when she had to take a class at the White high school. While she did not experience blatant discrimination there, she got the feeling that she was not welcomed in the school. She documents her experience participating in the sit-in at the University of Florida, dubbed Black Tuesday of 1971. She comments on the work her Alumni association is doing to maintain the legacy of R.J.E and to help the community Brittany O’Neil was a UF Law student at the time of this interview, and she was interviewed at the unveiling of a digital display on a wall in the University of Florida Levin College of Law, titled “The Legacy of Virgil D. Hawkins: The Struggle for Equality at the University of Florida.” She describes the inspiration for the exhibit, some of the work that went into it, and the significance that she finds in it In this interview, Charles Demps talks about his life growing up in Tallahassee, Florida. The family eventually left Tallahasse and went to Perry Florida before settling down in Gainesville, Florida. While settling in Gainesville, Mr. Demps talks about how the town was like during the Jim Crow Era along with some prominent Black neighborhoods and how they have changed now Ellen Jordan grew up in Jonesville, Florida, and moved to Gainesville at the age of fifteen. She talks about the role of her church, Mt. Pleasant, in her life, and about her late husband, who served in the US Army. Mrs. Jordan attended Florida A&M University. She describes the farm she grew up on, and the community in Gainesville, and describes herself as “neutral” about the Civil Rights Movement—neither for nor against. Mrs. Jordan graduated from Lincoln High School. She also describes the barbecue stand she used to run next door to her house, and transitions into memories of the Black businesses that used to be on 5th Avenue in Gainesville Janie Williams was born and raised in the Porters Quarters neighborhood in Gainesville, Florida. Her mother was a domestic worker and her father was a barber, and she describes her family and community life from when she was growing up, including her household chores, the role of her church in her life, local Black businesses, and offers extended reflection on her experiences attending Lincoln High School. In the process, Ms. Williams shares her memories of what it was like to live under Jim Crow segregation, and she also describes policing in Gainesville at that time, and the role of the first Black police officers in the community. Mrs. Williams attended Clark University during the Civil Rights Movement, and relates her experiences seeing nationally-known leaders speaking in the Atlanta area. After returning, she taught at Lincoln High School, and after integration, she was transferred to Glen Springs Elementary Richard McClellan was born and raised in Gainesville, Florida, in the historic Pleasant Street neighborhood. He describes his experiences in attending Lincoln High School, and talks about the Civil Rights Movement in Gainesville though he states that he was not an active participant. His family has been involved with the historic Mt Mary Lee Myrick continues from her previous interview by explaining what she meant in telling the interviewer that “children were children” when she was growing up. She talks about working as a child, including farm work, and the wages that she could collect. She also talks about her parents’ differing approaches to parenting, and what her household was like growing up. She also talks about her pastor in Gainesville, Rev. Thomas A. Wright of Mt. Carmel Baptist Church, and her role in the church over the years. Mrs. Myrick discusses the values that she was raised with, and their continued importance in her life today. She talks about learning to drive for the first time when she was already an adult, and what the experience was like for her Isaac Jones (b. 1943) grew up in Gainesville, Florida. He talks about his experiences growing up with Jim Crow segregation, and also talks about what the 5th Avenue area of Gainesville was like during segregation—the community ethics, the Black-owned businesses, some memories of the Civil Rights Movement, and so on. He is a veteran of the US Air Force, and he describes attending Howard University and then later the University of Florida. He suggests that he was the second Black student to be in the College of Physical Education and Health at UF, and describes being able to clearly see that the instructors were trying to make Black students fail in their classes. Mr. Jones joined the UF football team as a walk-on. He shares some thoughts toward the end of the interview about his vision for revitalizing the economy in the 5th Avenue neighborhood Clara Griffin reflects on her more than fifty years of serving as a hairdresser in Gainesville, Florida in the 5th Avenue neighborhood, and the current state of Black beauty aesthetics and hair fashions. She also talks from her years of experience about different hairstyling techniques, products, and practices, and talks about the monetary aspects of her profession. She also talks about studying in Atlanta to become a beautician, and of competing in fashion shows as a hairstylist. Mrs. Griffin shares some of her memories also of the Black businesses on 5th Avenue, and the changes she has seen in the community Mamie Lee Leath (b. 1927) was born and raised in Gainesville, Florida, in the historic Porter’s Quarters neighborhood. She talks about her family history, including grandparents who had a farm out in Jonesville, and shares the community and household dynamics she grew up with, including her chores, and the businesses that she remembers from 5th Avenue. She also talks about her educational experiences in attending Lincoln High School, and the teachers she had there. Mrs. Leath describes her working life, including at the Atkins crate mill near Porter’s. The end of the interview entails further discussion of entertainment in Gainesville and in Black neighborhoods in other towns, and some of the Chitlin’ Circuit performers who came through Mamie Lee Leath (b. 1927) describes working at the Atkins crate mill in Gainesville, Florida, and being a farmworker on fields that were owned by the University of Florida. She talks about overall conditions for farmworkers, and the workers’ efforts to raise their wages. She explains that she left Gainesville before integration and returned after, and she describes the shock she experienced in terms of not simply the changes but the things that she feels the Black community lost in the process. Mrs. Leath also describes some of the Black businesses and business owners of Gainesville during the Jim Crow era, and shares stories and memories about them. She reflects on the impacts of World War II on the Black community in Gainesville. Segregation shaped the economic options available to African Americans in Gainesville, and Mrs. Leath especially discusses the impact on women. She also discusses some of the illicit or under-the-table economics surrounding bolita and bootlegging Mamie Lee Leath (b. 1927) describes working at the Atkins crate mill in Gainesville, Florida, and being a farmworker on fields that were owned by the University of Florida. She talks about overall conditions of farmwork, and the workers’ efforts to raise their wages. She explains that she left Gainesville before integration and returned after, and she describes the shock she experienced in terms of not simply the changes but the things that she feels the Black community lost in the process. Mrs. Leath also describes some of the Black businesses and business owners of Gainesville during the Jim Crow era, and shares stories and memories about them. She reflects on the impacts of World War II on the Black community in Gainesville. Segregation shaped the economic options available to African Americans in Gainesville, and Mrs. Leath especially discusses the impact on women. She also discusses some of the illicit or under-the-table economics surrounding bolita and bootlegging Patricia Curry discusses her experience growing up in Ocala, Florida during segregation. She tells of how she worked in tomato fields, watermelon fields, okra fields and more. Curry notes that she was encouraged to move to Gainesville to escape the racism the she experienced in Ocala. She notes how the discrimination was less severe in Gainesville but still very prevalent. She says that the election of Barack Obama was not an indication of the end of racism but rather a sugar-coating on the problem. She comments on the importance of the church community in supporting voting rights of Blacks during the Civil Rights Movement Hubert Curry was born in Phillipsville, Georgia in 1939. He talks about working on the cotton field at the age of six or seven in order to get some school clothes. Mr. Curry recounts his time attending an all-Black school during the time of segregation. He moved to Hallandale, Florida at the age of nine and washed dishes at an Italian restaurant. Mr. Curry then moved to Gainesville to work as a cook for a fraternity house, and he describes his experiences during the Civil Rights Movement Elijah Lewis was born on December of 1952 in Gainesville, Florida. He discusses his time at an integrated school in Interlachen. Mr. Lewis recounts a story of when the Black and White children of the high school disagreed for many years and how they had a difficult time agreeing on the school’s colors. He then talks about the segregation present at Shands Hospital, which is where he worked during that time. He goes into detail stating that Black patients were not wanted into surgery or in the O.R. Mr. Lewis describes his climb to supervisor of the nursing assistants in the O.R. He also reflects on a racially violent incident regarding a White student beating up Black students and how strained social relations were between Black and White students. He concludes with some thoughts on the need to work with younger generations in the Black community for the sake of the future In this interview, Virginia Hayes describes her childhood, including details about educational experiences and family. When discussing school, Mrs. Hayes focused upon her attendance at Lincoln High School in Gainesville, Florida. She then transitions to the topic of her father, a man who taught history and football in the high school. He also supervised recreation centers, resulting in the McPherson Center being named in his honor. Mrs. Hayes’s daughter joined the interview, and the women began discussing the effects of integration upon the school system as well as other encounters with the Civil Rights Movement In this interview, Ms. Levonia King delves into memories of her childhood and adulthood living in Newberry, Florida, and discusses her family’s deep roots in the area. Throughout her narrative, Ms. King speaks of the various forms of work she committed herself to from a young age, helping her family make ends meet. She also considers the sense of community she felt closely connected to in Newberry and highlights how she met her husband and began a family. Additionally, Ms. King touches upon how the Civil Rights Movement was perceived in Newberry, and she talks about her sentiments toward Barack Obama’s election as President of the United States In this talk, Paul Ortiz, Director of the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program, discusses the importance of Emancipation Betrayed which highlights African American History in Gainesville, and Florida in general. More specifically, he discusses Gainesville as an integral city in terms of African American leadership in Florida during the Jim Crow era. Further, Dr. Ortiz discusses significant events such as the creation of the Independent Party Plan, the presence of Lincoln High School, and the convening of “The State Conference of Colored Men”, which all occurred in Gainesville, but receive little to no historical recognition. He also touches on the roles of voter suppression and voter disenfranchisement during this era. Lastly, Dr. Ortiz ends with an anecdote about Dr. C. Eric Lincoln, author of The Avenue, Clayton City, and his adolescent experience in Athens, Alabama during the Jim Crow South In this interview, Ms. Gladys Thompson reflects on her life growing up in Taylor County and the Gainesville area. Her family lived in a predominantly White area because her mother worked for prominent White families in Lake Butler. Much of her family worked at Camp Blanding during World War II and her mother became a beautician under the guidance of Madam C.J. Walker. She witnessed riots at Lincoln High School during integration because White students were forced to attend. She ended up being able to take classes at the University of Florida while studying nursing because she worked as a histotechnologist in the labs at the College of Medicine. Ms. Thompson then worked in abortion clinics at North Florida Regional Hospital performing autopsies. Following that job, she worked at Alachua General Hospital working in Alzheimer’s research because she had experience with forensic anatomy Longtime resident and clerk of High Springs Eyvonne Andrews explains how different events within her Alachua County community and in the U.S. impacted her life and family Ceola Watkins was born August 25, 1925 at her home in High Springs. During that time, she was delivered by a midwife. She speaks about the death of her father when she was around four weeks old. After talking about her family members and religious upbringing, she talks about her experiences with segregation in her school life as well has her daily life. She recounts the lack of resources she had at her school and the lessons her mother taught to help her navigate the social world around her. She also talks about what it was like for her family during the Great Depression and how her mother always found a way to make sure her children were taken care of. She was present during Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.’s speech as well as had a chance to meet Mary McLeod Bethune and offers advice to future Black generations Belinda Daniels starts off by talking about her mother and father who met at Camp Blanding near Starke, Florida. Her mother worked in the commissary and her father was in the Marines at the time. Her mother went on the receive training from Santa Fe Community College to become a nurse’s assistant and her father worked in agriculture. Daniels details how she intended to joined the National Guard in 1978 but was ultimately places in the Army. She describes the struggles of serving as a woman in the military. Daniels discusses her time being stationed in Germany before the Iron Curtain fell. She discusses the various jobs she took after returning to the United States. After some time, she started working with the military again in a clinic at Camp Blanding. She talks about the relationships she had with her coworkers and the men in higher positions than her In this interview, Sharon Burney contrasts her Northern experiences with race relations with her Southern experiences, and then considers current issues within Black communities. In the North, Ms. Burney lived in nice neighborhoods and was one of the few Blacks in her grade school. When she moved to Washington D.C., she attended a predominantly Black high school. Ms. Burney shares the sentiment that her Northern education celebrated diversity, and only subtle racism was present. She then describes the more blatant racism, as well as colorism, she experienced upon moving to the South. Ms. Burney moved south to attend the University of Florida and laments the school’s historical and current lack of diversity In this interview, former PE teacher George Washington gives an overview of his childhood in a segregated northern Florida, his experiences in the military during Vietnam and his years at FAMU. He refers to his work on tobacco farms where he picked and rolled tobacco with his family before and during his time at school. He appreciates his education and credits both his parents and the teachers for allowing him to pursue a higher education at FAMU. He later lived through desegregation when his three younger sisters were able to attend an integrated high school and went to FSU. He notes that the shared community aspects he felt both in his hometown and at FAMU made him feel included and protected. After being drafted into the Vietnam War, he talks about the brotherhood that he felt amongst the soldiers. His religious values and patience were key aspects that he felt helped him live through segregation and that the Black community was constantly helping each other achieve the most with the best education available. He shares a lot of pride that he has for FAMU with the interviewer and really focuses on small communities and how taking the time to get to know someone personally defines his teaching style as well. The legacy he left and the impact he made on the childhoods of others is what brought him joy. It’s something he brought from his own personal experience growing up into the lives of those around him Byran Williams reflects on his experience attending Douglass Elementary and Middle School in High Springs, Florida. He remembers the jobs his parents and grandparents worked in the town. The interviewee explains his path to becoming a minster and eventually being the preacher for the church he grew up attending in High Springs. After seeing the discrimination coming from the town commission, Williams was inspired to get involved as an advocate for African Americans in the community, and eventually became mayor. He expresses the hope that Barack Obama’s election gave him although he acknowledges the anger and backlash that has come from an African American man being serving as president. He discusses his desire for the people of High Springs to vote and to oppose the still-present racism in High Springs Mr. Lewis Flagler is interviewed by his granddaughter about his life growing up in Pompano Beach, Florida. He ended up being recruited into the army but never went to boot camp. He was followed by MP’s from state to state because he had gone AWOL, but he never ended up serving in the military. He briefly discusses race relations between Whites and Blacks in Pompano Beach and West Palm Beach. He finishes the interview discussing President Obama’s election and how many people in his generation are unaware that racism is still prevalent today In this interview, Mr. Johnny Fair discusses his life growing up in Statesboro, Georgia during the presidency of Herbert Hoover with his daughter, Mrs. Jenkins, also present for the interview. His father was a sharecropper who grew corn, peanuts, cotton, and chufas. He moved to Gainesville when he was eighteen in 1943 and worked in a crate mill. Mr. Fair travelled up the east coast for work to gather crops during harvesting season, helped build and remodel many of the large buildings in downtown Gainesville, worked on constructing the railroad, and worked for the city picking up garbage In this interview, Ms. Claudia Rawls expands upon her experience living in an all-black community in Gainesville which instilled a sense of Black pride within her from a young age. She also delves into the impact that integration had on this sense of community, in regards to both the educational system and other community-based matters. Additionally, Ms. Rawls contrasts the intergenerational differences that exist within black communities now and in her past. Further, she describes her sentiments concerning the election of Barack Obama as President of the United States and expresses her belief in the importance of voting In this interview, Mrs. Jan Lawson recounts her childhood in the Porters area of Gainesville and her experiences working for the University of Florida. She attended Gainesville High School as one of the only Black students but also remembers the closing of Lincoln High School and the resulting school riots. She was ostracized by her peers for having White friends and attending Gainesville High School. Once Mrs In this interview Booker Dwayitan discusses his life growing in Gainesville. He discusses the Civil Rights movement and his personal encounters with racism in the city In this interview, Pauline Lawrence discusses growing up in Jamaica and attending the University of West Indies before going to graduate school at the University of Florida in the late 1960s, where she earned a PhD in entomology. She was the only woman in the program at the time she entered. She talks about the cultural differences she was not prepared for between Jamaica and Gainesville, including her encounters with racism. She also discusses her research at the University of Florida. She was hired as a professor at the University of Florida after graduation, and she talks about how the University has changed in her forty years on campus In this interview, Martha Franklin discusses her experiences growing up and attending Lincoln High School during the years of Jim Crow segregation In this interview, Claretha Bradley talks about growing up in Lake City and going to college in Jacksonville, where she became a nurse. During her career, she worked with AIDS patients and, later, the elderly in hospice In this interview with Ms. Frances Wilson, Ms. Wilson describes her experience working as a teacher in Alachua County and compares it to her time as a student in the county. Additionally, she underscores her involvement in her church community, including the many hours she spends volunteering in her community through her church. Furthermore, she touches upon the individuals she knew who were employed by the Atlantic Coast Line. Ms. Wilson also speaks of her experiences at her high school reunions, and of the strong connections she maintains with her high school friends Gwendolyn Williams was born in 1948 in Fort White, Florida, part of Columbia County, into a family of seventeen children. Williams talks about her home life, where she didn’t feel like life was a struggle even though her family was on welfare. Her father was a sharecropper and her mother was a seamstress, and she and her siblings worked summers in the fields picking cotton and tobacco. She was talks about how she was taught the value of hard work, treating people how you would want to be treated, and Church life. Williams also discusses her education, and her pride in Black teachers that were determined to teach even though they were given textbooks with missing pages. She also talks about her family, and how through a culture of hard work, she and her children became successful. However, she still faced discrimination in her work for the VA hospital. Finally, she ends on a discussion about “hidden racism”, and argues that even though Barack Obama became president, discrimination still legitimately affects Black people, and that the only way to combat it is to have a strong belief in God and do the right thing In this interview, Mrs. Bettie Blakely describes various locations and corresponding significant events around Inverness, Florida and Dunnellon, Florida as well as her involvement with the Tallahassee CORE (Congress of Racial Equality) chapter and her actions in founding a CORE chapter in Dunnellon. They passed a church called Second Bethel Baptist Church where Mrs. Blakely made a plea for people to become involved in the Civil Rights Movement. She was also involved with the NAACP chapter in Ocala at Covenant Baptist Church. They also passed the previous location of Booker T. Washington High School Inverness, which was the only Black school in Citrus County. Mrs. Blakely mentions Patricia Stephens Due and her gratitude for a book that she wrote about her community and her involvement in CORE. She attended FAMU after Due’s arrest, and was involved in boycotts with Due’s CORE chapter In this interview, Mrs. Bettie Blakely describes her involvement with CORE in Tallahassee and Dunnellon as well as her relationships with Zev Aelony, Patricia Stephens Due, Reverend Townsend, and Reverend Frank Pinkston. She became involved with civil rights demonstrations at FAMU with Patricia Stephens Due and her CORE chapter. She was one of the students teargassed during a march toward downtown Tallahassee with Patricia and Priscilla Stephens. Following the school year of 1963, Mrs. Blakely started her own CORE chapter in Dunnellon, Florida with Zev Aelony. They would hold meetings at either First Bethel Missionary Baptist Church or Union Baptist Church and picket at local White-owned restaurants and stores. Mrs In this interview, Mrs. Mary Hall Daniels recounts growing up in the Porters area of Gainesville, Florida. Her family fled Rosewood during the Rosewood Massacre and lived in the woods for a week. They initially settled in a large house in Belleview with the help of her stepfather Henry Price, but were forced to relocate to Porters Quarters, a predominantly Black neighborhood in Gainesville. Her family attended Shady Grove Primitive Baptist Church and Lincoln High School. She shares memories of her principal, A. Quinn Jones. Once Mrs. Daniels finished high school, she wanted to become a nurse, but the only school was in Jacksonville and she could not attend. She ended up completing a Nursing Assistant course at Florida Community College at Jacksonville after moving there with her sister when she was fifty years old. She worked at St. Luke’s Hospital, then began working as a baker at Bolles Boarding School In this interview, Lurie and Brian Favors describe their involvement in African American studies and affairs at Penn State University. They both were at Penn State as students when they decided they wanted to address the problems that plagued the Black and Latino communities on campus. Through their creation of the Black Caucus with LaKeisha Wolf, they formed a support group and unified community for African American Students. They attended the Faculty Senate meeting, which was ended prematurely with no decision. Following the meeting, they had the president and Faculty Senate of Penn State sign a document admitting that the administration failed to meet the diversity initiatives they promised. The school held a “No Hate at Penn State” rally to essentially cover-up the Black Caucus’s efforts to secure funding for a Black research institute, so they gathered thousands of people to march at the rally and demand the school administration’s support In this interview, Mrs. LaKay Banks recounts her life growing up in Tallahassee and her experiences being married to the first and only Black doctor in Gainesville. She grew up in a middle class neighborhood with her father as an auto- mechanic teacher at FAMU and her mother as a teacher in multiple high schools In this interview, Isaiah Branton talks about his genealogy and family history and the importance of knowing one’s past, especially for African Americans. He shares his family’s history going back through slavery, which he learned from his grandmother. He not only shares names of his ancestors, but also the hardships they went through in the mid- and late-1800s, including traveling to and living in the Gainesville area In this interview, Isaiah Branton talks about his genealogy and the importance of knowing one’s past, especially in African American culture, as this is his focus. He shares his family’s history, which he learned through his grandmother that goes back to slavery. He not only shares names of his ancestors, but also the hardship they went through in the mid and late 1800s. Additionally, Mr. Branton shares he life story, education, jobs and most importantly; how his upbringing was marked by racism, his life experiences about it and how it marked his life. Furthermore, he endeavors to identify the growing problem within the African American Community which he lists as the loss of the African American culture and how it seems to him that many African Americans now a days are ignorant to the slavery as a result of their actions In this interview, Ms. Marna Weston describes a few vivid memories that constituted her life in Florida and Philadelphia. She details the 1928 storm that crossed into West Palm Beach, where her family was living at the time, and how they survived its initial physical impact in the area and prolonged, subsequent impact on their lives. In addition, she elaborates upon her experience in her high school’s choir group and basketball team. Ms. Weston also discusses her thoughts about how Lawley, Florida has evolved over her lifetime and highlights a revolutionary act she and her cousin partook in during the Civil Rights Movement. Finally, she delves into her sentiments on the presidential election of Barack Obama and reflects on stories passed down from her family about the Ku Klux Klan In this interview, Dr. Whitney Battle-Baptiste discusses her childhood in the Bronx and her journey within the field of archaeology as a Black woman. She is fluent in Mandarin because she attended kindergarten in Taipei and her mother studied Asian Studies and taught at the University of Taipei. Dr. Battle-Baptiste earned a bachelor’s in high school education at Virginia State University and began her master’s in history at the College of William and Mary with the intention of becoming a history teacher. She ended up leaving William and Mary to do a full-time internship at the Department of Archaeological Research at Colonial Williamsburg. She completed her PhD in archaeology at the University of Texas at Austin. In terms of her professional career, Dr. Battle-Baptiste has worked at numerous archaeological sites around the United States and teaches at the University of Massachusetts Amherst In this interview, Mrs. Cassie Rosemary Williams and Mrs. Mildred Barnett describe race relations in Polk County as well as their life experiences. Mrs. Barnett was born in Lake County then moved to New York as a teenager, and returned to Florida to live in Polk County as an adult. Mrs. Williams was born in Alabama then moved to New Jersey when her parents passed away. She moved to Panama City with her brother and got married then settled in Lake Wales. In Lake Wales, she worked in the migrant reading program with the school system. Mrs. Barnett worked as a doctor’s receptionist for fifteen years in New York, and could not find the same work when she moved to Polk County with her husband. Instead, she worked in the Donald Duck canning plant until her and her husband were able to purchase a house and then she worked as a caretaker for an elderly woman until she passed away. They both remember the church serving the Black community by uniting and taking care of people Born in August 28, 1932, Lexington Blair recalls his time working on the fields in the 1950s as well as the race relations in his community. Living during segregation, Mr. Blair reminisces on several instances of racism in his life such as being unable to sit in restaurants or see the cook who is preparing the food. He also states the racial slurs that he was called while living there as well as the violence exhibited when integration was introduced in 1964. MR Blair then goes into detail regarding the differences between races in both Jamaica and the United States. Rather than racism, in Jamaica it was more of financial status and therefore presented a shock to Mr. Blair. He then expresses his joy in witnessing the first Black President come into office and hopes the future generations take advantage of as many opportunities as they can in life Ella Mae Driskell was born in Polk County, Florida. Mrs. Driskell recalls her family’s involvement with the development of Bartow, Florida. She mentions her great- grandfather starting the first Black Church called First Providence and describes its importance to her family due to them being raised in the church. There were tensions between the east and west sides of Polk County as described by Mrs. Driskell and they were not racially related. She then mentions the role the first Black church had on creating the first school in Bartow, Florida for Black children. Ms. Driskell then goes on to emphasize the importance of good parenting on future generations as well as her reaction to seeing the first Black President come into office Linda Kimbrough, born in Polk County, Florida recalls her days as a community activist. She recounts her most memorable experience when she ran for city commissioner of Lake Wales and won the election with twice the votes as her competitor. She talks about becoming the first Black female mayor of Lake Wales and her educational journey attending the Florida Technological University. Mrs. Kimbrough describes the impact her college education had regarding her future careers. She then advises her generation to get more involved and for the younger generations to read more in order to obtain the power of knowledge Dr. David Padgett got his PhD at the University of Florida and was a major player in the civil rights movement for Black students on campus. He discusses his experiences as a member of the Black Student Union: zero-funding, assault by racist groups (i.e. Skinheads), the relationship with other minority groups on campus, confrontation with UF police, and the creation of a White Student Union. Dr. Padgett also discusses Winnie Mandela speaking in Gainesville due to the efforts of the Black Student Union, his relationship with UF Black faculty, the development of his political consciousness, working at all-Black colleges, and involvement in local elections In this interview, Carolyn Cohens discusses her works as a painter, a talent first inspired by a painter at Mt. Pleasant Baptist Church. Church was the center of her community, and she describes her experience with its services, prayers, songs, and religious paintings. The paintings provoke the topic of race because she considers the varying ways people depict Jesus. She explains the religious scenes she’s painted in detail, and continues by explaining her family’s influence upon her religious beliefs. Ms. Cohens momentarily shifts from painting to storytelling and then education. She explains that as a 1966 high school graduate, she did not witness the effects of schools’ integration until her child enrolled in school. She elaborates on the importance of education to future generations of Black students, and then returns to discussing the personal significance of her paintings. The last painting discussed is of a couple from Rosewood, prompting Ms. Cohens explain her role as a community historian In this interview, Ms. Gladys Perkins discusses her experience growing up in a loving, Christian household with a childhood illness, along with her mother’s strong role in the dynamic family unit. She highlights a few childhood memories, especially those pertaining to her family’s involvement in, and discussions surrounding, the Civil Rights Movement. Through this, she tells of a time when her own mother led a strike Ms. Gladys Perkins is being interviewed by Keilani Jacquot in Gainesville, Florida on May 21, 2012. The interview begins with a discussion of Ms. Perkins’s experiences of being a union steward and her observations about how unions have changed today. Ms. Perkins then describes the racism and sexism she experienced while working. Ms. Perkins discusses her involvement in voter registration drives and restoration of civil rights of the incarcerated. There is conversation about discrimination in distribution of public funds by Gainesville’s city government In this interview, Mr. James Bennitt Miller describes a few noteworthy moments in his life and sheds light upon how segregation was intertwined in them; this includes his recollection of the all-black community he grew up in and of his time in the Army during the Vietnam War. Additionally, Mr. Miller details the years he spent in college before and after the war, and the subsequent time he spent working three different jobs while at Buckey. Further, he discusses his multiple marriages and divorces, along with his children’s lives, before touching upon his favorite teachers and humorous times in high school. Finally, he speaks of the ways in which integration affected the students of Perry and his sentiments toward Barack Obama’s second presidential campaign In this interview, Ms. Hazel Armbrister’s covers her family’s migration to Perry, Florida and discusses a few of her family members’ particular life stories. She delves into her educational background, including the jobs she held after leaving Perry and outlines her eventual career as an educator in Broward County, being the first person to be issued a GED in Broward County. In speaking of this, Ms. Armbrister details the ways in which her master’s degree, from the University of South Florida, helped her as an educator. Additionally, she reflects on her involvement in the community, including with her church, St. Nicholas Episcopal Church in Pompano Beach. Finally, she speaks of her affiliation with the Democratic Party and its importance to her, and expresses her sentiments on the reelection campaign of President Obama In this interview, Mr. Jason Yulee, a U.S. Army veteran, discusses his experience enlisting in the military, being deployed to Iraq, and his life after service. More specifically, he outlines his motivation for joining the military, describes his family’s history of military involvement, and speaks of his sentiments concerning basic training and deployment. Mr. Yulee also highlights his educational experience following his service, along with the time he spent working at the Veterans Affairs office at Santa Fe College and the University of Florida. Additionally, he discusses how he believes military service is presently perceived in society and the impact it has had on his life, before examining the role his own race played into how he was treated in the army Vendarae Lewis, born in 1956, was from Bartow, Florida, and attended Union Academy, a Black school, the year before it closed down. She also talks about her family life and how her mother’s beauty shop is still open in Bartow today under daughter. She went to Bartow Junior and Senior High, and attended Tennessee State for college. At Bartow High School, she found that the rules were much more lax than what she had experienced at Union Academy, and after integration, the Black students that were at the top in Union Academy found that they had to prove themselves even more to White students – which they did. In college, she joined Delta Sigma Theta, one of the largest Black sororities in the Nation, where she enjoyed their belief in public service and politics. Finally, she ends the interview with a discussion of what Bartow needs as a community – unity across racial, class, and economic lines In this interview, teacher and track coach Mr. Jordan Corbett devotes much time to discussing his educational experience as a child, speaking of the athletic games he attended with his involvement in multiple sports. Furthermore, he details memories of his time in the military during World War II and highlights the ways in which others treated him and African American soldiers because of the color of their skin Rev. Jefferson Rogers is being interviewed by Paul Ortiz on 14 August 2012. Rev. Rogers grew up in Quincy, Florida and his grandmother was the first black school teacher in that town. He talks about how he frequently thinks about how close he was to being born into slavery. Rev. Rogers describes seeing chain gangs working in the middle of the city. He then describes his family history and how the first black doctor in Quincy, Dr. Stevens, helped in the cesarean birthing process of Rogers himself. Rev Henry and Mattie Leath were both born in the South—Henry in Gainesville and Mattie in Georgia—in the early 1930s. Mattie came to Gainesville when she was ten years old. Henry and Mattie lived in the Porters community, and they ended up meeting at Lincoln High school. Henry dropped out of school in 11th grade to work, and joined the military for a year, while Mattie went to Fort Valley State College in Georgia after high school to study English. Henry and Mattie then talk about life in Porters, the dearth of Black businesses and shrinkage of the community, and the train station. At the end, Henry and Mattie express pride in and love for Porters Quarters Rufus “Nine” Brooks is interviewed in his home in Gainesville, Florida. He begins the interview discussing his childhood and fathers’ career as a carpenter; his family lived on a quarter and did work in the Atkins Crate Mill in Otter Creek, and then later Gainesville. He then talks about different jobs he held throughout his life, such as working as a bell hop and factory labor, and mentions what his siblings did and how it was affected by their gender and skin color. Mr. Brooks discusses life without shoes, electricity, plumbing, and medicine. He talks about how he avoided going into military service and what daily life was like during times of racial segregation. Mr. Brooks starts to end the interview with a short descriptions of what he remembers when President Kennedy and Martin Luther King Jr. were both assassinated and finishes with a message to all: the truth will set you free Mrs. Barbara Smith is being interviewed after her talk at the 2012 Mebane Holiday Homecoming. She begins her interview with a short summary of her life accomplishments. She discusses her days at Mebane High school, teachers she remembers, her career with the Alachua County School Food Service, and awards she’s been honored. She sings her Alma matter and then describes her experiences at Mebane. She remembers A. Quinn Jones, Oliver Jones, and her brief time at Douglass High school in High Springs. Mrs. Smith also went to Lincoln High School for her seventh grade year, and she describes teachers and peers she remembers. She sings “Swing Low, Sweet Chariot” when reminiscing about the operetta she performed in at Douglass. The interview then turns to discussing her genealogy and then talking about her siblings and children. Ms. Smith talks about Black school rivalries in Florida, and then finishes the interview discussing Barack Obama and current controversies, such as redevelopment, that High Springs is facing Ms. Zelphia Chambers, along with Harland Mitchell and Mary Cummings, is being interviewed by Sherry Dupree and Justin Dunnavant in Starke, Florida on April 20, 2013. The interview begins with a discussion of Ms. Chambers’s family members Bill White grew up in Boise, Idaho in the 1980s – 1990s. He tells about his quest to become an African American archeologist at Boise State University. After struggling to find a job in the field with just a Bachelor’s degree, he continued his studies in the subfield of cultural resource management in the Archeologist at the University of Idaho. He goes on to tell about his experiences as a consultant and owner for a private cultural resource firm. He also wrote a number of books and training guides that focus on preparing students, especially minorities, for the challenges of the archeology field Jordon Corbett was raised in a segregated mining town called Pierce, Florida. He tells stories about his small community: the teachers, churches and events. After attending Bethune-Cookman University for one semester, he was drafted and travelled around the country during World War II. Corbett was able to attend North Carolina A&T after the draft and majored in mathematics. He went on to become a teacher in Bartow, Florida for thirty years Retha Mae Cooks was born in White Springs Florida on November 21, 1935. She was born at her grandmother’s house by a midwife to her father, who worked in a saw mill, and mother who stayed home to care for her and her seven siblings. The jones family moved to Lake City where their father’s family resided and Retha and her siblings learned to hunt and fish very young, which is how their father and mother provided food for their family. She assisted her mother in caring for her younger siblings and taking care of the home. After she got of age she moved back to White Springs and ended up meeting her husband. In eleven years, her and her husband saved enough money to build their own home. Her and her husband, along with a few other people founded New Jerusalem Baptist Church Mr. Bernard Williams is being interviewed by Derick Gomez in White Springs, Florida on 25 April 2015. The interview begins with tracing Mr. Williams’ family migration to White Springs. Mr. Williams then describes the race relations in White Springs as he was growing up. Mr. Williams was the first Black male to graduate from high school in White Springs; he notes that education for Blacks in White Springs only went through eighth grade until 1949. There is conversation about his ongoing mission to convert the disused Carver School into a community center for the town. Mr. Williams discusses segregation, various May Day celebrations in North Florida, the German prison camp in White Springs, his time in the military, and his career in education In this interview, William James talks about his daily routine, which consists of his belief in God and the Church. Mr. James emphasizes the importance of his belief and how it has played an integral part in his life; it has been present in his life since he was a young boy and continues to be. Mr. James briefly mentions Frank Pinkston, one of the past presidents of the NAACP, and Pinkston’s Humanitarian Award. He recalls that Mr. Pinkston was a religious man who became a minister before his untimely death. He also talks about his own contributions, such as buying six houses in Ocala to help those in need In this interview Larry sanders talks about his life growing up in Gainesville, Florida. He was born in St. Augustine and has lived in Gainesville all his life. He has been a member of the Mt. Pleasant United Methodist Church for sixty- eight years. He talks about his memories of going to church as a child to an adult. He also discusses how his church was instrumental in the civil right movement and a staple for his community. The interview also follows up with how the neighborhood has changed Mr. Charles Chestnut is being interviewed by Justin Dunnavant, Justin Hosbey, and Ryan Morini in Gainesville, Florida on August 11, 2015. The interview begins with a discussion of Mr. Chestnut’s mortuary education in the North and his subsequent return to the South, to Gainesville where he grew up. Mr. Chestnut then describes the race relations in Gainesville and his involvement in “testing” lunch counters in town. Mr Then PhD student Randi Gil-Sadler interviewed poet Nikki Giovanni on January 19, 2016. She began with word associations for Nikki Giovanni to expand upon. These words included Tennessee, poetry, Momma, Gus, Black, and Outer Space. Nikki Giovanni discusses her passion for Black youth to have opportunities to go to space as well as her friendships with other prominent Black female writers. She also reflects on her relationships with other Civil Rights giants like Rosa Parks. During the interview, she expresses her thoughts on the Donald Trump presidency and shares about her past work in poetry as well as the current project she was working on Mr. Anthony Major in this interview reflects on his family history, childhood upbringing, and the twists and turns of his acting and directing career. He shares his family migration from Quincy, Florida to Newtown, Florida outside of Sarasota due to Klan violence. He reflects on his time in grade school during segregation and its impact on the community. He shares his love of baseball and the ways he was able to pursue the sport. He discusses how he went to college in New York and began his acting career. Additionally, he reflects on his many successes and failures within the film industry and his entry into teaching at the university level Ms. Alberta Brown reflects on her life experiences in this interview. She shares how due to her father’s passing, her mother could not keep up with the sharecropping labor on her own. They moved to Palmetto and eventually to Sarasota, Florida where some family lived. She describes learning to love the work she had to do which included farming and cooking. She discusses working as a cook for two families, one where she lived in house and another where she commuted. She also shares her time having polio and the little to no health care access she had until she had her children Ms. Mason begins by explaining how and why her family came to move to Sarasota and the early development of Newtown. She then speaks about her experience in schooling and her experiences with segregation as a child. Ms. Mason describes how events such as the desegregation of schools changed the community and impacted her personally. She then recounts her journey into activism and public office before concluding the interview with her thoughts on how to improve the community Dr. Rachel Shelley reflects on her upbringing in Newtown, Florida and the full circle she made back to Newtown to serve her community as a teacher and eventually principal. Dr. Shelley grew up in project housing raised by her grandmother. She remembers her childhood fondly despite her poverty and lack of access to resources Mr. Thomas Clyburn reflects on his life experiences growing up in Newtown, Florida, a black community in Sarasota. Mr. Clyburn reflects how despite segregation, his community was thriving and he enjoyed his childhood. He discusses in detail the process of being selected to integrate Sarasota High School and leave Booker High School. He was the first Black male student to do so. He expresses his confusion and verbal abuse he received at the school. He also spoke of