iscusses growing up in Sarasota during segregation. She also talks about her father’s activism in local politics. Her father was also active in the NAACP and the Mason’s during the Civil Rights Movement Jesse Johnson talks about being fired for speaking out against the dishonest labor practices he dealt with while pouring cement. He reflects on his gratitude for being able to provide for himself and his pride for seeing his fellow community members be able to move up economically. Mr. Johnson also speaks about how people helped each other take steps up the ladder and people using their gifts in order to survive and thrive. This interview seems an excerpt of another interview. It starts and ends abruptly Mr. Jetson Grimes reflects on his life experiences in Newtown, Florida. He discusses his role models including his aunt who started a cleaning business. She had a great impact on him to become a businessman himself. He shares how he started to be a barber and open his own barbershop. He also reflects on the importance of barbershops in Black communities. Mr. Grimes also reflects on the process of desegregation in his community and personal experiences of desegregating a bus. He shares his involvement in integrating theaters and beaches as well as the attitude of the Black community during the time of desegregation in the Sarasota area Johnny Hunter talks about growing up in Sarasota. He joined the Air Force. When he got out, he formed a motorcycle club called “The Wheels of Sarasota.” He served eight and a half years at the Florida State Prison for selling drugs, which he maintains was a false charge. In prison, he started writing a newspaper column about prison, which led him to the newspaper business after he was released In this interview, Julian Ross Moreland and Margaret Beverly Moreland discuss growing up in Sarasota, and businesses open at the time. They briefly touch on their grandparent’s efforts to feed the community during the Depression. Mr. Moreland shed