ences in teaching Black and White students, followed by family life and church life. Barber also offers insight on the experience of being part of the group that integrated the beaches in Newtown and traveling to Tampa by ferry for classroom materials Mr. Robert Taylor reflects on his time growing up in Zellwood, Florida and Overtown, Florida. He was raised by his grandmother in the country in Zellwood where they grew all their food and were very independent. With his grandfather’s passing, him and his sisters moved to Overtown, Florida in Sarasota to live with his mom. He reflects on life in Overtown and the transition from living in the country to living in the city. He received an academic scholarship to attend Morehouse College, but was drafted into the army in 1943. He discusses the places he traveled with the military and going back to Morehouse on the GI bill. He came back to Sarasota and reflects on getting a pool for his community and providing swimming lessons and recreation for the Black Youth Mr. Trevor D. Harvey reflects on his upbringing in Newtown, Florida. A fire in his home when he was a small child had a great impact on him. He describes the businesses he and his grandparents used to frequent for their groceries and other needs in Newtown. He also shares in great detail his education in grade school and the teachers who mentored him. He graduated early and was offered a job as account manager at a Rent to Own company, so instead of attending Florida A&M University on a ROTC scholarship, he remained home and worked. He later attended Eckerd College. He reflects on his time serving as the president of the local chapter of the NAACP and the organization’s role in his community. He concludes the interview sharing his vision and hope for Newtown Mr. Wade Harvin reflects on his life experiences growing up in Delson’s Quarters in Overtown, Florida. After moving here in 1945 with his mom, he recalls with fondness the community in Delson’s Quarters. He discusses the three stores that operated in Overtown and how they sold things in halves knowing that the community was stricken with poverty. He shares stories of going to Pennsylvania very summer since the age of nine to work the fields and make money. He contrasted the integrated community in Pennsylvania with the segregated one in Florida. He shares his experiences with racial violence and the ways he participated in Civil Rights. Mr. Harvin also shares the ways the Black community socialized and created entertainment. He also shares his work career in sales and banking Mr. Walter Gilbert reflects on moving away to Pittsburg and coming back to Newtown, Florida when he was eight. He contrasts integrated society in Pittsburg and urban landscape to Newtown’s segregated schools and dirt roads. He speaks a lot about his activism like encouraging the Black football players to refuse to play football unless Black cheerleaders were allowed. He discusses his involvement with the NAACP and running for local office in order to bring changes and more representation of Blacks in the government. He helped change the voting to a single member district voting that allowed more Black folk in positions of power. He moved away to go to airline school and worked for Delta in Chicago, but moved back to Newtown. He immediately got back involved with community activism and he shares the importance of fighting and agitating until progress is made. Mr. Gilbert also speaks about his mentors who really shaped his passion and drive to help spark change in his community Mr. Wendell Patrick Carter speaks about his life growing up in Newtown, Florida being raised by his grandparents. He shares his experiences in school starting in Booker Elementary and graduating from Riverview High School. He outlines his path to becoming a professional football player in the NFL. He reflects on his time attending Florida State University to play football and being a player for the Los Angeles Rams. He played pro