da’s Institute of Black Culture, expresses his deepest thanks toward all of the individuals that have come together to construct the building for the Institute of Black Culture. Additionally, Mr. Roberts stresses the vitality of the Institute of Black Culture on the history of the University of Florida’s campus, and he specifically outlines the impacts it will have on Black students seeking a familiar, secure environment Ms. Sophia Threat shares her family history and life experiences growing up in Groveland, Florida. She shares her memories attending integrated schools. Her family worked in the citrus groves in the surrounding areas and she would work after school to help her family. She discusses how she was the niece of Samuel Shepherd and Walter Irvin who were part of the Groveland Four case. She recalls how her mother told her that they were sentenced and would not speak of it again. She reflects how it was something no one would talk about but everyone would know about. She discusses her over two-decade career as a corrections officer in Groveland. She reflects on the violence she witnessed against Black inmates In this interview, John Nelson recalls his time as the president of the NAACP from 1986 to1992. Mr. Nelson speaks about voting rights, the Ted Turner case, as well as the fact that as Black organization, he and members had to push to achieve anything In this interview John Nelson talks about his experiences living in Monticello, Florida after living in Orlando Florida. He became heavily involved with the Veterans of Foreign Wars and the American Legion. At that time there were two separate VFWs and American Legions based on race. Nelson worked to merge the two. He was also a fire chief and a Chief Deputy for the office of the fire marshal In this interview, Warren Henderson talks about being a farmer in North Central Florida. He grew up in Hawthorne, Florida. He comes from a long line of farmers. He also went to Lincoln High School. He went to FAMU and