ng encouraged to go to college. It was around this time he discovered the Baha’i faith and shares his experiences with it. He eventually went to federal jail and discovered the Nation of Islam and the Moorish Science Temple. He also talks about his family life and raising children within Gainesville In this interview Elmer Henry talks about growing up in the Depression era with his family in Alachua County. He also recall growing up in Gainesville, Florida after his family moved from Jacksonville, Florida. He also got drafted into the United States Navy and shipped to San Diego California for training. It was in the Navy where he became a cook. After training he set sail on the USS Mississippi for Pearl Harbor. He talks about his experiences in time of war and what it was like to be in the military as a Black man In this interview Barbara Norris talks about growing up in Chase City, Virginia with her family and her experiences of growing up in the south as a Black woman. Upon graduating as valedictorian at West End High School, she received a scholarship to Virginia Union University. It was at this university that she met her husband, Frank, and started their family. In 1962 the family moved to Ocala, Florida where her husband became an outspoken advocate for the Civil Rights Movement. She also talks about her husband’s position within the movement to further the cause. While her husband was busy with the movement, Norris recounts how it affected everyday life In this interview Hunt Davis Jr recalls the conference where Chinua Achebe and James Baldwin visited UF in 1980. He recounts his personal interactions with Achebe and discusses Achebe’s practicality as a person. Mr. Davis then talks about the effects the 1980 conference had after on teaching African-American literature In this interview, Bernadette Cailler talks about co-chairing the Achebe and Baldwin meeting at the University of Florida. She talks in detail about planning the whole event from start to finish