an population on campus. Horne was also involved in the Black theater which put on political plays for neighboring middle and high schools. Horne also advocated and pushed for a Black Studies department at the university. He also was one of the organizers for Black Thursday, a protest that was led by students demanding an inclusive place on campus. Horne also talks about his time off campus and spending it on Fifth Avenue Josephine Bryant attended the University of Florida from the summer of 1974 to December of 1977 and graduated with a degree in Business Marketing with a minor in finance. She also joined the Delta Sigma Theta Sorority. In this interview Bryant talks about her experiences of going to the University of Florida and how it has changed since then given the political climate in this time Leonard Spearman Jr attended the University of Florida from 1971 to 1975 where he finished with a Liberal Arts degree in Psychology. Mr. Spearman talks about his decision process to attend UF along with the warnings given to him by the Black Student Union Association located in Gainesville. He also goes into detail about his involvement with the founding of the Kappa Alpha Psi Fraternity Sharon Glover, in this interview talks about her experience in deciding what university to attend. Ms. Glover also talks about the molding of her identity at UF through her involvement with being involved in Greek life, her peers from Jacksonville and living in Broward Hall. She also talks about her involvement with the creation of the Minority Business Society In this interview, Vincent Green talks about his time spent attending the University of Florida. He speaks about the military’s educational plan for him and how he pursued his own degree in business instead of engineering. He goes into detail about the community work he was a part of as well as the lack of discomfort regarding racial tensions on campus. He also talks about meeting his wife of thirty-three years at the university In this interview, Hazel Levy goes into detail regarding her family and early childhood years which include early education as well as the racial dynamic in Milwaukee, Wisconsin compared to her earlier years in Michigan. Ms. Levy speaks about her impressions of the University of Florida when she first attended as well as the issues trying to become an undergraduate researcher for the College of Science. She also mentions how certain negative experiences had made her disconnected from the university and and then goes into detail about her graduate school experiences and beyond This interview was conducted as part of a collaboration between Dr. Hazel Levy and SPOHP, which focused on Black women research faculty at the UF College of Medicine. Dr. Antonette Bennett was born in Kingston Jamaica, and in this interview describes some of her experiences growing up there. In particular, she discusses her early education and how she became introduced to the sciences and went on to work as a medical researcher. That path includes her studies at the University of the West Indies, and her early experiences in laboratory work. She also discusses playing on the national Jamaican volleyball team around the same time. Following that, she attended Florida Atlantic University, and then matriculated into the UF PhD program in the College of Medicine. Dr. Bennett talks about her experiences in the PhD program, including the challenges facing Black women in grad school, and describes the research that she was currently engaged in at the time of the interview In this interview Dr. Mckenna talks about her family life growing up in Nigeria and moving to the United Kingdom. She went on to high school and college and began a career in organic chemistry. It was in the United Kingdom that she met her husband and moved to the United States where she and her husband took a research position at Purdue University. From Purdue University they relocated back to the United Kingdom where she and her husband took a faculty position at the University of Warrick. She states it is at the University of Warrick that the University of Florida pursued her to come and develop a structured biology program based on her expertise and credentials. Dr In this interview Mr. Roberts talks about his time as the Director of the Institute of Black Culture (IBC). He goes into detail regarding his experiences that helped prepare him to become the Director of the IBC. Mr. Roberts also delves into his visions for the IBC and its future during his time as President along with some of his first memories of the IBC itself. He talks about how the space of the IBC was utilized amongst students and the challenges presented running and maintaining the institute In this interview, Mr. Eddie Barrington focuses on his experiences in the Civil Rights Movement in Tallahassee, Florida, working with the Inter-Civic Council led by Reverend C.K. Steele, and helping to organize the Tallahassee Bus Boycott of 1956 In this interview Walter Anderson discusses his life growing up in Alachua, Florida. When he graduated high school, he went to go play basketball for a Hampton Junior College in Ocala Florida. After Hampton Junior College dissolved and became Central Florida College, Anderson went to play basketball for North Carolina A&T State. It was at this time that he was drafted into the United States Army for the Vietnam War. He started out as a Guidon Bearer and eventually went to paratrooper school. After leaving the military, Anderson went back to playing Basketball in college. Due to his PTSD from the war Anderson left college and went back to Alachua, Florida. He also describes his experiences as a veteran in the United States and living with PTSD. He eventually became a counselor within Alachua, County Dr. Duchess Harris, grew up in New England. This interview was conducted while she was visiting UF for a speaking engagement through the African American Studies Program. She describes some of her experiences growing up in Connecticut, as well as some of her wider family history. She also describes her experiences in graduate school in getting both a JD and PhD. Much of the interview focuses on her research and teaching as an American Studies and Legal Studies scholar, including her book Black Lives Matter, her publications in academic journals such as La Raza Law Journal, and some of her favorite materials to use in the classroom—ranging from classic authors like Lorraine Hansberry and James Baldwin to more recent works by authors like Ta-Nahesi Coates and Pauline Gumbs Dr. Sharon Austin is the director of the African American studies program at the University of Florida. In this interview, Dr. Austin talks about the progress that the University has made with regard to racism and justice, but also how there is still work to do, especially given the little amount of Black faculty at the University, and the fact that she is the first Black woman to receive a Political Science degree In this interview Sharon Austin first talks about her early childhood years with her parents Annie Ruth Wright and Willie James Wright and her two siblings Janice and Marvin. Ms. Austin recalls when Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated and what she was doing at the time of the incident. She then speaks about her education experiences in Westwood High School and Christian Brothers College. Ms. Austin remembers her first true encounters with racism while attending Christian Brothers College and how it affected her. Afterwards, she goes into detail about getting her Ph.D. and becoming the African American Studies Program Director at the University of Florida Mr. Marion Coleman was born in Grove Park, Florida, and moved to Gainesville when he was four years old. In this interview, he describes his experiences growing up and living his life in Gainesville, including becoming one of Gainesville’s first Black firefighters In this interview Dr. Warford talks about his experiences as a doctoral student at the University of Florida. He was a member of the Loyal Order of the 99, and organizations that were meant to protect Black students against acts of racisms and to solidify their identity on campus. While being involved with the Loyal Order, Dr. Warford founded the Black Male Development Circle. This organization was meant to encourage and engage Black males from on and off campus. Dr. Waford has also has since moved to Tallahassee and is a part of the faculty of Florida A and M University In this interview, Councilwoman Teresa Watkins Brown speaks about her early life with her family and the entrepreneurial skills they all possessed. She speaks about her education experiences during integration in the late 1960s to early 1970s. Ms. Brown then later delves into the the racial divide between the Black and White communities where she lived and they’re decline over the years as prominent areas In this interview, William Ferrell talks about his life growing up in Americus, Georgia. His family moved to Ft. Myers Florida when he was five. As he got older, he worked as an usher in the Grand Theater in Ft. Myers. He also served in the United States Army. After leaving the army, Ferrell went to Michigan to work in a steel factory. Ferrell also talks about his experiences as a police officer during time of pre and post segregation. He discusses the race riot of 1971 in Ft. Myers and how it started. He also discusses life in Ft. Myers and what it was like for a Black person. Ferrell has also been an active member of St. Johns First Missionary Baptist Church Mr. Arthur Madison is interviewed here by his granddaughter Quanisha. He talks about family history, including growing up near Albany, Georgia, and how his father died after a mule kicked him hard in the chest. He shares memories from his childhood such as picking enough pecans to go to the movies later that same day, and also describes some of his parents’ history and that of his siblings, nieces, and nephews. Mr. Madison speaks on his Christian faith throughout the interview, and also chronicles some of the funerals the family held through the years At the time of this interview, Akil Reynolds was the vice president of the Black Student Union at the University of Florida, and in this interview he explores some of the meaning the BSU had for him as a student, and discusses some of its history and contemporary operations. He also speaks on UF’s history and its complex relationship with the Black community over time. Mr. Reynolds also offers his memories of the original Institute of Black Culture; at the time of the interview, the original building had been demolished and the new structure had not yet been built. Throughout the interview, he shares some thoughts on UF’s success in meeting the needs of Black students, particularly in the time of the 2017 white supremacist march in Charlottesville and Richard Spencer’s speaking engagement at UF later that same year At the time of this interview, Akil Reynolds was the vice president of the University of Florida Black Student Union. He emphasizes a need for UF to recognize and own up to past injustices, and to actively support social justice moving forward. In addition to sharing memories on how and why he decided to attend UF, he discusses his experiences as a student and details his involvement in student organizations and other extracurricular activities Rev. Flavius Josephus Johnson grew up in Mobile, Alabama. His parents worked for a Jewish family, and that family persuaded his parents to name him after the noted ancient Jewish historian Flavius Josephus. Rev. Johnson went to a mostly-White school for much of his life because of the connection to that family, though later in life he attended the Allen Institute, and went on to matriculate to Alabama State University. He shares his recollections of growing up in Alabama during Jim Crow segregation, and also his memories of his time serving in the U.S. Army during the Korean War, and his work as a Methodist minister after he was discharged Mr. Wyard Dennis was eighty-seven at the time of this interview. He describes growing up just outside of Jonesville, Florida, not far from the towns of Newberry and Gainesville. He describes his experiences as a lifelong farmer, and his involvement in area churches, particularly Pleasant Plains United Methodist Church in Jonesville. Mr. Dennis describes his experiences in the U.S. military, serving in Europe during the Korean War. After leaving the Service, he recalls being the head cook at Hume Hall when it first opened on the University of Florida campus. Over his lifetime in addition to serving as a cook at UF, he worked at the Florida Farm Bureau, and worked at the Florida State Prison Earsel Lewis grew up near Philadelphia, Mississippi, and in this interview shares some memories of his father’s life as a sharecropper, but focuses primarily on his military service during the Korean War. That service includes combat experiences in Korea, for which he won several medals including a Purple Heart, and also experiences with racism and segregation stateside and in daily life in the military. He closes the interview with stories from his work in ministry, to which he was called later in life In this interview, Estelle Forehand recounts her experiences growing up and living in Quincy, Florida. In discussing these experiences, she covers such topics as local Black-owned businesses, religion, cooking and food, as well as other aspects of life in Gadsden County. Ms. Forehand also explains her experiences with voter registration during the Civil Rights Movement in Quincy In this interview, Ms. Stevens recounts her family’s experiences living in Southern Georgia, as well as her own experiences upon moving to New York. She discusses her career in nursing, as well as her adoption of children from foster care. Also covered is her role in church leadership, as well as her recollections of the Civil Rights Movement in both New York and the South, though she was not directly involved Mr. Reed, a decorated veteran, is being interviewed by SPOHP volunteer Michael Parker on June 1, 2018. Mr. Reed starts talking about his life of growing up in McIntosh, Alabama during the Depression, getting drafted into the military for World War II being a part of D-Day working as a stove mechanic at that time. After his return, he settled down and lived his life being a life insurance agent, a single father of nine children, and living as a Black man during and after segregation in the south. He dealt with being part of the federal grand jury of the events of Bloody Sunday 1965 and his close encounter with the Ku Klux Klan Katrina Rolle was the director of the Institute of Black Culture in the 1990s, and she talks about her role as its director in this interview. She goes over what the IBC meant to the students during her time, intersectionality between other houses, and the importance of networking Judge Isaac Anderson speaks about growing up in South Florida. His was one of the last generations in segregated schools In this interview, Fort Meyers elder Bessie Washington recalls her life as the youngest of twelve children growing up in various cities in Central Florida. Washington was born in Gretna, Florida, but moved to Lake Wales, where her father helped design the Bok Tower. After her father’s death, Washington and her family moved to Bradenton, then Sarasota, where Washington met her first husband. After the death of her first husband, Washington moved to Fort Meyers where she met her second husband. Later, she and her second husband moved to Brooklyn, New York, where they managed rental properties. Washington recalls her careers as a beautician and seamstress, noting that she had to retire after her breast cancer diagnosis. Although she had to endure a double mastectomy, Washington survived her battle with cancer and went on to retire in Fort Meyers, Florida In this interview, Mr. Russell Brown starts by discussing his upbringing in the Dunbar area and how integration began during his years attending Dunbar High. Mr In this interview, Ms. Nina Rogers discusses coming to Lee County in 1965 as well as her memories of Dunbar during the time. She then goes into detail abut teaching in Lee County, including Dunbar Elementary School, J. Colin School and Calusa Elementary. Ms. Rogers talks about both Dunbar High School and Dunbar Middle School and their significance to the community In this interview, African American Fort Meyers elder Jacob Johnson recalls his childhood and early life in Fort Meyers, as well as on Sanibel Island. Johnson recounts the horrific lynching of two schoolboys of Williams Academy in Fort Meyers, who were wrongly accused of raping a White girl. Johnson remembers the psychological effects of the lynching on both his family and the broader Black community of Fort Meyers as a whole. Johnson explains that the lynching was part of a wave of terror attacks against Black communities in the area during the period. This violence, Johnson remembers, and caused his family and other Blacks residents to flee the area due to fear for their safety. Johnson also recounts the impact of educators such as one man, Professor Dickson, who created a high school for Black students called Dunbar. Johnson remembers that the efforts of Dickson and other Black educators resulted in his admission into Florida A&M University In this interview, Fort Meyers elder Jacob Johnson recalls his memory of the Black community during the 1920s, 1930s, and 1940s on Sanibel Island. He remembers the Black community’s struggle for property ownership during the first half of the twentieth century on Sanibel lsland, recalling that White property owners refused to sell land to Blacks. He remembers raising chickens, catching fish, and growing crops alongside his brothers and sisters in order to support the family through the Depression. Johnson also recalls the impact of some of his teachers at Williams Academy, the first Black school in the area. Johnson remembers how members of the community formed the Sportsman Club, which rallied to rebrand the Black area of town as “Dunbar,” instead of other derogatory names used in the past. He concludes with memories of the Sportsman Club’s fundraising activities and mentions that he plans to donate the club’s charter to a soon-to-open Black history museum In this interview, Elder Luke Black recounts his experiences growing up in Chipley, Florida, as well as his life in Quincy, Florida and Fort Myers, Florida. These experiences include his work in tobacco farming as a young person, as well as preaching and eventually becoming a pastor in Fort Myers. Elder Black also reflects on changes he has witnessed in Fort Myers over the years, as well as the role of religion in his community, and the relationship between the African-American and White communities in Chipley and Fort Myers Interviewers Janet Snyder Matthews and Nina Denson Rogers sit down with Pat McCutcheon, an African American elder from Lee County, Florida to recollect his life story. He recalls that his father moved to Lee County to become an independent vegetable farmer. He remembers that many African Americans in Lee County acted as truck farmers, growing fresh vegetables for transportation and sale around the country. He recalls that his mother worked as a cook for prominent families in the Fort Meyers community. However, the arrival of the Great Depression plunged his family into economic hardship, with the majority of their savings in the bank lost to the crash. He credits the Depression to the death of his father in 1933, explaining that he died from stress. McCutcheon recalls the layout of Fort Meyers during the 1920s, including details about its residents and their relationships to one another. McCutcheon recalls the collective style of parenting employed by members of his community, in which families watched out for each other’s children. He recalls the significance of the Dunbar School—opened with donation money from the Black community—which educated African American teenagers in Fort Meyers under the leadership of Professor Dixon, a relative of McCutcheon on his father’s side In this interview, several former students of the Williams Primary Academy in Fort Meyers discuss the layout of the school. The project is part of an effort to recreate the William’s Academy building in a historically accurate manner. Interviewees discuss the location of classrooms, offices, doorways, and other features of the building In this interview, Savannah Campbell recalls her childhood in Twin Lakes, Florida, where she was raised on a farm. She remembers that her family survived by growing, eating, and selling crops such as string beans, tomatoes, sugarcane, and potatoes. Campbell recalls that she attended a nearby elementary school until the fourth grade, when members of the Ku Klux Klan burned down her school and several other buildings in the community. Campbell worked as a custodian at Buckingham Army Base before beginning a teacher career at Sunnyland. Campbell endured several major hardships over the course of her life, including the loss of both of her children and her first husband. In spite of this, Campbell maintains a positive outlook on life. She explains that she is spending her retirement as the mother of her church, Saint Paul’s, where she assists the pastor and acts as church treasurer In this interview, Janet Snyder Mathews sits down with Black Lee County elder Pat McCutcheon to discuss his life in Fort Meyers, Florida. McCutcheon attended the Williams Academy in the mid-1920s, one of the most acclaimed schools for Black students in the state. He then attended Dunbar Academy, run by his father’s relative Professor Dixon. He recalls the layout of the school for Mathews, remembering the location of various features like doorways and staircases In this interview, the University of Florida’s Senior Director of Multicultural Affairs, William Atkins reminisces on his times at UF when he was a young Black student pursuing his undergraduate degree and being the first in his family to be allowed admission to UF. Atkins recalls his experiences going to a predominantly Black high school in Tampa and a legacy of attending such schools in his family. In attending a historically Black high school, the University of Florida was not emphasized in the college admissions process and Atkins learned from his high school teachers about UF’s racist history. Despite that, he recalls attending the Florida Invitational Step Show with a friend attending UF and how this expression of Black identity on campus steered him in a new direction for college applications. Atkins soon began attending UF following his acceptance, an achievement his parents were not able to achieve due to UF being segregated in their time. He recounts his time in different student organizations such as BSU (Black Student Union), pledging Alpha Phi Alpha, and the various efforts these organizations made to educate the Black student body. These experiences with education were formative, and led to Atkins pursuing a PhD in education at the University of Florida following some years in Michigan where he received his master’s. Upon returning to UF, he was working with MFOS (Machen Florida Opportunity Scholarship) and later became the interim Director of Multicultural Affairs after the department experienced a tumultuous time. Atkins details his experiences in his current position as the Senior Director of MCDA, and the demolishing of La Casita and IBC (Institute of Black Culture) buildings in order to create a new, more modern facility and the historical context behind these projects In this interview, Ida Mae Golden shares the story of her life in Lee County, Florida, after her birth in the late 1920s. She grew up alongside seven siblings in what she defines as a relatively poor family. Her father, originally from Jacksonville, Florida, was employed with the Eldebaut Company as a construction worker. The family’s limited finances led the children to create their own toys out of junk materials, an effort Golden remembers fondly. Golden recalls the layout of the Black community in Fort Meyers at the time, remembering important social hubs in the community and other landmarks from her childhood. Golden recalls first attending William’s Academy for primary school before moving up to the relatively prestigious all-Black Dunbar High School. Later, her children would go on to be among the first to integrate the formerly all-White Fort Meyers High School. After graduating high school, Golden worked as a domestic in private homes. Golden, who attended Friendship Baptist Church, recalls the importance of the church in her community and personal life alike. She recalls the efforts of Isadore Davis, who she dubs Father of the Civil Rights Movement in Fort Meyers, crediting him with emboldening the Black community to take action agains Julian Valdivia sits down with Hubert and Betty Toney, an elderly African American couple living in Fort Meyers, Florida. Hubert Toney was born in Casper, Georgia, and moved to Fort Meyers in the late 1950s to attend the Dunbar School. Betty was born and raised in Fort Meyers and also attended the Dunbar School. She recalls the hardships of earning an education during Jim Crow, remembering that her textbooks and class materials were typically received second-hand from White schools. She remembers the immense effort of her teachers to overcome these barriers in order to impart a decent education on herself and other students. Hubert went to work for a local construction company before he was hired by Jerome Lumber, where he worked fo In this interview, Fort Meyers elder George Mayo recounts his life for interviewer Julian Valdivia. Mayo was born in Panama City Florida, but permanently moved to Fort Meyers in 1947. In Fort Meyers, his father worked at a sawmill, his mother worked in a boarding house, and he began attending the Dunbar School. At Dunbar, Mayo and other students learned from disused textbooks discarded by the local White school. Mayo, who graduated high school during the heat of the Korean War, decided to escape the monotony of a life in agricultural labor by enlisting. He spent three years on duty, where he was sent to various parts of the Deep South, including Alabama and South Carolina. On leave, he married his wife of sixty-four years when he was just nineteen. After returning from leave, he was sent to Korea where he remained for eighteen months in Busan serving as a driver for the military police. After returning from the service, Mayo attended Florida A&M University, graduating shortly thereafter In this interview, Fort Meyers native Lovie Wells, Jr recalls his life for interviewer Julian Valdivia. Born in Fort Meyers in 1952, Wells was the child of two teachers and the oldest of four siblings. Prior to his teaching career, Wells’ father enlisted in the Army and became a master sergeant trained in guided missiles In this interview, Levon and Nancy Simms recount their experiences as Black students in the city of Fort Myers before and after desegregation. Nancy, the daughter of a high school English teacher, recounts moving from Miami to Fort Myers because of her parents’ divorce, and the stark differences between services and opportunities available to Black people in both cities. She recalls Miami having separate movie theaters and decent beaches for Black folks, while Fort Myers had almost none. Levon reminisces about working on Zipperer Farms with both his parents and the differences in wages for Black folks. He later became a teacher after attending FAMU, and recounts a student walk out organized at North Fort Myers High School that extended to other high schools within the area. The walk out received attention from local police departments, with some high schools experiencing a heavy police presence that also included canines. They later suggest other people to interview who were at the high school where the canine unit was present In this second interview, Levon and Nancy Simms continue recounting their experiences as two Black people in Lee County, mentioning difficulties and discrimination in their search for an apartment when they were younger, along with demonstrations and their involvement in the NAACP. Together they detail their struggles in finding an apartment following getting married, and how they were not shown apartments because of racist, discriminatory practices by realty offices. Later, when looking to build a home, they encountered similar issues with banks as they searched for a loan to build on a plot of land they already owned. Both educators later became involved with their community via the NAACP. Levon additionally served on the City Council for nine years in an attempt to bring Black businesses back to Martin Luther King Boulevard, among other efforts Interviewer Julian Valdivia sits down with Mattie Harris, a Fort Meyers elder born in 1935. Harris’s father worked on a railroad and ran a tobacco farm in central Florida, which he received as part of his inheritance from his grandfather. Harris’s family moved from Florida to Brooklyn, New York, where her father was drafted into the service in industrial labor at the outbreak of World War II. Harris then moved to Miami after the death of her mother and attended Booker T. Washington High School while living with her grandmother. She returned to her hometown in Colombia County, Florida, where she completed high school at Richardson High in Lake City. She attended Gibbs Junior College and began taking classes at Florida A&M University, but marriage and the challenges of raising children prevented her from finishing her degree. She moved to Milton, Florida, where higher education opportunities for African Americans were virtually nonexistent, which eventually led to her family’s migration to Lee County. In Lee County, Harris took classes at Edison, University of South Florida, and Barry University, earning a degree in elementary education. She worked at the department of health and rehabilitation for a period of time, then as a substitute teacher, then began working as a reading teacher under a program called Title I in Lee County. Now, Harris keeps busy by acting as the historian for Mount Olive, a church in the Fort Meyers community In this interview, Julian Valdivia sits down with Mattie Young, a Fort Myers native and the daughter of well-known local politician Veronica Shoemaker. As a young girl, she joined the Girl Scouts, participating in a variety of events and programs. Raised by a widowed single mother and devout Christian, Young became involved with her faith at an early age, attending Mount Olive Methodist Episcopal Church. She remembers her mother’s early political campaigns, remembering that a love for her community spurred her mother to become active in local politics. Before politics, her mother had been active in PTA, the Dunbar Little League, and other local associations. Shoemaker was elected to the city council in the 1980s, where she emphasized creating a clean- living environment for Fort Myers residents regardless of socioeconomic status. Young also remembers her mother’s involvement with NAACP-led integration protests including sit-ins and marches. She followed in her mother’s footsteps, participating in a mass walk-out at the integrated North Fort Myers High School, protesting the maltreatment of Black students. After graduating high school, she attended Florida State University. Looking back on her time at North Fort Myers High School, Young reflects that the walk-out was a major step in bringing the Black and White students together by openly addressing the issues brought on by integration In this interview, Julian Valdivia talks with Gwendolyn Brooks, who grew up in Fort Meyers, Florida. Although Brooks traveled throughout the United States for her education and school, she frequently returned to Fort Meyers to visit family members In this interview, former educator Frederick White recounts his years at Fort Myers Junior High during integration, and the environment that followed. After receiving his education degree from Allen University in Columbia, South Carolina and returning to Fort Myers to teach, he received difficult news: he was being drafted. Frederick White reflects on what it was like to be drafted, in basic training, and serving in the Vietnam War as a Black man, and later, to come home to a country in turmoil. He recounts getting back into teaching after briefly adjusting to life back home, and his frustration that many schools in the area had poor curriculums that did not include the Black experience. He fondly remembers coworkers and mentors who helped him get work as a teacher that eventually led to his thirty years at Fort Myers Junior High (now Fort Myers Middle). While teaching, he also led a troop of Boy Scouts and was involved in community team sports. This community work was inspired by members of the Black community in Fort Myers who came before him and led the way. He mentions how these experiences informed his path, now as a minister who speaks about history and racism In this interview, Julian Valdivia talks with Fort Myers native Walt Wesley, Sr. Wesley recalls that he was born in 1945 to Leroy and Rosie Presley, who both worked as educators in the Lee County School System after graduating from Florida A&M University. He recalls that his father, a World War II veteran, was active in the community, and was involved in American Legion, a volunteer sheriff’s organization, the board of a credit union and veteran’s affairs. Wesley began his education at Everglades Elementary in Everglades City, Florida. He went on to attend junior high at Immokalee before attending Dunbar High School in Fort Myers, where he learned to play basketball. Later, he moved to Kansas to play basketball at the University of Kansas In this interview, Fort Myers native Marion Redden Sims shares her life story with interviewer Julian Valdivia. She was born in 1945 as one of the five daughters of Archie and Bertha Redden, who worked as a roofer and domestic respectively. She began attending school at Dunbar Elementary and graduated from Dunbar High School in 1963, where she was a cheerleader. Her family became involved with the Civil Rights Movement through their membership in Friendship Baptist Church, a church known for organizing anti-segregation protests and sit-ins in the community. After graduating high school, Sims attended Tennessee A&I University, where she earned a bachelor’s degree in nutrition with the goal of becoming a hospital dietician. She attended a year- long internship at the Tuskegee Institute in Alabama, after which she joined the Air Force to work in hospital dietetics. After leaving the service, she attempted to get a job at a hospital in Naples, Florida, but was turned down and instead decided to open a small business. While back in Fort Myers, she re-engaged with the Civil Rights Movement through Friendship Baptist Church under Reverend Isadore Edwards, participating in demonstrations against racial discrimination. She eventually landed a job assistant director of the food service department at Sarasota Memorial Hospital, where she remained for over twenty years, becoming director of the department. She retired after she felt called into the ministry and became ordained under the Christian Methodist Episcopal denomination In this interview, Black Fort Myers elder Ronald Davis recalls his life for interviewer Julian Valdivia. Davis was born in 1947 in Evansville, Indiana. His father, a World War II veteran in the Army Air Corps, worked at a telephone company at the time of his birth. The family moved to Fort Myers, Florida when Davis was still a child after a physician advised his mother to move to a warmer climate. His mother became the secretary for Riverside Baptist Church, where she remained for twenty-two years, and his father earned employment with a local phone company. Davis attended Edgewood Elementary School in Fort Myers. He went on to attend junior high at James Stephens Middle School—formerly called Lee Junior High—an institution he later became the principal of. Davis went on to attend Edison Junior College before graduating from the University of Florida. Though he was offered a fellowship to continue his education in computer science, he was informed of the draft board’s decision to draft him unless he willingly enlisted into the service or obtained an occupational exception. He landed a job as a math teacher at Bradford County Middle School, where he began his lifelong career as an educator. He taught school for five years in Maryland while earning a master’s degree. Davis began teaching in Fort Myers in the late 1970s, where he remained until he retired In this interview, interviewer Samantha Crisanti sits down with Ebony Elizabeth Thomas, an African American scholar-activist originally from Detroit, Michigan. She attended Bates Academy for the Gifted and Talented in Detroit, which she credits for giving her the foundation for a successful life. Originally, she attended Courtis Elementary School, but transferred when a teacher informed her parents of her exceptional academic abilities. She went on to attend Renaissance High School, a magnet school in Detroit, before heading to Florida A&M University to earn a higher education. Although she enjoyed academics, an attempted sexual assault during her college years left her psychologically distraught, especially after being ostracized by school administrators and psychologists she appealed to. The event, however, caused her to advocate for the Black Feminist Movement, insisting that the women’s movement should not be restricted to White feminists. After FAMU, she worked as a public school teacher in Detroit for six years before going back to college to obtain a master’s degree from Wayne State and a doctorate from Michigan. Thomas goes on to recount some of her family history, including her father’s background as an alcoholic Vietnam Veteran suffering from debilitating psychological trauma. She also shares that her great- grandfather was lynched in 1919 in Alabama, a detail she discovered while browsing Ancestry.com. Today, Dr. Thomas studies children’s literature, with a focus on the depiction of children of color in twenty-first century literature In this interview John Bonaparte’s granddaughter sits down with him to discuss his life in Central Florida and his careers in the Navy, as a newspaper printer, and as a carpenter. Bonaparte, who served in World War II on supply convoys bringing food to various bases around the globe, reflects on segregation in the military. He also reflects on the effects of segregation and desegregation alike in civilian life In this interview, Essie Anderson talks about her early life and family, her time as a Custodial Supervisor at the University of Florida, the University Murders in the 1990s, her views on different Presidents of the University of Florida, and her retirement. Anderson relives her childhood talking about the school she went to and her life. She also talks a lot about her relationship with her family talking about their careers and aspiration. Anderson worked at the University of Florida as a Custodial Supervisor working at the university for over twenty years. As a Custodial Supervisor she has seen the University of Florida change from how Black students and faculty were treated to her perceptions of different university presidents. Throughout the interview Anderson praised Dr. Lombardi for his leadership at the University of Florida and for taking the time to talk to custodial workers. She also talks about the University Murders in the 1990s and how the university handled the situation and protected their workers. In the conclusion, Anderson talks warmly of her family and her plans in retirement including spending time with her grandkids and volunteering In this interview, agriculture student Marna Weston interviews University of Florida Building Services staff member Kenneth Green. Green begins with an in-depth recollection of his family history on both his mother and father’s sides. Then, Green recalls his childhood in Chicago with several siblings. He remembers attending several different Catholic schools in the Chicago area, where he developed a lifelong love for learning. Green recalls that himself and his two brothers all joined the Armed Forces after graduating high school, however, Green explains that he was honorably discharged due to a congenital heart defect. He recalls several tragic murders in his family, including the death of an uncle to police violence, the death of a stepson to gang conflicts, and the murder of his uncle by his aunt. Although he is a Catholic, Green explains he has been exposed to Voodoo and Roots spiritual practices through other members of the Black community. Green reveals some of what he learned about these spiritual practices while living in South Carolina. Green then recalls his experiences as an employee with Building Services for the University of Florida, comparing it to his work as a janitor in Chicago. Green concludes by describing his love for animals, particularly snake In this interview, John Booth discusses his experience working with the Airmark Food Corporation with the University of Florida. Mr. Booth also recounts his time attending Lincoln High School during the era of segregation and how different attending school was during his time. Mr. Booth then goes into detail about how different the city of Gainesville was during the era of segregation In this interview, which was conducted on Elizabeth Durant’s 101st birthday, Ms. Durant reflects on the reasons for her good health and longevity, claiming them to be because of her faith and Love. Ms. Durant briefly talks about desegregation and the effect it had on the Black community, as well as her opinion on the matter Marna Weston interviews Jaquelin Jenkins and Eric Jenkins in Deland, Florida Unknown people at Rollins College interviewed. Keywords: [African American History; Rollins College Ms. Filer begins this interview by discussing her childhood in Gainesville, Florida, with a particular focus on her experiences at Lincoln High School. She goes on to recount her experiences in schooling, eventually being one of the first black students at UF’s school of nursing. She goes over her experiences in the medical field at length, as well as her experiences in local civil rights groups such as Gainesville Women for Equal Rights. Also of note in this interview is her discussion of the Cotton Club and her church involvement, as well as the experiences of her parents working in Gainesville In this interview, Kevin Sharpe goes into detail about his love and involvement of music since a young age. He goes into detail about his experiences travelling with Duke Ellington as a child singer. Mr. Sharpe also mentions his involvement with the Newark Boys’ Chorus. He then talks about his introduction to classical music through the self-teaching of the piano. Through this love of classical music, Mr. Sharpe talks about his various collegiate experiences in the field of classical music and all the lessons it gave him. Towards the end, Mr. Sharpe goes into detail about his experiences working at UF and how it has changed during his time working there In this interview, Mary Kenney, UF Alumni, discusses her upbringing being adopted at the age of four and how that experience was. Ms. Kenney also talks about her relationship with religion and how it was partially influenced by her parents. She then talks about her involvement with church in Gainesville as well as the worship team. Ms. Kenney delves into her involvement in groups at the University of Florida and her experiences with racial tensions in grade school. Towards the conclusion of the interview, she talks about how her experience at UF impacted her identity In this interview, Keirten Nivol, a Dominican-Haitian computer science major at the University of Florida, shares his experience as an immigrant and student with Christine Berry of the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program. Nivol remembers his childhood in Santiago, Dominican Republic, noting the overarching sense of community within the city’s diverse demographic. Nivol recounts his family’s fourteen-year struggle to apply for residency to the United States, which began before his birth. Nivol’s family settled in Broward County, Florida where he attended Blanche Ely High School. Nivol later arrived at the University of Florida, where he began taking classes in computer science and public policy. Nivol credits his interest in public policy to his involvement with Dream Defenders, an organization that promotes nonviolent protest and civic engagement to Black, Brown, and immigrant youth. He also remains active in the Black Student Union and National Society of Black Engineers. Nivol reflects on the academic challenges posed in the United States that disproportionately affect Black, Brown, and immigrant students. He notes the lack of diversity at the University of Florida, attributing it to the university’s history of racial discrimination and ineffective affirmative action Vivian Lee and Arthur Cunningham reflect on their childhood and adolescence in mid- twentieth century Florida. Their experiences dealing with segregation and institutional racism as African-Americans are detailed, however they each recall many joyous moments in their communities among the harshness of society. Cunningham additionally describes his life as a Black man in both the South and the North, indicating the two are not as different as they’d like to think. Lee goes into detail about her adult and senior life, demonstrating contentment with her journey and the struggle to come to terms with age. Their values of grit and appreciation for life shine through, despite all the struggles they were forced to endure as African-Americans in a period of extreme discrimination In this interview, Madison, Florida elder Monroe Lee recounts the first half of his life. He begins with recollections of his family history, highlighting his multiethnic lineage, although he primarily identifies as Black. In his earliest memories, Lee worked alongside his family members in the tobacco and cotton fields. In later years, Lee attended public high school, during which he had time to dual enroll and take courses in drafting and architecture at a local community college. Lee then began his career with the Geographic Survey, which took him as far as Hawaii, where he surveyed a volcano. Fearing the surveying job was taking a toll on him, Lee decided to pursue a career in preaching. He married Zulla Lee, and together they had one daughter. Throughout his twenties and thirties, Lee worked a variety of jobs. He briefly worked on a horse ranch in Alachua, Florida, and often took jobs designing churches, schools, and other structures. The interview with participants agreeing to resume the following day In this interview, Cecile Scoon talks about her upbringing and her transfer from the Caribbean the United States at the age of fifteen. She talks about her experience attending the National Cathedral School for Girls in D.C. After, Cecile Scoon talks about what is was like pursuing a law career. She then talks about her time in Panama City as well as ger work with the League of Women’s Voters In this interview, activist Robert Brown Jr. recalls his involvement in the Civil Rights Movement in Tallahassee. He remembers the Tallahassee Bus Boycott and the efforts of the late Laura Dixie towards ending segregation in Tallahassee, Florida In this interview, Joe Hartman sits down with Ocala elder Whitfield Jenkins for a follow-up interview concerning his life as a football player, military veteran, NAACP activist, and correctional officer. Jenkins recalls that he was born in Bethlehem, a small town outside of Ocala, Florida in 1940. He grew up on his father’s forty-acre farm and attended a two room all-Black schoolhouse in town. He recalls that his brother, now a pastor in Sarasota, Florida, was incarcerated at the Dozier School for Boys in Marianna, Florida after frequently running away from home. After attending the Fessenden School in Ocala, Jenkins attended Florida A&M University, where he played football. After finishing at FAMU, Jenkins was drafted into the military during the Vietnam War, where he served for two years as a military police officer. After his time in the service, he met his wife Lauretta. Jenkins then worked briefly at the newly-integrated Osceola High School teaching science and math. Later, Jenkins became one of the first African Americans to gain a high-ranking position in the Department of Corrections After watching the film “Footsteps to Freedom,” Reverend Henry Steele and Curtis Richardson discuss their upbringing and experiences with racism. Reverend Steele discusses his introduction to the Civil Rights Movement through the Bus Boycott and lunch counter demonstrations. Reverend Steele grew up in an activist Baptist family, and he discusses the intersection between church and civil rights, with a focus on the movement in Tallahassee. Steele talks about historical figures like Dr. Ralph David Abernathy, whom he worked with. Curtis Richardson, on the other hand, talks about his position as the city commissioner and mayor pro tem of the city of Tallahassee. Richardson mostly discusses racial discrimination in the school system and Tallahassee neighborhoods. Richardson built his family’s home on the south side of town, where he said people are leaving from. Richardson is working on improving the area’s conditions without aiding gentrification and raising property taxes. Finally, Gloria Jefferson Anderson uplifts women who held a significant role in the Civil Rights Movement and encourages everyone to not give up sharing history In this interview, lifelong Micanopy, Florida resident George Woodard recalls his life working a variety of hands-on labor jobs. Primary, Woodard remembers working for several decades at a crate factory alongside several of his family members. On his off time, he would work odd jobs in Gainesville. He also worked a variety of agricultural jobs in Micanopy, including the cultivating and harvesting of peas, watermelons, beans, and other produce. Woodard never married and has lived in his family home for his entire life. In his free time, he enjoys bicycling, shooting pool, and talking with visitors to the Micanopy General Store, where he occasionally works Reverend Dr. Lorenzo Edwards pastored for fifty years, became president of the Marion County chapter of the NAACP, was elected to the Ocala City Council, and served as the dean of minority affairs at Central Florida Community College. In this interview, he tells important stories from his own life. However, with Edwards’s extensive knowledge of Ocala—especially Ocala’s Black community—this oral history also documents the history of Ocala, Florida, from segregation, through integration, and to the present day. Edwards describes growing up during Jim Crow segregation, and he describes later fighting against that segregation through peaceful demonstrations and protests. He explains his educational history, from earning an AA at Central Florida Community College to earning a BA and an MA at the University of Florida. He tells the story of how he was motivated to preach—motivation which began when he found a Bible at a golf course he caddied at as a young man. He reflects on his impact on community members during his role as pastor at Mount Moriah In this panel, West Point cadets reflect on their experiences speaking with African American elders in the Gainesville, Florida area. The cadets share their interpretations of the term “White privilege” and reflect on the necessary steps to reduce racial discrimination in the United States. The panel discusses the issues of gentrification, education inequality, and racial profiling in light on their experiences speaking with African Americans in Gainesville LaFanette Soles-Woods gives a guided tour of the many landfills in her neighborhood, where she works to raise awareness about environmental threats from toxic chemicals in the environment from these waste dumps in the Pensacola, Florida area In this interview, Joe Davis talks about his time in the military, his family, and Pensacola. Davis made a career out of being in the military including being an infantry man and a recruiter. Due to his successful career, Davis was able to retire and work in Customs, a postman, and later open his own seafood truck. Davis also talks about his family and about how he tries to teach his family about life and God. Raised in Pensacola, Mr. Davis cares a lot about the community talk about the housing crisis and how Black people have been disapprovingly put in jail. He talks about creating a plan where the community donates a dollar a month so they don’t have to rely on the government and can tackle the housing and prison issues. Davis concludes the interview talking about his relationship with God and gives advice to how people should be trying to help one another In this interview, Mr. Ronald Johnson discusses his personal life history, including his childhood experiences with racism in the Florida education system, his time in the Marine Corps, and his work after retiring as a veteran and becoming involved in Pensacola. He provides valuable insight into the history of the Black community across the US South, including the process of integrating schools, as well as the perspective of race within the military In this interview, Dr. John Veasley recalls his time growing up in the small town of Kosciusko, Mississippi as a Black boy attending segregated and later, integrated, schools. He notes that in integrated schools, he was not given adequate instruction or encouraged in the same way his Black teachers had taught him in segregation. He has retraced his family history and shares about his great grandparents working under Freedman Bureau labor contracts and sharecropping, and how this led to being raised around farming. He fondly reminisces on his time at Tougaloo College in Jackson, Mississippi and getting involved after being inspired by Freedom Fighters. He notes the difficulty he faced as a Black man applying to dental schools following college graduation, noting that many programs had a quota for Blacks, and that the number was very low. He attended dental school at Howard University, and recounts later joining the Coast Guard because of a US Public Health Service loan. He shares his experiences about being a Black dentist in the Coast Guard in New York, and how it was difficult to move higher up the ladder in rank due to racial discrimination. He transferred to a federal prison in Pensacola, Florida and notes the differences between the climate towards Black people from Mississippi, and the northeast Lifelong educators, Ethel Wingate Coleman and Julia Martin Arnold reflect on their lives, education, education, careers, and continued community involvement Henry Minor recalls growing up in Pensacola, Florida in the late-1920s. He describes being drafted into military service upon his high school graduation in 1942. After eighteen months in the navy in Guadalcanal, he returned home and was preparing for a second period overseas when World War II ended, allowing him to end his service. He explains how his mother taught him to play piano, hoping he would play in a church one day. These skills eventually turned into his long-held musical career, prompted when two members of the Black community in Pensacola hired him to play in their new night club. Minor found that the earnings from his musical gigs were much greater than other jobs available to him at the time. Eventually, he auditioned other musicians and put together his own band, Chick Minor and the Blue Notes. Under different band names and with different bandmembers, Minor played around clubs in the Southeast for around forty years. His advice to young musicians is to learn to play the music people want to year, rather than simply what the musicians themselves want to play. He explains, “You got to listen and play. In this interview, Dr. Eurydice Stanley discusses her life as an African American girl growing up in Paso Robles, California and moving around with her family, as well as explaining her early experiences attending Florida A&M University, an HBCU, and later serving as in the US military. Dr. Stanley describes the prevalence of racism and racial discrimination, as well as her activism and community work to promote diversity, acceptance, and inclusion. She mentions her work with Diversity Connections, Equal Opportunity, and as an officer for the Public Affairs Detachment and other military training programs, as well as describing her personal life. She discusses the grief of losing family members to suicide, her mentor-mentee relationship with Alice Dixon, a member of the first all-female, all-Black unit to fight for the US in WWII, and her experiences raising children in Pensacola’s school system. Along with her past, Dr In this interview, Ieshia Williams talks about her upbringing, her time in the military and her reflections after. At the beginning of the interview, Williams talks about the difference between growing up in Georgia compared to her time in California including how the curriculum was different and the culture shock it induced. At Stone Mountain High School, Williams was introduced to the recruiter and decided that she would join the Air Force shortly after high school. Williams talks about her time in the Air Force including the sexism and trauma of being in the military. In conclusion, Williams talks about reflecting on her time in the Air Force including understand the social structures that existed within the military Samuel Watson remembers his experiences as a black student at an all- black school in Pensacola, Florida. There, his teacher Mrs. James inspired his passion for both teaching and learning. He joined the band in high school and discovered a passion for music that he carried with him for the rest of his life. He continued his education at Florida A&M University, working a job at a resort cafeteria in Hot Springs, Virginia serving wealthy white patrons to put himself through college. Later, he completed a master's degree at Indiana University, then worked as a history teacher, administrator, and coach in Palm Beach county schools during integration in the 1970s. After retiring from his work as an educator in Palm Beach, Watson completed his doctoral degree at Florida Atlantic University In this interview, Mamie Webb Hixon reminisces on her time at Ms. Lily James’ school and her lessons there. She recalls that Ms. Lily was a prominent figure in her life that set her on a path towards education. She lists her other teachers, all the way through twelfth grade, and what it was like to be a student in segregated schools in Pensacola, Florida. She describes her years at Talladega College, an HBCU in Alabama and how she felt insulated from but inspired by the Civil Rights Movement. She recalls how immediately after college, she began teaching and how she became director of the writing lab at a predominantly White institution, University of West Florida. She compares the benefits of HBCUs against other universities and how her passion for Black literature was ignited, not in college, but afterwards in her quest to understand her history. She details her family history, and how she had White family members and how this experience was for her Retired physical education teacher Frankie McIntosh recalls her experiences as a black student in the segregated Escambia County school system, and later as a teacher in the same county, now integrated. She describes how she accomplished her education, from attending Booker T. Washington high school, where she later taught, to graduating from Florida A&M University after studying health and physical education In this interview, former educator Sylvia Todd recounts attending school before and during integration in Pensacola, Florida, and later, her experiences teaching at the very same schools. She details her extracurricular activities while attending grade school including clubs for girls such as the YWCA and Y-Teens and following her college years, her involvement with education-oriented sorority Phi Delta Kappa. In her recollections, she includes mention of being assigned to several different schools “across the railroad tracks,” and out of date, battered textbooks among equally scarce school materials. She reminisces on her teachers at A.M. DeVaugn Elementary School and John A. Gibson School, and how those teachers informed her choice to be a Black woman in education at Booker T. Washington High School Interviewer Ryan Morini sits down with Black Pensacola elder Marilynn Wiggins to discuss her life growing up in the Florida Panhandle alongside her eight siblings. Wiggins remembers living in what she calls the “Shanty Town” of Pensacola, near downtown and named for its covert moonshine production. She recalls the layout of the Black section of downtown Pensacola during Jim Crow, which she refers to as the “Tanyard,” remembering the locations of important businesses and social hubs. She also remembers the effects of segregation on the landscape of Pensacola, recalling that some businesses resisted integration efforts well into the 1960s. Wiggins also recalls a riot among the Black community incited by the murders of Beulah Glover and James Huff on Pensacola Beach. She reflects on the current effects of gentrification in her neighborhood, lamenting the efforts to erase the heritage of the Tanyard as a historically significant area for Black Pensacolians. She notes that new houses built in the neighborhood are no longer affordable for many Black former residents, resulting in a mass exodus of Black Tanyard residents from their historic homesteads. She also reflects on the effects of police brutality on Black Pensacola, insisting that additional training should be implemented among the Pensacola Police Department to prevent unnecessary use of force In this interview, Lily D. Townsend talks about her time in segregation, her career, and the influence the church had her life. Townsend talks about how her neighborhood was integrated before integration spread across the state. Although she lived in an integrated neighborhood, when she went to downtown Pensacola she still had to live with segregation and pretending not to know her friends downtown Reginald Lewis recalls his early childhood living in Ocala, Florida, and working at Paradise Park, a Black-run nature park featuring freshwater springs. Lewis explains the cultural significance of Paradise Park on the black community locally and nationally, as it was featured in the approved destinations for black segregation-era travelers listed in the green book. Lewis describes his rural upbringing including his experiences fishing and hunting as a child. Lewis emphasizes the influence of his grandfather and stepfather on his outlook on life. He recounts his experience in the military during the Vietnam War and the time he spent in Thailand while in the service. He recounts how he used the training he received in the military to become the first black lineman in Ocala after repeatedly being denied employment due to his race. Then, he describes his later work as a lineman and firefighter with the U.S. Forest Service In this interview, Evelyn Foxx, leader of the Alachua County NAACP, reflects on her life and activism efforts for interviewers with the Sam Proctor Oral History Program. She remembers attending her first NAACP meeting in her home state of Georgia as an elementary schooler, an event which launched her into a lifelong involvement with the organization. One of her major efforts in Alachua County centered around combating the school-to-prison pipeline, which she defines as a system in which young Black students become trapped in a cycle of incarceration beginning with arrests for minor incidents at school. Foxx notes that the election of President Trump has presented a number of new challenges both for her organization and her personal life, recalling that the day after the election she began receiving death threats from a local White supremacist. After months of harassment—including the placement of a Confederate flag on Foxx’s lawn—the incidents ended with the perpetrator’s arrest In this interview, Korean War veteran Eddie Thomas shares his life story with interviewer Don Obrist. Thomas was born in Alapaha, Georgia on August 16, 1933. His family moved to Ocilla, Georgia, where he lived until he joined the Army in 1952 as a teenager. Thomas reflects that he saw the military as an opportunity to advance socioeconomically while bettering himself in the process. After an orientation in Fort Jackson, South Carolina, Thomas transferred to Maryland for basic training before heading to Atlanta for advanced training in motor vehicle ordinance. Once his training was completed, he was posted in Atlanta for the duration of his Army service. In June of 1954, he joined the Air Force and was stationed at Tyndall AFB in Panama City, Florida before being sent to Kinpo, Korea. In Korea, Thomas served as an aero-policeman, commanding the K-9 unit and earning a promotion to airman second class. After ending his tour of duty, Thomas was assigned to Greenville Air Force Base in Mississippi in 1956. He recalls the racial turmoil which ensued after fifteen-year-old Emmet Till’s murder. Next, he went to Goodfellow AFB in Texas, where he was falsely accused of sexual misconduct by a White colleague’s wife, resulting in his general discharge on an honorable condition. After leaving the Air Force in 1958, Thomas used his GI Bill to take classes in carpentry and masonry, an education which set the foundation for his self- employment as a house painter and carpenter from 1971 to 2017. During the period from 1983 to 2000, Thomas worked undercover with Florida Department of Law Enforcement to identify and arrest individuals involved in the illicit drug trade. Thomas also served as the second Black Deputy Sheriff in the history of Tifton, Georgia In this session, interviewer Elery Osterstag talks to Gulf County native and current resident of Fort White, Florida, Kemberly Jackson. Jackson spent the first half of her childhood in Gulf County, where her mother worked in a nursing home and her father worked in pulp wood. Later in her childhood, the family moved to Quincy, Florida, in Gadsden County, where her mother re-married a local preacher. She recalls working odd jobs as a child in Quincy, from catching fish bait to picking blueberries. She remembers some of her mother’s favorite recipes, recalling that many ingredients she used were sourced from the ocean or locally-raised crops and livestock. The interview concludes with a discussion of Jackson’s own cooking techniques, which she credits her mother for passing down to her In this interview, students with the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program sit down with Black Vietnam veteran Ronnie Griffin at the VFW in Monticello, Florida to document his life story. Griffin was born and educated in Greenville, Alabama, before he began junior college in Florida. Once in Florida, he was drafted for service in Vietnam in 1968. He spent nearly a year in Vietnam before leaving active combat for Fort Hood, Texas. He worked his way up the ranks, retiring from the military after twenty-one years as a Chief Warrant Officer. Griffin credits his time in Vietnam as his most horrific experience in the military, explaining that his deployment coincided with the North Vietnamese Tethered Fence offensive, a north-to-south campaign to push all occupied forces out of Vietnam in an effort to end the war. Griffin explains that his work in ammunition depots made the NVA offensive of particular concern to him, as a primary objective of the campaign was to destroy American ammunition supplies and fuel. He reflects on the long-term effects of exposure to high levels of stress, noting that many of his comrades struggled with substance abuse and post-traumatic stress disorder upon their return. Griffin credits the support of his faith and family for helping him through the transition from active-duty combat, noting that he suffered from severe nightmares at times. Griffin’s legacy of military service lives on in his two sons, one of which has earned the rank of Lieutenant Colonel In this interview, students with the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program visit the Veterans of Foreign Wars chapter in Monticello, Florida, to interview Ned Hill, Jr, a Vietnam veteran and lifelong Monticello resident. Hill graduated from Howard Academy in Monticello, then briefly attended Suwannee River Junior College before beginning a mechanical program at Florida A&M. After his graduation from FAMU, Hill was drafted in September of 1967. He went to basic training in Sand Hill Fort Benning before taking AIT in Fort McClellan, Alabama. He returned to Fort Benning for airborne training before his deployment to Cameron Bay, Vietnam as a part of the 173rd Airborne Infantry. While in Vietnam, Hill primarily participated in search-and-destroy missions. In early November of 1968, after over a year in Vietnam, Hill was injured in combat and sent to California, where he worked as a troop instructor. He returned to Florida, working alongside his grandfather on the family farm in Tallahassee while searching for permanent employment. Hill remembers that racial discrimination made it incredibly difficult for him to secure a job, so he instead took to working contract labor building houses. He eventually secured a job at a mechanic service station in Tallahassee, where he faced suspicion and discrimination from his coworkers on the basis of race. In 2008, the company failed, leaving Hill unexpectedly jobless. After his service, Hill was diagnosed with post traumatic stress disorder and anxiety, which was compounded by his tendency to drink excessively. Hill began seeing a psychiatrist, attending church, and making regular visits to the VA for treatment, leading him down towards recovery and enabling him to help other veterans in similar circumstances In this interview, University of Florida students Megan Pitt and Aanchel Shah join Vietnam veteran Ben Ransom Jr at the VFW Center in Monticello, Florida, to recount his life story. Ransom grew up in Monticello, Florida, where he worked on his family’s farm and attended Howard Academy High School. After graduation, Ransom studied history and political science at Florida A&M University for two years before joining the Marine Corps in 1970. Ransom was initially deployed to Okinawa, Japan, then spent time in Vietnam before finishing his tour of duty in Parris Island, South Carolina. While in Vietnam, Ransom worked as a mechanized supply administrator. He reflects that he enjoyed his time in the service because it gave him the opportunity to see the world, traveling to the Philippines, Japan, Thailand, Vietnam, Singapore, and British Hong Kong. Although the war gave him the opportunity to travel, Ransom concedes that he remains haunted by events he witnessed in Vietnam, including the attempted suicide of one of his platoon members. In addition to mental trauma, Ransom also suffers from Parkinson’s as a result of Agent Orange poisoning. Ransom left the Marines in 1973, finishing his degree before going to work as a high school teacher. He returned to Florida A&M University to earn a master’s degree before going to work for the university as an agricultural technician. Then, he went to work for the Florida Department of Children and Families, a subsect of the Department of Health and Rehabilitative services, where he stayed for thirty-three years investigating child abuse and working with delinquent youth. At the time of this interview, Ransom remains involved in the local Veterans of Foreign Wars chapter in Monticello, Florida, where he is surrounded by a community of veterans with similar experiences In this interview, University of Florida students Aanchel Shah and Megan Pitt sit down with Black veteran Henry McKinney at the Veterans of Foreign Wars chapter in Monticello, Florida. McKinney was born in Monticello, Florida on October 29th, 1946 as one of eight children. He attended Howard Academy High School, graduating in 1964. He remembers spending his childhood summers working in the fields, harvesting pecans and cotton in addition to working his family’s small farm. In June of 1964, McKinney moved to Detroit, Michigan, looking for better opportunities. By December in Detroit, McKinney joined the Army, hoping to follow in the footsteps of his father, a World War II veteran. McKinney spent twenty-seven years in the Army, starting with his basic and advanced training in Fort Knox, Kentucky. Then, McKinney went to Fort Benning, Georgia for airborne infantry training. After his training, McKinney joined the 173rd Airborne and was stationed in Okinawa, Japan before arriving in Vietnam in 1965. After his tour of duty, he returned to Fort Campbell, Kentucky, where he noticed intense discrimination against returning Black troops. In spite of the discrimination, McKinney earned the rank of E-5 while stationed at Fort Campbell. McKinney exited the service in December 1967 but re-enlisted a year later and was stationed in Fort Howard, Maryland to study military intelligence. From Maryland, McKinney was sent to Detroit to manage the riots and protests brought on by the Civil Rights Movement and the assassination of Martin Luther King Junior. However, the use of military power on civilians proved controversial and armed forces were withdrawn from domestic use on protestors. Next, he travelled to Thailand where he remained for two years before returning to aid the race relations crisis in Detroit. As the interview concludes, McKinney reflects on his time in the military, positing that his time in the service taught him self-reliance and perseverance In this interview, Vietnam veteran William Harrison recalls his life as a truck driver, construction worker, and infantryman for students with the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program. Harrison was born in Jefferson County, Florida, as one of three children. Harrison is a father to three daughters, grandfather to two grandchildren, and a husband to his wife of forty-three years. While taking classes in criminology at Tallahassee Community College, Harrison was drafted into the Army. He was first sent to Fort Benning, Georgia, for basic training before completing advanced training at Fort McClellan, Alabama. After a brief pit stop in Thailand, Harrison met up with his platoon in Vietnam. While in Vietnam, Harrison primarily conducted ground surveillance, taking four-day trips into the jungle to identify potential threats from the North Vietnamese Army. After a year in Vietnam, he returned to the States, stationed at Fort Sill, Oklahoma before joining the reserves. After his discharge, Harrison began working in construction before finding work as a truck driver. During this time, Harrison also met his wife of over four decades, who became the mother of his three daughters. He reflects that his time in the military made him more politically active, remembering that before his service he had never registered to vote. He remains involved in the Monticello chapter of VFW, attending monthly meetings and other events and providing resources for fellow combat veterans In this interview, students Abby Lovett and Aanchel Shah meet with Bethel Baptist Church member Deloris Massey-Harpoole to discuss the historically Black Allen subdivision in Tallahassee, Florida. Harpoole, a lifelong resident of Tallahassee, explains that she has become involved in a project to document the history of the Allen Subdivision, which had been neglected in official city documents. Harpoole is a proud graduate of Florida A&M University, which she posits gave her the leadership skills and education to become a statewide leader in Florida. In her professional life, Harpoole served as the state policy director for educational opportunity programs and the statewide director of the college outreach program with the Department of Education In both the beginning and end of the interview, Reverend Henry Steele discusses the significance of love and community. Love is his driving force. Through the interview, Steele emphasizes the intersection of activism and church, explains how he became a minister, discusses his position and affiliation with the historical figure Ralph Abernathy, talks about his family, with an emphasis on his father who opened his home to diverse populations, and breaks down steps to further progress as a country. To Steele, Tallahassee remains a pivotal place and haven for civil rights and social justice In this interview, longtime partners Alex and Dorothy Brickler recount their lives—both together and prior to meeting—to three student interviewers. Dorothy recalls that she was born in Pennsylvania before moving to Charlotte, North Carolina as her father, a biologist, struggled to find work due to racial discrimination. Later, she and her father moved to Tallahassee where he started working as a professor of biology at Florida A&M University. In Tallahassee, where she lives to present day, Dorothy married a man from New Jersey, Alex Brickler. Alex and Dorothy’s father worked in a medical practice in Tallahassee together. Alex was born in Indianapolis, Indiana, with a high school teacher as a father. He and his father moved to Ohio, where his father worked as a mechanical engineer. From watching his father work, Alex developed an interest in engineering which lent itself to his later career in the Air Force. The couple met at Howard University while taking a biology course and have been sharing a home in Tallahassee ever since In this interview, Allonia Griffin discusses her early childhood and how she had an active role in raising her siblings. She also talks about farm life growing up as well as the limited resources regarding groceries and activities. Ms. Griffin then speaks on her church upbringing and what it meant and still means to her In this interview, Ms. Vi Whitfield discusses her experiences with racial inequality, discrimination, and violence as well as how such themes have been prevalent for many generations. Whitfield recounts her life as a young woman in Newberry, Florida and explains how she and others organized to fight for civil rights and social justice. As an activist, Whitfield asserts the importance of sharing the truth, even when it’s as painful as the killing of six African American individuals, known as the Newberry six lynching. Ms. Whitfield delves into the issues of racism and violence that the country has faced throughout history and relates it to her hopes for truth and justice This is a recording and transcription of the service held at Pleasant Plain United Methodist Church in Newberry, Florida, to commemorate and honor the lives of the six African Americans that were lynched in the town in 1916. The program, consisting of prayers, poems, and history, tells the story of the Black community in North Central Florida and serves as an homage to these individuals, their families, and the countless others who have experienced racism and violence. By recognizing the truth of the past, the presenters discuss the atrocities of slavery in the United States and leave the audience with a message of hope for unity, equality, and justice In this tape, interviewer Joe Hartman sits down with Ocala, Florida, elder Regis Boatwright. Boatwright recalls her childhood as an African American in segregated Ocala, where she lived with her mother, grandparents, and siblings In this interview, Joe Hartman sits down with McCoy, Florida elder Rebia Berry to discuss her life as an educator in central Florida. Berry recollects several generations of family history, including the life of her mother, Pearl, her grandparents, and her great-grandparents. She recalls her family owning a large plot of land for several generations, which they used for farming but also allowed as a community space for churches and other public buildings to be constructed on. Berry recalls her education at Bethune-Cookman College in Daytona, which she later used to become a teacher. Berry recalls her first teaching assignment, where she single-handedly ran an all-grades, one-room schoolhouse and served as the teacher, principal, janitor, and bus driver. Berry goes on to describe her colorful teaching career and reflects on the effects of integration on the education system. She remembers her children’s experiences as students amidst the chaos of integration, and reflects on the future of education in Florida In this interview, former Marion County educator Joe Hartman interviews Frank Washington, a lifelong educator and school system administrator in Marion County. Washington recalls his childhood in Ocala and his desire from a young age to become a school principal. He remembers the efforts of his family and siblings working on their hundred-acre farm, which remains in the family to this day. Washington went on to attend Florida A&M University on a football scholarship, later earning a master’s degree and working as an teacher, administrator, and personnel director for Marion County School Systems. He and his late wife Barbara had two children together, and Washington is a grandfather to five grandchildren. He remains involved with his local church and his fraternity Hazel Land, lawyer and civil rights activist, reflects on her life growing up in Florida. She details her humble beginnings in Brooksville and how this environment impacted her future education journey which would take her to Tuskegee University for undergraduate school and University of Florida for law school. Before she became a lawyer, Land worked with the NAACP for many years, eventually serving as the state director for Tennessee. Throughout her academic and personal struggles, especially as she adjusted to a predominantly white law school, she managed to persevere and became the first African American woman to graduate from University of Florida’s Levin College of Law Robert Bowser details his time growing up as an African-American man in the 1930s and on, navigating struggles of racism and discrimination. He begins with his tumultuous childhood in New York’s foster care system and experience with integration in high school. Later on the interview, Bowser begins to focus primarily on his career with the United States Navy, where he rose in the ranks despite the prejudices of his often White officials, as well as the effect on his family life. At fifty-six, Bowser turned to the church, becoming a reverend in order to teach the lessons of the Bible and continues to do so in any way he can today. Throughout the interview, we see Bowser overcome countless obstacles during his life presented due to societal standards and shortcomings, ultimately culminating in a happy ending with his children around him In this interview, New York City native Kathleen Haskins recounts the life and work of her husband, Dr. James Haskins, who was among the first Black professors at the University of Florida. She recounts their shared love for writing, and the challenges and rewards of writing children’s books. Haskins reflects on her own experience as a White woman writing books on African American topics, explaining that her work has allowed her to see the world from different perspectives. Haskins also recounts some of her experiences working in a New York City Museum in the aftermath of 9/11 Cottie Wright gives insight into her experience and interaction with African American Studies. Her passion for history and political science fueled her choice of discipline at both Alabama State University and University of Florida. She noted that Alabama State University was an all-black school as opposed to the University of Florida. Wright highlights the importance of teaching an inclusive history of US from high school to college level which brings to fore the contributions of African American. Living through period of segregation in Montgomery, Alabama where she was born, activism soon became an integral part of her life. In recent years, her support for the Civil Rights Movement has metamorphosed into advocacy for particularly African American Studies, and in broad sense, teaching of a complete form of US history In this interview, Cantonment, Florida elder Eddie Lee Osborn recounts his life in Cantonment over the course of nearly one hundred years, from his birth in 1924 to the recording of this interview in 2020. He describes growing up on his grandfather’s farm, where they raised livestock and crops to sustain themselves. He recalls that his family hardly ever visited the store, instead relying on themselves and the natural resources around them for supplies. He recalls walking several miles to school each day, where he attended classes with two teachers, Mrs. Rogers and Mrs. Williams In this interview, Mr. Joseph Holmes discusses his experience as a Black military veteran. He recounts his early life, his family and education, as well as his experiences living through the Jim Crow era as a child and young man. He explains how he joined the US Marine Corps, the training he received at Parris Island and his time at Camp Pendleton, describing race relations among members of the military. While never deployed in Vietnam, Mr. Holmes talks about the fear surrounding the war, the traumatic nature of what other veterans experienced, as well as his own experiences as an electrician, charged with repairing Huey jets. He discusses his work as Post Commander for the American Legion Post 193, and how the military, as well as the broader community, has changed over time Robbie Gregg reflects on his twenty-four years of service in the United States Navy. He explains joining the Navy in 1982 upon his graduation from high school, knowing his family was unable to afford his university tuition and hoping to receive funding from the G.I. Bill after his service. In his words, “I joined the Navy to pay for school, but I wanted to see the world.” With his high school success in electronics, he became a naval engineer. He describes ascending naval ranks as comparable to college life: “it’s all about studying.” Eventually, after his studying, Gregg became an E8, or a chief petty officer. He details the social changes that occurred during his service, including bans on facial hair and cigarettes and the introduction of women into military service. Additionally, he discusses racism on naval ships and explains his observation that racism was not tolerated by the Navy. Towards the end of his service, Gregg served as a drill instructor. He explains how his instruction differed from the instruction he had received, as greater attention was given to the mental stress of the recruits In this interview, Dr. Paul Ortiz and Mr. Ronald Preer discuss Preer's diverse array of careers throughout his life in Pensacola, Florida. Preer recalls his work as an Army Mechanic stationed for two years in western Germany, where he was stationed near Fulda Pass, a region known as high-risk for potential Soviet invasions. Preer recalls how he narrowly avoided a tour in Vietnam. Then, Preer discusses the difficulties of finding a well-paying job as an African American upon his return from Germany In this interview, Dr. Paul Ortiz sits down with Pensacola native Michael Roberts, who recalls his life and education in Pensacola, Florida. Michael remembers attending segregated schools for first and second grade, and then transitioning to integrated schooling at CA Weis Elementary School. Roberts attended Brentwood Middle School, an integrated institution, and then went on to Escambia High School. He graduated from Florida State University in 1980 and went on to attend graduate school at the same institution. He went on to receive a doctoral degree from the University of West Florida in Pensacola. Roberts remembers the painstaking efforts that his mother endured to raise him alone after the death of his father when Roberts was still in elementary school. He also remembers the hardship brought on by his brother’s death in a motorcycle accident, which left his remaining brother as his only male role model Larry Robbins recounts his life growing up in the Cantonment area of Pensacola, Florida. Robbins recalls the changes to the region over time, reflecting on both the positive and negative aspects of these developments. He describes his education at St. Joseph Catholic School, then later at Ransom School, and reflects on the impacts of segregation on his education experience. He also discusses his careers in the military and at the International Paper mill, where he worked in a variety of roles. Robbins explains that he chose these careers because he did not want to go into the puckwooding industry, which was a popular occupation in the area at the time because his father died in a puckwooding accident. Robbins closes with a reflection on his hopes for the future of the Cantonment area and his current role in the Cantonment Improvement Committee (CIC) In this interview, lifelong Cantonment, Florida resident Robert Gross recalls his life in Cantonment, Florida, a small settlement in the Florida Panhandle’s Escambia County. Gross remembers his early education at a local Catholic School and his transition to public school. Later, Gross recalls his decades of work at the International Paper plant in Cantonment, where he learned the skills of virtually every position in every department. Gross explains that his opportunities at International Paper were the result of the activism of black paper plant employees before him, who successfully sued the plant for denying promotions to black employees. He also recalls the extraordinary lengths the company went to prevent the hiring and promotion of black men, and the even more ambitious efforts undertaken by black paper mill employees to combat discriminatory hiring and promoting practices. Finally, Gross reflects on his hopes for the future of the Cantonment area Born in Pensacola, Florida in 1960, David Alexander went on to serve as his hometown’s first African American police chief. In this interview, he begins by describing his childhood and education in Pensacola. He reflects on his early experiences with racism, as well as his “relationships with people that countered the negativity.” He recounts the Escambia High School race riot in 1976, at which time he was a sophomore. He details the events of the riot he witnessed, including white people throwing rocks at black students, the arrival of the National Guard, and use of teargas on campus. Later, he points out that his white class president argued to the school board against allowing the Klu Klux Klan to hold a rally at Escambia High School, causing the school board to deny the permit—although the city government provided a permit for the rally to be held downtown. Alexander goes on to describe his professional life, including his hiring by the Pensacola Police Department in 1983. He describes the series of events in his career that led him to become Pensacola’s first black police chief, as well as his style of leadership and his approach to repairing the relationship between the police and Black communities. Additionally, he explains the role of faith throughout his career. At the time of this interview, Alexander was running for Sheriff of Escambia County In this interview, African American Pensacola elder James Moultry recalls his childhood, education, and his experiences before and after being drafted into the service during the Vietnam War. He recalls attending the Booker T. Washington School in Pensacola for both junior and senior high school. Moultry remembers the obstacles to education posed by segregation and recalls how it impacted his prospects for the future. Before joining the service, Moultry remembers working as a barber for serval years after attending a barber’s school in Connecticut. Moultry spent his tour of duty in an artillery unit in North Carolina, where he made the rank of E-5 within eighteen months. Moultry declined to stay in the service, however, after realizing that he would be sent to Vietnam if he remained. Moultry recalls his life after service, in which he married his wife and became employed with the United Postal Service. Moultry lost his job with the postal service when he moved from Connecticut to Pensacola, Florida, and opted to join the police force, where he served for nearly three decades. Moultry then recalls his efforts at starting one of the few Black chapters of the American Legion in Pensacola, which he remained active in to the date of this interview. Moultry concludes with his hopes for the future of American Legion and the memorialization of Black military history in Pensacola In this interview, Escambia County elder Joseph Lewis recalls his childhood in the rural town of Barth, Florida, where he attended school at nearby a nearby Baptist church because the local school only accepted White students. Barth, who was born in 1923, remembers that for high school he and other Black residents of Barth were bussed twenty miles away to Century, Florida, which housed the closest school accepting Black students, Century High School. Lewis recalls playing basketball because it was the only sport his school had the funding to support. He recalls learning English from Lily James, sister of the famed General Chappie James. Lewis attended college at Florida A&M University for just a semester before he was drafted into the United States Navy during World War II. Lewis explains that he was among the first cohort of Black Navy servicemen, as Black Navy members prior to his service were admitted only as cooks or janitorial staff. Lewis attended basic training in Chicago, Illinois, before his deployment on the all-Black crew of the USS SC330. He recalls that the ship was a re-commissioned vessel used during the First World War, and that although the ship was dilapidated, he and his fellow crew members grew fond of it Born in Repton, Alabama in 1947, veteran Walter Wallace and his family moved to Pensacola, Florida one year after his birth. In this interview, he details his experiences growing up during Jim Crow Era segregation in Pensacola, including attending segregated schools. Describing his hard-working single mother, he reflects on the importance of community support provided by the Black Pensacola community. As a young teenager, Walter delivered papers for the Pensacola News Journal, which he and his young coworkers eventually boycotted to demand fair pay. He says that small gains were made, but growing older, he decided to search for work elsewhere. Reflecting on the impact of the Civil Rights Act—which occurred two years before his high school graduation—on Pensacola, Walter explains, “Immediately, it wasn’t no great change Born in Pensacola, Florida in 1952, Edgar Carter received seven medals— including those for Good Conduct, Meritorious Service, Presidential Citation, Marksmanship, and Vietnam—for his service in the U.S. Air Force. In this interview, he reflects on his childhood and schooling, describing Pensacola’s process of educational integration that began during his junior year of high school. He also describes growing up during segregation in the Jim Crow era U.S. South. He explains joining the Air Force soon after his high school graduation in 1971. He details his experiences in basic training and throughout his military assignments over the next several years until his service concluded in 1978. In 1979, Carter began working at Saint Regis Paper Company, but soon after, he switched jobs and worked at a chemical plant until 1998. After that particular chemical plant closed, he was hired at another, where he worked from 1999 until 2016, at which point he “medically retired” due to multiple surgeries In this interview, Cantonment, Florida local Osmond Sharpless recalls his nine decades of life in the Pensacola, Florida area. Sharpless, born with the help of a midwife in June of 1931, grew up on a three-acre lot along Muskogee Road in Cantonment, Florida. The Great Depression played a major role in the early years of Sharpless’s life, and he remembers his father’s efforts to provide for their family during the hardship by retrieving abandoned wood to process in his own sawmill. His father also used wood from the sawmill to renovate the school buses for Escambia County School System. Sharpless also shares his extensive knowledge of American history, particularly the history of the Mississippi River Delta Region. He recalls the early conflicts between European nations for possession of Florida and other lands in the southeast, and the impact of these conflicts on the course of history. Particularly, Sharpless remembers Florida’s involvement in the cattle trade and the resulting Florida “cracker” culture which emerged during the nineteenth century. Sharpless also recalls the impact of integration in Pensacola, particularly as it pertained to employees at International Paper Mill and to students in Escambia County public schools, including Sharpless’s own daughter In this interview, Dr. Christopher Busey talks about his early life, what he learned from his community, his time as an undergrad at the University of Florida, his time as a professor at the University of Florida, and his thoughts on Ethnic Studies. Dr. Busey talks about his family life growing up and the connections he made with people in his community. Busey credits his upbringing and his interactions to community to his understanding Black and Afro-Latinx identity and his research. This is due to the community that surrounded his growing up and his time as a teacher in Orlando, seeing what students went through on a daily basis. Busey also talks about his time as an undergraduate student mentioning how there used to be a program that used to recruit African American and Latinx students. This program taught future undergraduates what to expect from college and helped them through their undergraduate career In this interview, Dr. David Canton talks about how he learned about his community’s history, how education can lead to change, and his time and plans as the Director of African American Studies at the University of Florida. Canton learned about Black and African American History because his father insisted that it would be good to round out his education. His father also pushed Canton to attend Morehouse College which gave him a curriculum centered African American, Black and white authors At the 100 Year Remembrance Ocoee Election Day Massacre Ceremony, Dr. Paul Ortiz discusses the legacy of the 1920 Ocoee Election Day massacre and the efforts subsequently undertaken to educate the public on the massacre's history. He recalls the efforts of Bill Maxwell and the City of Ocoee Human Relations Diversity Board in making the commemoration of the massacre possible during the 2010 MLK Day parade and during the 100 Year Remembrance ceremony. Dr. Ortiz expands to historically situate the Ocoee Massacre amidst an array of similar anti-Black pogroms that occurred during the early 20th century in response to Black economic prosperity and political participation In this interview, Bertha Watts, a Lake City native and resident of Tampa, remembers the life and tragic death of her father-in-law Lester Watts. Lester Watts and his wife, Bernice, were walking down Pleasant Street in Gainesville, Florida, in 1942 when two White men violently assaulted the couple. Although Bernice survived the attack, her husband, Lester, died of a gunshot wound. The two men suffered no legal penalties for their actions. Bertha Watts remembers how the family and surrounding community dealt with the tragedy during the time. She also emphasizes the importance of passing down family history to future generations As another UF focused interview, Carlos Alvarez talked about his interactions with President O’Connell, including a dispute and heated letter from the president This interview with Ray Eberling centered on race relations and activist organizations at UF between the 1960s and 80s. Some of the groups include Southern Student Organizing Committee, a southern affiliate of the Students for Democratic Society, the Student Mobilization Committee and the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. All of these organizations carried antiwar sentiments and supported the Civil Rights efforts. Although now their objectives would be tame, Eberling explains how they were radical at the time. Because of this and the right-leaning legislature, UF President O’Connell dismissed them at times. Eberling discusses interactions with O’Connell, his role as a “moderate segregationist,” and his own participation in the sit- ins and rallies. He also explains the formation and evolution of the Black Student Union, which developed into the African American Studies department on campus, as well as The Independent Florida Alligator. Finally, Eberling discusses key politicians and activists at the time like Governor Claude Kirk and H. Rap Brown